window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"pyq-mock-2": [
{"q":"According to the Premack principle, the most effective reinforcer for a child who rarely does homework is:","concept":"The Premack principle, proposed by David Premack (1959, 1965), says that reinforcement is relative, not a fixed property of certain stimuli. Any behaviour that a person engages in at a high baseline rate (when free to choose) can reinforce a behaviour that occurs at a lower baseline rate, provided access to the high-probability behaviour is made contingent on first doing the low-probability one. Premack's classic demonstration used children given free access to pinball and candy: children who preferred pinball ate more candy when candy-eating earned pinball time, and children who preferred candy played more pinball when pinball earned candy. He later showed the same reversal in rats by manipulating access to drinking versus wheel-running: when deprived of running, rats would drink to earn running. The everyday version is 'Grandma's rule': first eat your vegetables, then you get dessert. The key exam points are: (1) the reinforcer is an activity (a response), not a thing; (2) you measure baseline frequencies first to rank behaviours; (3) the contingency must run from low to high, never the reverse. Do not confuse it with the response deprivation hypothesis of William Timberlake and James Allison (1974), which refines Premack by saying any behaviour restricted below its baseline can act as a reinforcer, even a lower-probability one. It is also distinct from simple positive reinforcement with praise, tangible rewards like money, or classical conditioning by pairing stimuli, which do not depend on the relative-probability logic. In clinical practice, Premack contingencies are used in parent training, classroom management, inpatient behavioural programmes and activity scheduling in depression. Option b is right because video games are the child's high-frequency activity and making access to them contingent on completing low-frequency homework is exactly the Premack arrangement.","traps":{"a":"Verbal praise is a social (secondary) positive reinforcer and can work, but it is not built on the Premack logic of using a high-probability behaviour to reinforce a low-probability one. It tempts because praise is the generic 'good reinforcer' answer.","c":"Money per assignment is a tangible or generalised conditioned reinforcer, closer to a token or contingency-contract approach than to the Premack principle. It tempts because it looks like an obvious contingent reward, but the stem asks about the Premack principle specifically.","d":"Pairing homework with a neutral activity like drinking water is a classical-conditioning style pairing, and the activity is neither high-probability nor contingent. It tempts because it mentions an 'activity', but a neutral, non-preferred behaviour cannot serve as a Premack reinforcer."}},
{"q":"In Albert Ellis's REBT, the 'B' in the ABC model refers to:","concept":"Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy (REBT) was founded by Albert Ellis (1955, set out in Reason and Emotion in Psychotherapy, 1962). It was first called Rational Therapy, then Rational Emotive Therapy (RET, 1961), and REBT from 1993. Its central model is ABC: A is the Activating event (an adversity, real or imagined), B is the Belief system about that event, and C is the emotional and behavioural Consequence. Ellis's core claim, echoing the Stoic philosopher Epictetus ('people are disturbed not by things but by the views they take of them'), is that A does not directly cause C; B mediates it. Rational beliefs are flexible preferences ('I would like to be approved of') and lead to healthy negative emotions such as concern or sadness. Irrational beliefs are rigid demands, the 'musts', 'shoulds' and 'oughts', and their derivatives: awfulising, low frustration tolerance ('I can't stand it') and global self-downing or other-downing. Ellis grouped the musts into three: I must do well, you must treat me well, and the world must be easy. Therapy extends the model to D (Disputing the irrational belief: empirical, logical and pragmatic disputes) and E (an Effective new philosophy), with some versions adding F (new Feelings). Contrast this with Aaron T. Beck's cognitive therapy, which targets automatic thoughts and schemas through collaborative empiricism rather than Ellis's more directive, philosophical disputation, and with behavioural functional analysis, where an ABC means Antecedent, Behaviour, Consequence, a common examiner confusion. Option b is right because in Ellis's ABC model the B stands for the person's belief about the activating event, which is the mediator REBT targets.","traps":{"a":"Behavioural consequence is part of C in Ellis's model (C covers emotional and behavioural consequences), not B. It tempts because in the behaviourist ABC (Antecedent, Behaviour, Consequence) the B is Behaviour.","c":"Baseline functioning is a behaviour-assessment term (the pre-intervention rate of a behaviour), not a component of REBT's ABC. It tempts only because it starts with B and sounds technical.","d":"Biological predisposition is part of the diathesis-stress idea; Ellis did say humans have a biological tendency to think irrationally, but it is not the B of the ABC model. It tempts because Ellis did write about innate irrationality."}},
{"q":"Beck's cognitive triad in depression consists of negative views about:","concept":"Aaron T. Beck (1967, Depression: Clinical, Experimental and Theoretical Aspects; 1979, Cognitive Therapy of Depression with Rush, Shaw and Emery) proposed a cognitive model of depression with three linked levels. At the deepest level are schemas, stable core beliefs laid down in early experience (for example 'I am unlovable'). When a matching stressor activates a schema, it produces systematic logical errors or cognitive distortions: arbitrary inference, selective abstraction, overgeneralisation, magnification and minimisation, personalisation, and dichotomous (all-or-nothing) thinking. The surface output is negative automatic thoughts. The content of those thoughts clusters into the cognitive triad: a negative view of the self (I am worthless, defective, inadequate), of the world or ongoing experience (others are rejecting, life is full of obstacles, situations are unfair), and of the future (things will never get better, hopelessness). Hopelessness about the future is the element most closely tied to suicide risk, and Beck built the Beck Hopelessness Scale (1974) alongside the Beck Depression Inventory (1961). Examiners like to confuse the triad with other triples: a temporal triple (past, present, future) is not Beck's, and thoughts, feelings and behaviours is the generic CBT 'hot cross bun' or cognitive triangle, not the depressive triad. Also do not confuse Beck's triad with Martin Seligman's learned helplessness or the attributional reformulation by Abramson, Seligman and Teasdale (1978), which explains depression by internal, stable, global attributions. Option b is right because Beck's triad is specifically a negative view of the self, the world and the future.","traps":{"a":"Past, present, future is a temporal grouping; the future does belong to the triad, but past and present are not components. It tempts because 'future' is correct and the triple sounds neat.","c":"Self, others, environment overlaps loosely (self is right, and the world includes others), but Beck's third element is the future, not the environment. It tempts because it reads like a paraphrase of self-world.","d":"Thoughts, feelings, behaviours is the generic CBT cognitive triangle (how cognition, emotion and action interact), not the content of depressive thinking. It tempts because it is the most familiar CBT triple."}},
{"q":"Systematic desensitisation, developed by Joseph Wolpe, is based on the principle of:","concept":"Systematic desensitisation was developed by the South African psychiatrist Joseph Wolpe and set out in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition (1958). Wolpe borrowed the term reciprocal inhibition from Charles Sherrington's neurophysiology, where activating one muscle group inhibits its antagonist. Applied to emotion, the principle states that if a response incompatible with anxiety (most often deep muscle relaxation, using a shortened form of Edmund Jacobson's 1938 progressive muscle relaxation) is made to occur in the presence of an anxiety-evoking stimulus, the anxiety response is weakened, because the two states cannot coexist. Wolpe's early work used cats made 'neurotic' by shock in a cage and then fed in progressively more similar rooms until they ate in the original cage. The clinical procedure has three steps: (1) training in relaxation, (2) building an anxiety hierarchy of 10 to 20 graded situations rated in SUDS (Subjective Units of Distress, 0 to 100, a scale Wolpe devised), and (3) pairing relaxation with imagined (or in vivo) items, moving up only when an item no longer evokes anxiety. The mechanism is classical counterconditioning, the same logic as Mary Cover Jones's 1924 treatment of Peter's fear of rabbits by pairing the rabbit with food. Assertiveness and sexual arousal were Wolpe's other anxiety-inhibiting responses. Later research suggests exposure and extinction may explain much of its effect, but the exam answer for the stated principle remains reciprocal inhibition. Contrast flooding and implosion (no gradation, no relaxation) and operant methods. Option a is right because Wolpe explicitly built systematic desensitisation on reciprocal inhibition, the pairing of an incompatible relaxation response with the feared stimulus.","traps":{"b":"Operant extinction is withholding the reinforcer that maintains an operant behaviour (Skinner). Desensitisation is a respondent counterconditioning procedure; it tempts because modern accounts mention extinction as a possible mechanism.","c":"Stimulus generalisation is the spread of a conditioned response to similar stimuli (Pavlov, Watson's Little Albert). It explains how fears spread, not how Wolpe treats them, which is why it tempts.","d":"Punishment reduces behaviour by adding an aversive or removing a pleasant consequence; desensitisation uses no aversive consequence at all. It tempts only as a generic behavioural term."}},
{"q":"Token economy programmes were systematically developed for chronic psychiatric inpatients by:","concept":"A token economy is an operant-conditioning programme in which clients earn conditioned (secondary) reinforcers, the tokens, for specified target behaviours and later exchange them for backup reinforcers such as privileges, snacks or outings. Because tokens can be traded for many backups, they act as generalised conditioned reinforcers, which reduces satiation and lets staff reinforce immediately. The systematic clinical form was developed by Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin at Anna State Hospital, Illinois, published in 1965 and in the book The Token Economy: A Motivational System for Therapy and Rehabilitation (1968). Working with chronic, long-stay psychiatric inpatients (largely schizophrenia), they showed that self-care and ward-job behaviours rose sharply when tokens were contingent and fell when the contingency was removed, a reversal (ABA) design demonstration of control. Ayllon had earlier, with Jack Michael (1959), shown that ward staff could act as 'behavioural engineers'. Key design elements to remember: clearly defined target behaviours, immediate token delivery, a menu of backup reinforcers, an exchange rate, and planned fading toward natural reinforcers to support generalisation. Related large outcome work is Gordon Paul and Robert Lentz (1977), who found a social-learning (token) programme outperformed milieu therapy for chronic patients. The method stems from B. F. Skinner's operant principles, but Skinner did not build the ward token economy. Limitations include poor generalisation outside the ward and ethical concerns about withholding basic rights. Option b is right because Ayllon and Azrin are the ones who systematically developed the token economy for chronic psychiatric inpatients.","traps":{"a":"Skinner supplied the operant theory and Bandura social learning theory, but neither ran the original ward token economy. It tempts because Skinner is the default name for anything operant.","c":"Eysenck and Wolpe were pioneers of behaviour therapy, but Wolpe is known for systematic desensitisation and Eysenck for personality theory and critiques of psychotherapy outcome. It tempts because both are 'behaviour therapy' names.","d":"Beck and Ellis founded cognitive therapy and REBT respectively, which target beliefs rather than operant contingencies. It tempts as a pair of famous therapy founders."}},
{"q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the four standard treatment modes in Linehan's Dialectical Behaviour Therapy (DBT)?","concept":"Dialectical Behaviour Therapy (DBT) was developed by Marsha M. Linehan (manual 1993) for chronically suicidal women who met criteria for borderline personality disorder. Its theory is biosocial: emotional vulnerability (high sensitivity, high reactivity, slow return to baseline) transacts with an invalidating environment, producing pervasive emotion dysregulation. The 'dialectic' is the balance of acceptance (validation, drawn from Zen practice) and change (behavioural problem solving). Standard comprehensive DBT has four modes, each serving a function: (1) weekly individual psychotherapy, which enhances motivation and uses a diary card, a target hierarchy (life-threatening behaviours first, then therapy-interfering behaviours, then quality-of-life interfering behaviours) and chain analysis; (2) a weekly skills training group, teaching four modules: core mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness (with the DEAR MAN, GIVE and FAST skills); (3) between-session telephone (skills) coaching to generalise skills to real life; and (4) a therapist consultation team that keeps therapists motivated and adherent. Know the distinction that a family genogram belongs to Murray Bowen's multigenerational family systems therapy, popularised in clinical use by Monica McGoldrick and Randy Gerson (1985); it maps three or more generations with symbols for relationships and patterns. DBT can include family skills groups for adolescents, but genogram construction is not one of its four modes. Also distinguish DBT from Jeffrey Young's schema therapy and from mentalisation-based treatment (Bateman and Fonagy), other BPD treatments. Option d is right because family genogram construction is a Bowen family-systems tool, not one of DBT's four standard modes.","traps":{"a":"Individual psychotherapy is a core DBT mode, where the therapist uses diary cards and chain analysis. It tempts because the stem says NOT and readers may think DBT is a group-only therapy.","b":"The skills training group is the best-known DBT mode, teaching mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness. It is included, so it is not the answer to a NOT question.","c":"Telephone consultation (phone coaching) is a genuine DBT mode used for skill generalisation between sessions. It tempts because phone contact sounds non-standard for therapy, but it is part of the model."}},
{"q":"Maxwell Jones is most strongly associated with which therapeutic approach?","concept":"The therapeutic community is associated above all with Maxwell Jones (1907 to 1990), a Scottish psychiatrist who worked with soldiers with 'effort syndrome' at Mill Hill during the Second World War and then at the Industrial Neurosis Unit at Belmont, which became the Henderson Hospital in Sutton, England. His books Social Psychiatry (1952; US title The Therapeutic Community, 1953) and Beyond the Therapeutic Community (1968) set out the model. The term itself was coined by Tom Main (1946) at Northfield Military Hospital, where the Northfield experiments (with Wilfred Bion and S. H. Foulkes) also shaped group therapy. The core idea is that the whole social environment of the ward is the treatment, not just individual sessions. Features include daily community meetings of all patients and staff, flattened hierarchy and shared decision making, open communication, and 'living-learning' situations in which everyday conflicts are examined. Robert Rapoport's study Community as Doctor (1960) summarised the Henderson principles as democratisation, permissiveness, communalism and reality confrontation. The model influenced personality-disorder units, prison programmes (Grendon), and the separate hierarchical 'concept' therapeutic communities for addiction (Synanon, Daytop). Distinguish it from milieu therapy in general, from psychodrama by Jacob L. Moreno, logotherapy by Viktor Frankl, and solution-focused brief therapy by Steve de Shazer and Insoo Kim Berg. Option a is right because Maxwell Jones is the name most strongly linked to developing and spreading the therapeutic community model.","traps":{"b":"Psychodrama was founded by Jacob L. Moreno (1920s to 1930s), using enactment, role reversal and an auxiliary ego on a stage. It tempts because Moreno is also a pioneer of group methods, like Jones.","c":"Logotherapy is Viktor Frankl's existential 'meaning' therapy. It tempts only because Frankl, like Jones, is a mid-century European psychiatrist.","d":"Solution-focused brief therapy was developed by Steve de Shazer and Insoo Kim Berg in the 1980s, using the miracle question and scaling questions. It tempts as another named 'school' unrelated to Jones."}},
{"q":"Viktor Frankl's logotherapy is BEST described as a form of:","concept":"Logotherapy was founded by the Viennese psychiatrist Viktor E. Frankl (1905 to 1997) and is often called the 'third Viennese school of psychotherapy', after Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis (will to pleasure) and Alfred Adler's individual psychology (will to power). 'Logos' here means meaning. Frankl argued that the primary human motive is the will to meaning; frustration of it produces an 'existential vacuum' and sometimes noogenic neurosis (neurosis arising from spiritual or existential conflict). His account of surviving Nazi concentration camps, Man's Search for Meaning (1946; English 1959), illustrates that meaning can be found even in unavoidable suffering. He proposed three routes to meaning: creative values (what we give to the world, work and deeds), experiential values (what we receive, love, beauty, nature) and attitudinal values (the stance we take toward unavoidable suffering). Core techniques: paradoxical intention (deliberately wishing for the feared symptom, for example trying to blush or sweat more, used for phobias and anticipatory anxiety), dereflection (shifting attention away from excessive self-observation or hyper-reflection, used for sexual dysfunction and insomnia), and Socratic dialogue to uncover meaning. Logotherapy sits within existential-humanistic therapy, alongside Rollo May and Irvin Yalom (whose four 'ultimate concerns' are death, freedom, isolation and meaninglessness). Note that paradoxical intention also appears in strategic family therapy, but there it is a directive; in Frankl it rests on meaning and self-distancing. Option b is right because logotherapy is an existential therapy organised around the search for meaning.","traps":{"a":"Token reinforcement is an operant method (Ayllon and Azrin's token economy); logotherapy has no reinforcement schedule. It tempts only if 'logo' is misread as a behavioural term.","c":"Unconscious conflict is the focus of Freudian psychodynamic therapy; Frankl explicitly positioned logotherapy against reducing motivation to pleasure or drives. It tempts because Frankl trained in Vienna amid psychoanalysis.","d":"Irrational beliefs are the target of Albert Ellis's REBT. It tempts because logotherapy uses Socratic dialogue, which also appears in cognitive therapies, but its aim is meaning, not disputing irrational musts."}},
{"q":"The 'empty chair' technique is most characteristic of which therapeutic school?","concept":"Gestalt therapy was developed by Frederick (Fritz) Perls with Laura Perls and Paul Goodman (Gestalt Therapy: Excitement and Growth in the Human Personality, 1951). It borrows the Gestalt psychology idea that people perceive whole figures against a ground, and applies it to needs: a healthy person lets the most pressing need become 'figure', satisfies it, and lets it recede. When needs or feelings are blocked, they remain as 'unfinished business' that keeps intruding into the present. The therapy is experiential and present-centred, emphasising awareness of the here and now, responsibility, and contact. The empty chair technique asks the client to imagine a significant person (for example a dead parent) or a disowned part of the self in an empty chair and speak directly to it, often switching chairs to reply. The two-chair variant stages the 'topdog' (demanding, shoulds) versus 'underdog' (resisting, excuses) split. The aim is to complete unfinished business and integrate polarities, not to interpret. Leslie Greenberg's emotion-focused therapy later researched chair work systematically. Related Gestalt ideas: contact-boundary disturbances such as introjection, projection, retroflection, deflection and confluence. Distinguish it from psychodrama by Jacob L. Moreno, which also uses enactment and an empty chair-like device but on a stage with a group and auxiliary egos; examiners sometimes use this to confuse candidates. Person-centred therapy is non-directive and would not set up such exercises. Option c is right because the empty chair is the signature technique of Fritz Perls's Gestalt therapy.","traps":{"a":"CBT works with thought records, Socratic questioning and behavioural experiments; it may borrow chair work occasionally but it is not characteristic. It tempts as the default 'most common therapy' answer.","b":"Person-Centred Therapy (Carl Rogers) is non-directive and relies on empathy, congruence and unconditional positive regard rather than structured enactments. It tempts because both are humanistic schools.","d":"Interpersonal Therapy (Klerman and Weissman) is a time-limited treatment for depression focused on grief, role disputes, role transitions and interpersonal deficits. It tempts because it addresses relationships, like dialogue with an absent person."}},
{"q":"Bandura's observational learning involves four sub-processes in which sequence?","concept":"Observational learning (modelling) is the core of Albert Bandura's social learning theory (Social Learning Theory, 1977), later broadened into social cognitive theory (1986). The classic evidence is the Bobo doll studies (Bandura, Ross and Ross, 1961, 1963): children who watched an adult model hit an inflatable doll later reproduced the specific aggressive acts. Bandura's 1965 variant showed the learning versus performance distinction: children who saw the model punished imitated less, but when offered incentives they all reproduced the behaviour, proving they had learned it. From this he described four sequential sub-processes. (1) Attention: the observer must notice the model; this is affected by model features (status, similarity, attractiveness) and observer features (arousal, prior reinforcement). (2) Retention: the behaviour is stored through imaginal and verbal coding and rehearsal. (3) Reproduction (motor reproduction): the observer must be physically able to convert the memory into action, refined by feedback. (4) Motivation: the observer performs the behaviour when there is an incentive, through direct, vicarious or self-reinforcement. A simple mnemonic is ARRM. Vicarious reinforcement and punishment (seeing the model rewarded or punished) operate at the motivation stage. Distinguish this from Skinner's operant learning, which requires the learner's own reinforced response, and from Bandura's later concept of self-efficacy (1977), which concerns expected capability. Clinically, modelling underlies participant modelling for phobias and social skills training. Option b is right because the correct order is attention, then retention, then reproduction, then motivation.","traps":{"a":"This order puts retention before attention, which is impossible: you cannot store what you never noticed. It tempts because it contains the right four labels, only shuffled.","c":"Starting with motivation confuses the learning versus performance distinction; Bandura showed learning can occur without incentives, with motivation governing later performance. It tempts because motivation feels like it should come first.","d":"Reproduction cannot precede attention and retention, since one reproduces a stored representation. It tempts as a plausible-looking reshuffle of the right terms."}},
{"q":"Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP) for OCD was operationalised primarily by:","concept":"Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP), also called exposure and ritual prevention, is the first-line psychological treatment for obsessive-compulsive disorder. Its origin is usually traced to Victor Meyer (1966) in London, who treated inpatients with supervised exposure and prevention of rituals, building on animal avoidance research. Edna B. Foa, at the University of Pennsylvania, operationalised and standardised it into the manualised protocol (Kozak and Foa, 1997; Foa, Yadin and Lichner, Exposure and Response (Ritual) Prevention for OCD, 2012), and ran the major trials, including the multisite study by Foa and colleagues (2005) comparing ERP with clomipramine. The procedure: build a fear hierarchy of obsessional triggers, then do in vivo and imaginal exposure, starting moderate and moving up, while the client refrains from compulsions, mental rituals and reassurance seeking. Foa and Michael Kozak's emotional processing theory (1986) explained the mechanism: the fear structure must be activated and corrected by disconfirming information, shown by within- and between-session habituation. A more recent account, the inhibitory learning model of Michelle Craske (2008, 2014), emphasises expectancy violation over habituation. Foa also developed Prolonged Exposure for PTSD. Distinguish ERP from Wolpe's systematic desensitisation (relaxation plus graded imagery), from Beck's cognitive therapy, and from DBT. Option a is right because Edna Foa is the clinician-researcher who operationalised and manualised ERP for OCD.","traps":{"b":"Aaron Beck developed cognitive therapy for depression and anxiety, and cognitive models of OCD came later from Salkovskis and Rachman. It tempts because CBT for OCD is often framed as Beck-derived.","c":"Joseph LeDoux is a neuroscientist who mapped the amygdala's fear circuits (the 'low road' and 'high road'). It tempts because his work informs fear extinction, but he did not develop a clinical treatment.","d":"Marsha Linehan developed DBT for borderline personality disorder and chronic suicidality. It tempts as a well-known name in manualised behavioural therapy."}},
{"q":"Schema therapy, developed by Jeffrey Young, was originally designed for patients who:","concept":"Schema therapy was developed by Jeffrey E. Young (Cognitive Therapy for Personality Disorders: A Schema-Focused Approach, 1990; Schema Therapy: A Practitioner's Guide with Janet Klosko and Marjorie Weishaar, 2003). Young, who trained with Aaron Beck, noticed that standard short-term CBT worked poorly for chronic, characterological patients: those with rigid lifelong patterns, vague targets, poor access to thoughts and feelings, or difficulty forming the collaborative relationship CBT assumes. He integrated cognitive, behavioural, attachment, Gestalt (chair work, imagery) and object-relations ideas. Core constructs: early maladaptive schemas, broad self-defeating themes formed when core childhood needs (secure attachment, autonomy, freedom to express needs, spontaneity and play, realistic limits) are unmet. Young lists 18 schemas in 5 domains: disconnection and rejection, impaired autonomy and performance, impaired limits, other-directedness, and overvigilance and inhibition. Coping styles are surrender, avoidance and overcompensation. Schema modes (child modes, maladaptive coping modes, dysfunctional parent modes, and the healthy adult) are moment-to-moment states, especially central in borderline personality disorder. Techniques include limited reparenting, imagery rescripting, empathic confrontation and chair dialogues. Its strongest evidence is the trial by Giesen-Bloo and colleagues (2006), where schema therapy outperformed transference-focused psychotherapy for borderline personality disorder. Distinguish it from Beck's schemas in depression (narrower) and from DBT. Option a is right because Young designed schema therapy for patients who did not respond to standard CBT, especially those with personality disorders.","traps":{"b":"Acute psychosis requiring hospitalisation is managed with antipsychotics and supportive care, not a long-term schema-focused approach. It tempts only as a 'severe patient' option.","c":"Simple specific phobias respond very well to brief exposure therapy (even one-session treatment by Lars-Goran Ost), which is exactly the kind of case standard CBT already handles. It tempts because it is an anxiety condition.","d":"Schema therapy is long term (often one to three years for BPD), the opposite of brief solution-focused work by de Shazer and Berg. It tempts because 'therapy designed for a specific purpose' sounds like brief intervention."}},
{"q":"Salvador Minuchin is associated with which model of family therapy?","concept":"Structural family therapy was developed by Salvador Minuchin (Families and the Slums, 1967; Families and Family Therapy, 1974), working first with delinquent boys at the Wiltwyck School and later at the Philadelphia Child Guidance Clinic, where he also studied 'psychosomatic families' with anorexia (Psychosomatic Families, 1978). The model sees symptoms as maintained by a family's structure, the invisible set of rules that organise how members interact. Key concepts: subsystems (spousal, parental, sibling); boundaries that regulate contact between them, ranging from rigid (leading to disengagement) to clear (healthy) to diffuse (leading to enmeshment); hierarchy, with parents in charge; and dysfunctional patterns such as triangulation, detouring and cross-generational coalitions. The therapist 'joins' the family, maps the structure with a family map, and then restructures it through enactments (getting members to act out the problem interaction in session), unbalancing, and boundary making. Distinguish the near neighbours examiners love: strategic family therapy by Jay Haley and Cloe Madanes (and the MRI brief therapy of Watzlawick, Weakland and Fisch) uses directives and paradox to change problem-maintaining sequences; Bowen's multigenerational therapy uses differentiation of self, triangles, the genogram and the multigenerational transmission process; experiential family therapy is associated with Carl Whitaker and Virginia Satir; Milan systemic therapy (Selvini Palazzoli) uses circular questioning. Option b is right because Minuchin founded structural family therapy, built on subsystems, boundaries and hierarchy.","traps":{"a":"Strategic family therapy is Jay Haley's and Cloe Madanes's model (with the MRI group), using directives and paradoxical tasks. It tempts because Haley worked alongside Minuchin in Philadelphia and both models are action-oriented.","c":"Multigenerational family therapy is Murray Bowen's, centred on differentiation of self, triangles and the genogram. It tempts because both Bowen and Minuchin talk about triangulation.","d":"Experiential family therapy is associated with Carl Whitaker (symbolic-experiential) and Virginia Satir (communication, sculpting). It tempts as another classic family therapy school from the same era."}},
{"q":"Carl Rogers's three 'core conditions' for therapeutic change are:","concept":"Carl R. Rogers developed client-centred, later person-centred, therapy (Counseling and Psychotherapy, 1942; Client-Centered Therapy, 1951). His 1957 paper 'The necessary and sufficient conditions of therapeutic personality change' listed six conditions: two people in psychological contact; the client in a state of incongruence (vulnerable or anxious); the therapist congruent in the relationship; the therapist experiencing unconditional positive regard; the therapist experiencing empathic understanding of the client's internal frame of reference; and the client perceiving, at least minimally, the therapist's regard and empathy. The three therapist-provided conditions are the famous 'core conditions'. Congruence (genuineness, authenticity) means the therapist's outer behaviour matches inner experience, no professional facade. Unconditional positive regard means warm, non-judgemental acceptance of the person, not approval of every behaviour. Accurate empathy means sensing the client's private world 'as if' it were one's own without losing the 'as if' quality, and communicating that understanding through reflection. The theory behind them: people have an actualising tendency, but conditions of worth imposed by others create incongruence between self-concept and experience; a relationship offering the core conditions lets the client drop defences and move toward becoming a 'fully functioning person'. Research by Charles Truax and Robert Carkhuff operationalised the conditions, and they fed into the broader 'common factors' literature on the therapeutic alliance. Contrast with psychoanalytic processes, behavioural techniques and CBT components listed as distractors. Option b is right because Rogers's three core conditions are unconditional positive regard, congruence and empathy.","traps":{"a":"Transference, resistance and interpretation are core psychoanalytic concepts from Freud; Rogers explicitly rejected interpretation as directive. It tempts as another triad of classic therapy terms.","c":"Reinforcement, modelling and shaping are behavioural and social-learning techniques (Skinner, Bandura). It tempts as a neat technical triple, but Rogers's model is relational, not technique-based.","d":"Cognitive restructuring, behavioural activation and relapse prevention are components of CBT packages. It tempts because they are standard modern therapy elements, but they are not Rogers's conditions."}},
{"q":"Disulfiram is used as an adjunct in the treatment of alcohol dependence because it:","concept":"Alcohol is metabolised in two steps in the liver: alcohol dehydrogenase converts ethanol to acetaldehyde, and aldehyde dehydrogenase (ALDH, mainly ALDH2) converts acetaldehyde to acetate. Disulfiram (Antabuse), introduced for alcohol dependence by Erik Jacobsen and Jens Hald in Denmark in 1948, irreversibly inhibits ALDH. If the person then drinks, toxic acetaldehyde accumulates and produces the disulfiram-ethanol reaction: facial flushing, throbbing headache, nausea and vomiting, sweating, palpitations and tachycardia, breathlessness, hypotension, and in severe cases arrhythmia, collapse or death. The same flushing occurs naturally in many East Asians with an ALDH2 variant, which is protective against alcohol dependence. Disulfiram is therefore an aversive or deterrent drug: it does not reduce craving; it works through fear of the reaction, and its effectiveness depends heavily on adherence, so supervised dosing (for example by a spouse) is the evidence-based way to use it. Because inhibition is irreversible, the reaction can occur up to about two weeks after the last dose, and patients must be abstinent at least 12 hours before starting and avoid hidden alcohol (mouthwash, cough syrups). Contraindications include heart disease and psychosis. Compare the other anti-craving drugs: naltrexone, an opioid antagonist that blunts alcohol-induced reward (dopamine release in the nucleus accumbens is reduced indirectly); acamprosate, which modulates glutamatergic NMDA transmission to reduce protracted withdrawal-related craving; and baclofen, a GABA-B agonist. Option b is right because disulfiram works by inhibiting aldehyde dehydrogenase so that drinking produces aversive acetaldehyde symptoms.","traps":{"a":"Reducing dopaminergic reward in the nucleus accumbens describes naltrexone's anti-reward effect (via opioid receptor blockade). It tempts because it is a real alcohol-treatment mechanism, just not disulfiram's.","c":"Partial agonism at GABA-A receptors is not disulfiram's action; GABA-A agonism describes benzodiazepines, used for alcohol withdrawal, and baclofen acts at GABA-B. It tempts because alcohol itself acts on GABA-A.","d":"NMDA modulation to reduce craving describes acamprosate. It tempts because acamprosate is the other classic adjunct drug and examiners pair them deliberately."}},
{"q":"EMG biofeedback is most clinically useful for which of the following conditions?","concept":"Biofeedback uses instruments to record a physiological process the person is normally unaware of and feeds it back as a visible or audible signal, so the person can learn voluntary control through operant shaping. The idea that autonomic and muscle responses could be operantly conditioned was promoted by Neal E. Miller (1969), and the field developed through the late 1960s and 1970s. Major modalities: EMG (electromyographic) biofeedback measures muscle electrical activity through surface electrodes, usually over the frontalis, trapezius or masseter; thermal (skin temperature) biofeedback measures finger temperature as an index of peripheral vasodilation, classically used for migraine; EDA or GSR (electrodermal) biofeedback indexes sympathetic arousal; heart rate variability biofeedback targets resonance breathing; and EEG biofeedback (neurofeedback) trains brain-wave patterns, studied for ADHD and epilepsy. For tension-type headache, frontal and neck EMG biofeedback, usually combined with relaxation training, has strong evidence, with Thomas Budzynski, Johann Stoyva and colleagues (1973) providing an early controlled study and later meta-analyses (for example Nestoriuc, Rief and Martin, 2008) showing moderate to large effects. Other good indications for EMG feedback include temporomandibular disorder, chronic low back pain, and neuromuscular rehabilitation after stroke. Biofeedback is a self-regulation technique for stress-related and psychophysiological disorders; it is not a treatment for psychosis, mood episodes or personality disorders. Remember the pairing for exams: EMG with tension headache, thermal with migraine and Raynaud's. Option a is right because tension-type headache, driven partly by pericranial muscle tension, is the classic indication for EMG biofeedback.","traps":{"b":"A bipolar depressive episode is treated with mood stabilisers, specific antidepressant strategies and psychotherapies; EMG feedback has no role in its core treatment. It tempts only because biofeedback reduces stress in general.","c":"Acute psychosis requires antipsychotic medication and a safe environment; self-regulation training needs insight and concentration the acutely psychotic patient usually lacks. It tempts as a 'serious illness' option.","d":"Antisocial personality disorder is a pervasive pattern of disregard for others' rights; it is not a psychophysiological condition and muscle-tension feedback does not target it. It tempts only as a distractor diagnosis."}},
{"q":"Reinforcing successive approximations toward a target behaviour is known as:","concept":"Shaping, or the method of successive approximations, was described by B. F. Skinner (in the 1938 Behavior of Organisms and demonstrated famously in the 1940s and 1950s, for example teaching pigeons to turn or play ping-pong). When a target behaviour never occurs spontaneously, you cannot wait to reinforce it. Instead, you reinforce any response that resembles it, then raise the criterion so that only closer approximations are reinforced, using differential reinforcement and extinction of earlier forms, until the final target appears. Clinical examples: building speech in a mute child from any vocalisation to sounds to words (Ivar Lovaas's applied behaviour analysis), or increasing time out of bed in depression. Rules: start where the person is, keep steps small, move on once a step is stable, and go back a step if the behaviour breaks down. The neighbours examiners use to confuse: chaining links a sequence of already-learned discrete responses (each step serving as the cue for the next) into a complex routine, done forwards or backwards (backward chaining begins with the final link, as in teaching a child to dress); prompting adds a supplementary cue (verbal, gestural, modelling or physical guidance) to make the correct response more likely; and fading gradually withdraws those prompts so the natural discriminative stimulus controls the behaviour. Shaping changes the response itself; prompting and fading change the stimuli; chaining joins responses. Option b is right because reinforcing successive approximations toward a target behaviour is the definition of shaping.","traps":{"a":"Chaining links separate behaviours into a sequence (for example the steps of toothbrushing), reinforcing completion of links rather than gradually reshaping one response. It tempts because both build complex behaviour step by step.","c":"Prompting adds an extra cue, such as physical guidance or a verbal hint, to evoke the correct response. It tempts because it also helps produce a new behaviour, but it manipulates antecedents, not approximations.","d":"Fading is the gradual removal of prompts so that natural cues take over. It tempts because it is also gradual, but what changes gradually is the cue, not the reinforced response."}},
{"q":"Which of the following is an example of negative punishment?","concept":"In operant conditioning (B. F. Skinner), consequences are classified on two dimensions. The first word refers to what happens to a stimulus: positive means a stimulus is added, negative means a stimulus is removed (think of plus and minus, not good and bad). The second word refers to the effect on behaviour: reinforcement increases the future probability of the behaviour, punishment decreases it. That gives four cells. Positive reinforcement: add something pleasant, behaviour increases (a treat for sitting). Negative reinforcement: remove something aversive, behaviour increases; this includes escape and avoidance learning (pressing a lever turns off shock; taking a painkiller removes a headache). Positive punishment: add something aversive, behaviour decreases (a spank, a reprimand). Negative punishment: remove something pleasant, behaviour decreases; its two main clinical forms are response cost (losing tokens, privileges or screen time) and time-out from positive reinforcement. The single most common exam error is equating negative reinforcement with punishment; negative reinforcement always strengthens behaviour. Clinically, negative reinforcement explains why avoidance maintains anxiety (Mowrer's two-factor theory, 1947) and why compulsions persist in OCD. Punishment is usually less effective than reinforcing an alternative behaviour, because it shows what not to do, can produce fear, aggression and avoidance of the punisher, and suppression is often temporary. Option b is right because removing a pleasant consequence (screen time) to reduce tantrums is negative punishment.","traps":{"a":"Adding a spank adds an aversive stimulus to reduce behaviour, which is positive punishment. It tempts because it is punishment, but the 'positive' (added) dimension is wrong.","c":"Removing a shock when the rat presses the lever increases lever pressing, which is negative reinforcement (escape). It tempts because it involves removal, the trap of confusing negative reinforcement with negative punishment.","d":"Giving a treat for sitting adds a pleasant stimulus that increases sitting, which is positive reinforcement. It tempts only as the most familiar example of operant learning."}},
{"q":"According to DSM-5-TR, the minimum duration required for a diagnosis of schizophrenia is:","concept":"DSM-5-TR (American Psychiatric Association, 2022) diagnoses schizophrenia with six criteria. Criterion A: two or more of delusions, hallucinations, disorganised speech, grossly disorganised or catatonic behaviour, and negative symptoms (diminished emotional expression or avolition), each present for a significant portion of time during a 1-month period (or less if successfully treated), and at least one must be delusions, hallucinations or disorganised speech. Criterion B: marked decline in work, interpersonal relations or self-care. Criterion C: continuous signs of the disturbance persist for at least 6 months; this 6-month period must include at least 1 month of active-phase (Criterion A) symptoms and may include prodromal or residual periods, during which only negative symptoms or attenuated Criterion A symptoms (odd beliefs, unusual perceptual experiences) may appear. Criteria D to F exclude schizoaffective and mood disorders with psychotic features, substance or medical causes, and add a rule for a history of autism or childhood communication disorder (prominent delusions or hallucinations for at least 1 month are then required). The duration ladder is a favourite exam theme: brief psychotic disorder lasts at least 1 day but less than 1 month with full return to function; schizophreniform disorder lasts at least 1 month but less than 6 months; schizophrenia lasts 6 months or more. ICD-11 (WHO) by contrast requires symptoms for at least 1 month, with no 6-month rule, and dropped the classical subtypes, as did DSM-5. Option c is right because DSM-5-TR requires at least 6 months of total disturbance, including at least 1 month of active symptoms plus any prodromal or residual phases.","traps":{"a":"One month of active symptoms is the minimum active-phase duration inside the 6 months, and it is also the ICD-11 total duration requirement. It tempts because it is a real number in both systems, but it is not the DSM-5-TR minimum total duration.","b":"Three months is not a threshold for schizophrenia; a disturbance between 1 and 6 months is schizophreniform disorder. It tempts because it sits in the middle of the duration ladder.","d":"Twelve months of continuous psychosis is not required; continuous active psychosis is not needed at all, since prodromal and residual phases count. It tempts because chronicity is part of the stereotype of schizophrenia."}},
{"q":"Bleuler's '4 As' of schizophrenia do NOT include:","concept":"Eugen Bleuler, the Swiss psychiatrist at the Burgholzli in Zurich, coined the term schizophrenia in 1908 and developed it in Dementia Praecox or the Group of Schizophrenias (1911). He rejected Emil Kraepelin's emphasis on inevitable deterioration (dementia praecox, 1899) and focused instead on a 'splitting' (Spaltung) of psychic functions, meaning a lack of integration among thought, emotion and behaviour, not split personality. He divided symptoms into fundamental (primary) symptoms, present in every case, and accessory (secondary) symptoms, which could be present or absent. The fundamental symptoms are remembered by later textbooks as the 4 As: Associations (loosening of associations, the thought disorder Bleuler considered central), Affect (flattened, incongruous or inappropriate), Ambivalence (simultaneous contradictory feelings, wishes or thoughts), and Autism (withdrawal into an inner fantasy world with detachment from reality; Bleuler coined this word too). Some lists add Attention or Avolition as extra As, but the classic four are the ones above. Accessory symptoms include hallucinations, delusions, catatonia and behavioural disturbances. The historical irony examiners exploit is that Bleuler's accessory symptoms (hallucinations and delusions) are exactly what Kurt Schneider (1959) later elevated as first-rank symptoms and what modern DSM and ICD criteria rely on most, because they are more reliably rated. Option c is right because auditory hallucinations were, for Bleuler, accessory symptoms, not one of the fundamental 4 As.","traps":{"a":"Loosening of associations is the first and, for Bleuler, central A. It tempts because it is also a 'positive' disorganised symptom, which candidates may wrongly think of as accessory.","b":"Affective flattening (and inappropriate affect) is Bleuler's Affect A. It tempts because it is a negative symptom and modern frameworks treat negative symptoms separately.","d":"Autism, in Bleuler's sense of withdrawal into inner life, is one of the 4 As. It tempts because today 'autism' means autism spectrum disorder, a different condition."}},
{"q":"Schneiderian first-rank symptoms of schizophrenia include all EXCEPT:","concept":"Kurt Schneider (Clinical Psychopathology, German 1950, English 1959) proposed first-rank symptoms (FRS) as pragmatic signs that, in the absence of organic disease, strongly suggest schizophrenia. They cluster into three groups. (1) Auditory hallucinations of three specific kinds: audible thoughts (Gedankenlautwerden or echo de la pensee, hearing one's own thoughts spoken aloud), voices arguing or discussing the patient in the third person, and voices giving a running commentary on the patient's actions. (2) Passivity or ego-boundary phenomena: thought insertion, thought withdrawal and thought broadcasting (diffusion of thought); made feelings (affect), made impulses (drives) and made volitional acts; and somatic passivity (bodily sensations imposed by an outside agency). (3) Delusional perception: a normal perception is given a sudden, personally significant delusional meaning (two-stage: perception, then unrelated meaning; for example, seeing a red car and knowing one is chosen to save the world). Schneider also listed second-rank symptoms (other hallucinations, perplexity, depressive or euphoric mood change, emotional blunting, sudden delusional ideas). Know the limitations: FRS occur in mania and other psychoses and are found in only about 60 percent of people with schizophrenia, so DSM-5 removed the special weight that DSM-IV gave to bizarre delusions and Schneiderian voices, and ICD-11 still lists several passivity phenomena among core symptoms. Loose associations belong to Eugen Bleuler's fundamental symptoms. Option c is right because loose associations are a Bleulerian thought-form disturbance, not a Schneiderian first-rank symptom.","traps":{"a":"Thought insertion is a classic Schneiderian passivity phenomenon. It tempts because it is a thought disturbance, like loose associations, but it is about thought possession, not thought form.","b":"Audible thoughts (thought echo) is the first of Schneider's three FRS auditory hallucinations. It tempts because some candidates wrongly think only third-person voices count.","d":"Delusional perception is a first-rank symptom, the only delusion on the list. It tempts because candidates often forget delusions appear among FRS at all."}},
{"q":"A patient insists his wife has been replaced by an identical-looking impostor. This is:","concept":"Delusional misidentification syndromes are delusions in which the identity of a person, place or object is believed to have been altered or duplicated. Capgras syndrome, described by Joseph Capgras and Jean Reboul-Lachaux (1923) as 'l'illusion des sosies' (the illusion of doubles), is the belief that a familiar person, usually a spouse or close relative, has been replaced by an identical-looking impostor or double. It occurs in schizophrenia, dementia (especially Lewy body and Alzheimer's disease), traumatic brain injury and other neurological illness. A leading explanation is the two-factor account of Hadyn Ellis and Andrew Young (1990) and later Max Coltheart: recognition of the face is preserved, but the normal emotional (autonomic) response to a familiar face is lost, a mirror image of prosopagnosia; a second factor, impaired belief evaluation, allows the 'impostor' explanation to be accepted. Patients with Capgras show reduced skin conductance responses to familiar faces. Its neighbours: Fregoli delusion (Courbon and Fail, 1927), the belief that a familiar person, often a persecutor, is disguising themselves as various strangers (hyperidentification, the opposite of Capgras); intermetamorphosis, where people swap identities; and subjective doubles, where a double of oneself exists. Cotard syndrome (Jules Cotard, 1880) is a nihilistic delusion that one is dead, rotting or lacks organs, seen in severe psychotic depression. Othello syndrome (named by John Todd and Kenneth Dewhurst, 1955) is delusional jealousy about a partner's infidelity, linked with alcohol misuse. Option b is right because the belief that one's wife has been replaced by an identical impostor is Capgras syndrome.","traps":{"a":"Fregoli delusion is the opposite misidentification: strangers are believed to be one familiar person in disguise. It tempts because both are misidentification delusions and are often paired in exams.","c":"Cotard syndrome is a nihilistic delusion (I am dead, my organs are rotting), typically in psychotic depression. It tempts as another eponymous French delusional syndrome.","d":"Othello syndrome is delusional jealousy about a spouse's infidelity. It tempts because the stem involves a wife, but the content here is replacement, not unfaithfulness."}},
{"q":"Folie à deux is now classified in ICD-11 under:","concept":"Folie a deux ('madness of two') was described by Charles Lasegue and Jules Falret (1877). A delusion arising in a primary person (the inducer, usually the dominant member with a genuine psychotic disorder) is taken over by a secondary person (the induced, often more passive, dependent, isolated, and closely bonded, such as a spouse, sibling or parent-child pair). The shared delusions are usually persecutory or grandiose and plausible in content, and the classic management point is that separating the pair often leads to the secondary person's beliefs fading, while the primary person needs treatment of their own psychosis. Variants include folie imposee, folie simultanee, folie communiquee and folie induite, and with more members, folie a trois or folie a famille. Its classification has shifted: ICD-10 had a separate category, F24 induced delusional disorder; DSM-III-R and DSM-IV had shared psychotic disorder. DSM-5 and DSM-5-TR (APA, 2022) dropped it as a separate diagnosis; the secondary person is diagnosed with delusional disorder if criteria are met, otherwise 'other specified schizophrenia spectrum and other psychotic disorder' with the example 'delusional symptoms in partner of individual with delusional disorder'. ICD-11 (WHO, effective 2022) likewise removed the separate category, so cases are coded within the schizophrenia or other primary psychotic disorders block, typically as delusional disorder or as an other specified primary psychotic disorder. Option c is right because, among the choices, ICD-11 places induced (shared) delusional states within the other primary psychotic disorders grouping rather than keeping a separate category.","traps":{"a":"A separate independent category was the older position (ICD-10 F24, DSM-IV shared psychotic disorder), which ICD-11 abolished. It tempts because many textbooks still teach the ICD-10 code.","b":"Schizoaffective disorder requires a concurrent major mood episode with schizophrenia-type symptoms; shared delusions in a dependent partner do not fit it. It tempts as another 'in-between' psychotic diagnosis.","d":"Schizotypal disorder is an enduring pattern of eccentricity and odd beliefs; in ICD-11 it sits in the psychotic block but describes a lasting trait, not an induced delusion. It tempts because it sounds like a milder shared oddness."}},
{"q":"A 28-year-old is found wandering in a different city with no memory of his identity, having travelled there with apparent purpose. The most likely diagnosis is:","concept":"Dissociation is a disruption in the normal integration of consciousness, memory, identity, emotion, perception and behaviour. Dissociative amnesia is an inability to recall important autobiographical information, usually of a traumatic or stressful nature, that is inconsistent with ordinary forgetting and not due to a substance, a neurological condition or another mental disorder. It is most often localised (a specific period) or selective (some events in a period), and less often generalised (one's whole identity and life history). Dissociative fugue is the classic presentation in which the person, with amnesia for identity, suddenly undertakes apparently purposeful travel or bewildered wandering away from home, sometimes assuming a new identity, and appears outwardly normal to others; it typically follows severe stress and ends abruptly, often with amnesia for the fugue itself. Classification matters: DSM-IV had a separate dissociative fugue diagnosis, but DSM-5 and DSM-5-TR (APA, 2022) made it a specifier, 'dissociative amnesia with dissociative fugue', and ICD-11 likewise codes it as 6B61.0 dissociative amnesia with dissociative fugue. The near neighbours: depersonalisation-derealisation disorder involves feeling detached from oneself or the world with intact reality testing and no memory loss; transient global amnesia is a neurological episode in older adults with anterograde amnesia and repetitive questioning, while personal identity is preserved, resolving within 24 hours. Key note: under DSM-5-TR and ICD-11 the formal diagnosis is dissociative amnesia with a fugue specifier, so the amnesia choice is defensible, but the classic answer for purposeful travel plus loss of identity remains fugue. Option b is right because purposeful travel to another city with amnesia for personal identity is the defining picture of dissociative fugue.","traps":{"a":"Dissociative amnesia without the fugue specifier is memory loss without purposeful travel. It tempts because in DSM-5-TR fugue is formally a subtype of dissociative amnesia, but the travel feature points to fugue as the more specific answer.","c":"Depersonalisation disorder involves feelings of unreality or detachment from oneself, with intact memory and identity. It tempts because it is another dissociative disorder, but there is no amnesia or travel.","d":"Transient global amnesia is a neurological syndrome of sudden anterograde amnesia with repetitive questioning, in which the person keeps their personal identity. It tempts because it involves sudden amnesia, but identity loss and purposeful travel rule it out."}},
{"q":"'La belle indifférence' — a striking lack of concern about a serious physical symptom — is classically associated with:","concept":"Conversion disorder, renamed functional neurological symptom disorder in DSM-5 and DSM-5-TR (APA, 2022), involves one or more symptoms of altered voluntary motor or sensory function (weakness or paralysis, abnormal movements, swallowing symptoms, speech symptoms, attacks or seizures, anaesthesia or sensory loss, special sensory symptoms, or mixed), with clinical findings showing incompatibility between the symptom and recognised neurological or medical conditions (for example Hoover's sign in functional leg weakness), causing distress or impairment. DSM-5 removed the old requirement to identify a psychological stressor and the requirement that the symptom is not feigned; ICD-11 calls it dissociative neurological symptom disorder and places it among dissociative disorders. The name 'conversion' comes from Sigmund Freud and Josef Breuer (Studies on Hysteria, 1895), who proposed that unacceptable psychic conflict was 'converted' into a bodily symptom, giving primary gain (keeping the conflict out of awareness) and secondary gain (sympathy, avoided duties). La belle indifference, a phrase associated with Pierre Janet and the hysteria tradition, is a surprising lack of concern about a seemingly serious disability, which psychoanalytic theory read as evidence that the symptom was 'solving' a conflict. The classical exam answer links it to conversion disorder, but research (Stone and colleagues, 2006, systematic review) found it in about 21 percent of conversion patients and about 29 percent of patients with organic disease, so it is neither sensitive nor specific and should not be used diagnostically. Contrast somatic symptom disorder and illness anxiety disorder, where concern is excessive, and factitious disorder, where symptoms are intentionally produced. Option c is right because la belle indifference is the classic descriptor of conversion (functional neurological symptom) disorder.","traps":{"a":"Somatic symptom disorder is defined by excessive thoughts, feelings or behaviours about somatic symptoms (high health anxiety, persistent worry), the opposite of indifference. It tempts because both are in the somatic symptom chapter.","b":"Illness anxiety disorder (formerly hypochondriasis) is preoccupation with having or acquiring a serious illness with few or no symptoms, marked by high concern. It tempts as another somatic-chapter diagnosis, but the attitude is anxiety, not indifference.","d":"Factitious disorder involves deliberate falsification or induction of symptoms to assume the sick role without obvious external rewards (Munchausen's). It tempts because symptoms lack an organic basis, but these patients usually seek medical attention eagerly rather than seeming unconcerned."}},
{"q":"Which Cluster B personality disorder is characterised by a pervasive pattern of grandiosity, need for admiration, and lack of empathy?","concept":"Narcissistic personality disorder (NPD) is a Cluster B ('dramatic, emotional, erratic') personality disorder. DSM-5-TR (APA, 2022) defines it as a pervasive pattern of grandiosity (in fantasy or behaviour), need for admiration, and lack of empathy, beginning by early adulthood and present in a variety of contexts, indicated by five (or more) of nine criteria: a grandiose sense of self-importance; preoccupation with fantasies of unlimited success, power, brilliance, beauty or ideal love; belief that one is 'special' and should associate only with high-status people; need for excessive admiration; a sense of entitlement; interpersonal exploitativeness; lack of empathy; envy of others or belief that others envy them; and arrogant, haughty behaviours or attitudes. The construct draws on psychoanalytic work: Heinz Kohut (1971) saw narcissism as arrested development of a cohesive self (the 'mirror hunger' for admiration), while Otto Kernberg (1975) described a pathological grandiose self defending against rage and envy. Clinicians also distinguish the overt (grandiose) from the covert (vulnerable, hypersensitive) presentation, though DSM criteria mostly capture the grandiose form. Cluster B neighbours are easy to mix up. Borderline personality disorder is about instability of relationships, self-image and affect, with frantic efforts to avoid abandonment and self-harm. Histrionic personality disorder seeks attention through seductive, theatrical, shallow emotionality, rather than admiration for superiority. Antisocial personality disorder is a pattern of disregard for and violation of others' rights (deceit, aggression, irresponsibility, lack of remorse) since age 15, with conduct disorder before 15. A quick memory key: borderline fears abandonment, histrionic wants attention, narcissistic wants admiration, antisocial violates rights. Option c is right because grandiosity, need for admiration and lack of empathy are exactly the three anchor phrases in the DSM-5-TR general description of narcissistic personality disorder.","traps":{"a":"Borderline personality disorder is defined by instability of interpersonal relationships, self-image and affect plus marked impulsivity and fear of abandonment, not grandiosity. It tempts because idealisation-devaluation can look like inflated self-regard.","b":"Histrionic personality disorder is excessive emotionality and attention seeking (theatrical, seductive, suggestible); the person wants to be the centre of attention, not admired as superior. The overlap in 'needing others' attention' is the trap.","d":"Antisocial personality disorder is a pervasive disregard for and violation of the rights of others with lack of remorse; callousness overlaps with low empathy, but grandiosity plus need for admiration is the narcissistic signature."}},
{"q":"A nursing assistant secretly injects insulin into her elderly patients to create medical emergencies, deriving satisfaction from the resulting attention. This is:","concept":"Factitious disorder is the deliberate falsification or induction of physical or psychological signs or symptoms, associated with identified deception, in the ABSENCE of obvious external rewards. DSM-5-TR (APA, 2022) has two forms. Factitious disorder imposed on self: the person presents themselves as ill. Factitious disorder imposed on another (previously called factitious disorder by proxy or Munchausen syndrome by proxy, a term coined by the paediatrician Roy Meadow in 1977): the perpetrator falsifies or induces illness or injury in another person (a child, an elderly or dependent adult, even a pet) and presents that person to others as ill. Critically, the diagnosis is given to the PERPETRATOR, not the victim, and the behaviour is evident even without external incentives. The inferred motive is psychological: gratification from the sick role by association, attention, sympathy, or the drama and control of a medical crisis. Methods include injecting insulin to cause hypoglycaemia, poisoning, suffocation, or contaminating samples. The original 'Munchausen syndrome' was named by Richard Asher (1951) after the tall-tale-telling Baron Munchausen. Contrast this with malingering, which is not a mental disorder but a V/Z-code condition: intentional production of symptoms motivated by EXTERNAL incentives (money, avoiding duty, evading prosecution, obtaining drugs). In conversion disorder (functional neurological symptom disorder) the symptoms are NOT intentionally produced. The examiner's two questions are: is it intentional, and is there an external reward? Intentional plus no external reward equals factitious; intentional plus external reward equals malingering; not intentional equals somatic symptom or conversion disorders. Option b is right because the nurse deliberately induces medical emergencies in other people (her patients) and her reward is internal (attention and satisfaction), which is the definition of factitious disorder imposed on another.","traps":{"a":"Malingering is intentional faking driven by an external incentive such as compensation, drugs or escaping duty. The nurse gains no tangible reward, only attention, so it does not fit; it tempts because both involve deliberate deception.","c":"Antisocial personality disorder is a broad pattern of rule-breaking and violating others' rights; harming patients sounds antisocial, but the stem describes the specific illness-inducing, attention-seeking pattern that DSM-5-TR codes as factitious disorder imposed on another.","d":"Conversion disorder (functional neurological symptom disorder) involves genuine, non-intentional neurological symptoms in the patient themself. Here the symptoms are deliberately produced in someone else, so the intentionality rule excludes it."}},
{"q":"DSM-5-TR criteria for ADHD require symptoms to be present before what age?","concept":"Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) is a neurodevelopmental disorder defined in DSM-5-TR (APA, 2022) by a persistent pattern of inattention and/or hyperactivity-impulsivity that interferes with functioning or development. The core criteria worth memorising: Criterion A requires six or more symptoms of inattention and/or six or more of hyperactivity-impulsivity, persisting for at least 6 months (five or more for adolescents aged 17 and older and adults). Criterion B: several inattentive or hyperactive-impulsive symptoms were present PRIOR TO AGE 12 years. Criterion C: several symptoms are present in two or more settings (home, school, work, with friends). Criterion D: clear evidence of interference with social, academic or occupational functioning. Criterion E: not better explained by another disorder and not occurring exclusively during schizophrenia or another psychotic disorder. Presentations are combined, predominantly inattentive, and predominantly hyperactive/impulsive, with mild, moderate and severe specifiers, plus 'in partial remission'. The history matters for exams. DSM-IV (1994) required that some impairing symptoms be present before age 7. Follow-up research (for example by Barkley and colleagues) showed that age 7 was arbitrary, that retrospective adult recall of onset is unreliable, and that many children, especially inattentive ones, are not noticed until school demands rise. DSM-5 (2013) therefore raised the threshold to 12 and changed 'impairing symptoms' to simply 'several symptoms', also lowering the adult symptom count to five and allowing comorbid autism spectrum disorder. ICD-11 (WHO) similarly speaks of onset in the developmental period, typically early to mid childhood. Option d is right because DSM-5-TR Criterion B requires several symptoms to have been present before age 12 years.","traps":{"a":"Age 5 is not an ADHD threshold in any DSM edition; it may tempt because hyperactivity is often first noticed by preschool teachers, but diagnosis before school age is made cautiously.","b":"Age 7 was the DSM-IV onset criterion (with impairing symptoms); DSM-5 replaced it with 12. This is the classic trap for students who learned the older edition.","c":"Age 10 has no role in the ADHD criteria; it is a plausible-looking midpoint between the old 7 and the current 12."}},
{"q":"Which feature differentiates Bipolar I from Bipolar II disorder?","concept":"Bipolar and related disorders in DSM-5-TR (APA, 2022) are distinguished by the TYPE of elevated mood episode. A manic episode is a distinct period of abnormally and persistently elevated, expansive or irritable mood AND persistently increased activity or energy, lasting at least 1 week and present most of the day nearly every day (or any duration if hospitalisation is needed), with three or more of: inflated self-esteem or grandiosity, decreased need for sleep, pressured speech, flight of ideas, distractibility, increased goal-directed activity or psychomotor agitation, and risky involvement in pleasurable activities (four if mood is only irritable). It causes marked impairment, or requires hospitalisation, or has psychotic features. A hypomanic episode has the same symptom list but lasts at least 4 consecutive days, is an unequivocal change observable by others, and is NOT severe enough to cause marked impairment or hospitalisation; if psychotic features are present the episode is by definition manic. Bipolar I disorder needs at least one lifetime manic episode; major depressive episodes are common but not required. Bipolar II disorder needs at least one hypomanic episode AND at least one major depressive episode, and there must NEVER have been a manic episode. Cyclothymic disorder involves at least 2 years (1 year in children and adolescents) of hypomanic and depressive symptoms that never meet full episode criteria. The terms trace back to Emil Kraepelin's (1899) 'manic-depressive insanity' and Karl Leonhard's (1957) split into bipolar and unipolar forms; David Dunner and colleagues (1976) proposed the Bipolar II category. Bipolar II is not 'milder bipolar': patients spend more time depressed and carry substantial suicide risk. Option b is right because the defining difference is that Bipolar I requires a full manic episode, whereas Bipolar II requires hypomania plus a major depressive episode with no history of mania.","traps":{"a":"This reverses the rule: psychotic features can occur in mania (Bipolar I) and in depressive episodes, but any hypomania with psychosis is reclassified as mania, so psychosis does not mark Bipolar II.","c":"Bipolar II has no manic episodes at all, only hypomanic ones, which have a shorter minimum duration (4 days versus 1 week). The trap is assuming II means 'more' of something.","d":"Bipolar I patients very commonly have major depressive episodes; they are simply not required for the diagnosis. Confusing 'not required' with 'not allowed' is the trap."}},
{"q":"The DSM-5-TR specifier 'with limited prosocial emotions' is applied to which diagnosis?","concept":"Conduct disorder (CD) in DSM-5-TR (APA, 2022) is a repetitive and persistent pattern of behaviour in which the basic rights of others or major age-appropriate societal norms are violated, shown by at least 3 of 15 criteria in the past 12 months (at least one in the past 6 months) across four groups: aggression to people and animals, destruction of property, deceitfulness or theft, and serious violations of rules. Subtypes are childhood-onset (at least one symptom before age 10), adolescent-onset, and unspecified onset, with mild, moderate and severe ratings. DSM-5 (2013) added the specifier 'with limited prosocial emotions' (LPE), which requires at least two of four characteristics shown persistently over at least 12 months and in multiple relationships or settings: lack of remorse or guilt; callous, lack of empathy; unconcerned about performance (at school, work or other important activities); and shallow or deficient affect. The specifier operationalises the callous-unemotional (CU) traits studied by Paul Frick and colleagues (for example Frick and White, 2008), who showed that youths with conduct problems plus CU traits have more severe, instrumental and stable aggression, are less responsive to punishment cues, and have a stronger genetic loading. It is the child counterpart of the affective core of psychopathy described by Hervey Cleckley (1941) and measured by Robert Hare's PCL-R. The important exam fact is the PLACEMENT: DSM-5-TR applies the specifier only to conduct disorder. Antisocial personality disorder, which requires age 18 or more and evidence of conduct disorder before 15, has no such specifier, although DSM-5's Section III alternative model lists a 'with psychopathic features' specifier for it. Option b is right because 'with limited prosocial emotions' is a specifier of conduct disorder in DSM-5-TR.","traps":{"a":"Antisocial personality disorder describes adults and in Section II has no LPE specifier; its psychopathy link (Section III 'with psychopathic features') makes it the most tempting wrong answer.","c":"Oppositional defiant disorder is angry, irritable, argumentative and vindictive behaviour without serious violations of others' rights; its only specifier is severity by number of settings.","d":"Intermittent explosive disorder is recurrent impulsive outbursts out of proportion to provocation, typically followed by distress or regret, which is the opposite of the remorseless profile LPE captures."}},
{"q":"Compared to obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD), obsessive-compulsive personality disorder (OCPD) is characterised by symptoms that are typically:","concept":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) and obsessive-compulsive personality disorder (OCPD) share a name but differ in structure. OCD, in DSM-5-TR's (APA, 2022) obsessive-compulsive and related disorders chapter, involves obsessions (recurrent, intrusive, unwanted thoughts, urges or images that cause anxiety) and/or compulsions (repetitive behaviours or mental acts performed to reduce that anxiety or prevent a feared event). The person attempts to ignore, suppress or neutralise them, and they are experienced as ego-dystonic: alien to one's self-image and distressing. DSM-5-TR uses insight specifiers (good or fair, poor, absent insight/delusional beliefs), and most patients recognise their beliefs are excessive. OCPD is a Cluster C personality disorder: a pervasive pattern of preoccupation with orderliness, perfectionism, and mental and interpersonal control at the expense of flexibility, openness and efficiency, shown by four or more of eight criteria (preoccupation with details, rules and lists; perfectionism that interferes with completion; excessive devotion to work; overconscientiousness about morality; inability to discard worthless objects; reluctance to delegate; miserliness; rigidity and stubbornness). These traits are ego-syntonic: they fit the person's self-concept and are experienced as correct, so the person sees others as the problem and often has limited insight into the costs. Distress, when present, is usually about others' reactions rather than the traits. The ego-syntonic versus ego-dystonic distinction comes from psychoanalytic ego psychology (Sigmund Freud and later ego psychologists) and is the classic way to separate a personality disorder from a symptom disorder. The two conditions can co-occur, and hoarding is now its own disorder. Option b is right because OCPD traits are typically ego-syntonic and accompanied by limited insight, whereas OCD symptoms are ego-dystonic with variable but usually present insight.","traps":{"a":"Ego-dystonic symptoms with good insight describe OCD itself, not OCPD; the trap is reading the stem's comparison backwards.","c":"Personality disorders are enduring and not confined to mood episodes; a pattern only present during mania would be attributed to the bipolar disorder.","d":"Personality disorders are by definition enduring, inflexible and stable from adolescence or early adulthood, not acute and quickly remitting; this describes an episodic condition."}},
{"q":"Pibloktoq, characterised by sudden dissociation, screaming, and erratic behaviour, is a culture-bound syndrome described in which population?","concept":"Culture-bound syndromes (DSM-IV's term; DSM-5-TR, APA, 2022, now speaks of 'cultural concepts of distress', including cultural syndromes, idioms of distress and cultural explanations) are patterns of distress and behaviour recognised within particular cultural groups. Pibloktoq, often called 'Arctic hysteria', was described among Inuit (Inughuit) people of Greenland and the Arctic, notably in the accounts of early 20th-century polar explorers such as Robert Peary's expeditions (described by A. A. Brill, 1913). An episode is an abrupt dissociative state lasting up to about 30 minutes: extreme excitement, screaming, tearing off clothing, running out into the snow or ice, mimicking speech (echolalia), and sometimes breaking furniture or eating faeces, followed by convulsive seizures or deep sleep and amnesia for the episode. Proposed explanations include hypocalcaemia or vitamin A toxicity, seasonal light deprivation, and cultural stress; some anthropologists (for example Lyle Dick, 1995) argue it was partly a product of explorers' contact and documentation. A must-know comparison table: amok (Malaysia, Indonesia, typically men): brooding followed by a sudden violent frenzy and amnesia; koro (Southeast Asia, China): acute fear that the genitals will retract into the body and cause death; dhat syndrome (South Asia, especially young men): anxiety and fatigue attributed to loss of semen; susto (Latin America): fright causing soul loss; latah (Malaysia, Indonesia): hypersensitivity to startle with echolalia and echopraxia; ataque de nervios (Latino Caribbean): screaming, crying and aggression after a family stressor; taijin kyofusho (Japan): fear of offending others; brain fag (West Africa): students' concentration and head symptoms. Option b is right because pibloktoq is the classic Arctic hysteria described among Inuit and other Arctic communities.","traps":{"a":"South Asian syndromes include dhat (semen-loss anxiety, mostly in men) and possession states; pibloktoq is not South Asian, and the 'Hindu women' detail is a distractor.","c":"Malay men are linked to amok, a sudden violent frenzy after brooding; its dissociation and amnesia resemble pibloktoq, which is the trap, but amok is homicidal rather than a screaming, running flight.","d":"West Africa is associated with brain fag syndrome (students' head and concentration complaints), not with pibloktoq."}},
{"q":"The hallmark difference between pyromania and intermittent explosive disorder (IED) is:","concept":"Both pyromania and intermittent explosive disorder (IED) sit in the DSM-5-TR (APA, 2022) chapter on disruptive, impulse-control and conduct disorders, but they differ in the behaviour, its build-up and its motive. Pyromania requires deliberate and purposeful fire setting on more than one occasion; tension or affective arousal before the act; fascination with, interest in, curiosity about or attraction to fire and its contexts (fire engines, firefighting); and pleasure, gratification or relief when setting fires or witnessing their aftermath. Crucially, the fire is NOT set for monetary gain, ideology, to conceal crime, out of anger or vengeance, to improve living circumstances, or because of delusions, hallucinations or impaired judgement, and it is not better explained by conduct disorder, mania or antisocial personality disorder. It is rare and diagnosed far less often than fire setting itself. IED is recurrent behavioural outbursts representing a failure to control aggressive impulses: either verbal aggression or physical aggression without damage or injury, twice weekly on average for 3 months, or three outbursts involving damage, destruction or physical injury within 12 months. The aggression is grossly out of proportion to the provocation, is impulsive or anger-based rather than premeditated or instrumental, causes distress or impairment, and the person must be at least 6 years old. Its research base owes much to Emil Coccaro, who linked it to serotonergic dysfunction. The useful contrast: pyromania is a planned, repetitive act with a fire-specific attraction and pleasure, while IED is an unplanned, reactive explosion of anger triggered by minor provocation, often followed by remorse. Option b is right because it captures exactly this contrast: deliberate, gratifying fire setting versus impulsive, disproportionate aggression.","traps":{"a":"IED criteria explicitly include verbal aggression OR physical aggression (with or without damage); limiting it to verbal outbursts misreads Criterion A.","c":"Neither disorder is age-restricted this way; IED needs a minimum age of 6 and pyromania can be diagnosed in children, adolescents or adults. Age splits tempt because conduct disorder versus antisocial personality disorder do work that way.","d":"Neither requires a mood disorder; in fact pyromania is excluded if fire setting is better explained by mania, and IED is excluded if outbursts occur only during another disorder."}},
{"q":"A patient presents with non-fluent, effortful speech but intact comprehension after a left frontal stroke. The most likely lesion site is:","concept":"Aphasia is an acquired language disorder from brain damage, usually left-hemisphere stroke in right-handers. The classical Wernicke-Lichtheim (Wernicke-Geschwind) model sorts aphasias by three bedside tests: fluency, comprehension and repetition. Broca's aphasia (expressive or non-fluent aphasia) was described by Paul Broca (1861) from his patient Leborgne ('Tan'), whose lesion was in the posterior part of the left inferior frontal gyrus. Broca's area corresponds to the pars opercularis and pars triangularis (Brodmann areas 44 and 45), next to the motor cortex controlling face and mouth. Patients produce slow, effortful, halting, telegraphic speech with agrammatism (missing function words and inflections), and often apraxia of speech; comprehension of everyday speech is relatively preserved, though complex syntax may be impaired; repetition is poor; and patients are aware of and frustrated by their errors. Right hemiparesis (arm more than leg) is common because the lesion sits near the motor strip. Wernicke's aphasia, described by Carl Wernicke (1874), follows damage to the posterior superior temporal gyrus (BA 22): speech is fluent but empty, with paraphasias and neologisms, comprehension is poor, and patients often lack awareness. Conduction aphasia (arcuate fasciculus or supramarginal region) has fluent speech and good comprehension but disproportionately poor repetition. Global aphasia impairs everything. Transcortical aphasias spare repetition. Angular gyrus lesions give alexia with agraphia, and with neighbouring damage Gerstmann syndrome (finger agnosia, left-right confusion, acalculia, agraphia). Modern imaging (Dronkers and colleagues, 2007, re-scanning Leborgne's brain) shows lasting Broca's aphasia usually involves deeper and wider damage than BA 44 and 45 alone. Option b is right because non-fluent effortful speech with intact comprehension after a left frontal stroke is Broca's aphasia, localised to Broca's area in the inferior frontal gyrus.","traps":{"a":"Wernicke's area damage gives fluent but meaningless speech with poor comprehension, the mirror image of this patient; it tempts because it is the other famous language area.","c":"Arcuate fasciculus lesions cause conduction aphasia: fluent speech and good comprehension but poor repetition, so the non-fluency here does not fit.","d":"Angular gyrus damage (parietal) produces alexia with agraphia and parts of Gerstmann syndrome, not effortful non-fluent speech, and the stem says frontal."}},
{"q":"The patient H.M. (Henry Molaison) sustained dense anterograde amnesia after surgical removal of which structures?","concept":"Henry Gustav Molaison (1926 to 2008), known as H.M., had intractable epilepsy and in 1953 the neurosurgeon William Beecher Scoville removed the medial temporal lobes on both sides: roughly the anterior two-thirds of the hippocampus, the amygdala, and much of the entorhinal and perirhinal cortex. William Scoville and Brenda Milner (1957) reported the result: a profound anterograde amnesia, the inability to form new long-term declarative memories, alongside a temporally graded retrograde amnesia for some years before surgery. Several capacities were SPARED, and these dissociations are what make the case foundational. His IQ and personality were essentially intact. His short-term memory (digit span) was normal, showing that short-term and long-term stores are separable. He could learn new motor skills: in mirror-tracing (Milner, 1962) he improved across days while denying he had ever done the task, demonstrating that procedural (non-declarative) memory does not depend on the hippocampus. He also showed perceptual priming. Later work by Suzanne Corkin (who studied him for nearly five decades) and post-mortem MRI and histology (Annese and colleagues, 2014) refined the lesion map. H.M. led to the modern taxonomy of declarative (explicit: episodic and semantic) versus non-declarative (implicit: procedural, priming, conditioning) memory, formalised by Larry Squire, and to the view that the hippocampus is needed for consolidating new declarative memories, not for permanent storage. Compare: Korsakoff amnesia is diencephalic (mammillary bodies, thalamus), Phineas Gage was frontal, and Clive Wearing had herpes encephalitis damaging the hippocampi. Option a is right because H.M.'s operation was a bilateral medial temporal lobe resection that included the hippocampus and amygdala, which produced his dense anterograde amnesia.","traps":{"b":"Bilateral prefrontal damage causes executive and personality changes (as in frontal lobotomy patients or Phineas Gage), not dense anterograde amnesia; it tempts because lobotomies were also 1950s psychosurgery.","c":"Left parietal and angular gyrus damage gives alexia, agraphia, acalculia and Gerstmann features, which are language and number problems, not amnesia.","d":"Right occipital and lingual gyrus damage affects vision (left visual field loss, topographic or colour processing), not memory consolidation."}},
{"q":"Phineas Gage's personality change after his accident is most classically attributed to damage to which brain region?","concept":"Phineas Gage (1823 to 1860) was a railway construction foreman in Vermont. In September 1848 a premature explosion drove a 3 foot 7 inch iron tamping rod (about 6 kg) up through his left cheek, behind his left eye, and out through the top of his skull. He survived and remained conscious, and his physician John Martyn Harlow reported (1848, 1868) that his intellect seemed preserved but his character changed: previously efficient and well balanced, he became fitful, irreverent, profane, impatient of restraint, obstinate yet capricious, unable to carry out plans; friends said he was 'no longer Gage'. Hanna Damasio and colleagues (1994), reconstructing the skull computationally, placed the damage in the ventromedial prefrontal cortex (vmPFC), including orbitofrontal regions, probably bilaterally; later reconstructions (Ratiu and colleagues, 2004; Van Horn and colleagues, 2012) suggest the damage was largely left-sided with white-matter disconnection. Antonio Damasio used Gage and modern patients such as 'Elliot' to build the somatic marker hypothesis (1994): the vmPFC links situations with bodily emotional signals that guide decisions, so damage leaves IQ intact but wrecks real-life judgement, social conduct and planning, shown on the Iowa Gambling Task (Bechara and colleagues, 1994). Note historian Malcolm Macmillan's caution that Gage later worked as a stagecoach driver in Chile, so the change may have partly recovered. Distinguish frontal syndromes: orbitofrontal or ventromedial lesions produce disinhibition and poor social judgement; dorsolateral lesions produce executive dysfunction (planning, set shifting, perseveration on the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test); medial or anterior cingulate lesions produce apathy and abulia. Option c is right because Gage's personality change is classically attributed to damage to the ventromedial prefrontal cortex.","traps":{"a":"The cerebellum coordinates movement and balance; damage gives ataxia and intention tremor, not personality change, and the rod passed nowhere near it.","b":"Temporal lobe damage is linked to memory loss (H.M.), language comprehension problems or Kluver-Bucy features; the rod went through the frontal lobe, so this confuses Gage with H.M.","d":"Occipital damage produces visual field defects and visual agnosias; the rod exited through the top of the frontal skull, not the back of the head."}},
{"q":"Endel Tulving's patient K.C. is famous for selectively losing which form of memory while preserving others?","concept":"Endel Tulving (1972) divided long-term declarative memory into episodic memory (personally experienced events located in time and place, with 'autonoetic' or self-knowing consciousness, a sense of mentally re-living the past) and semantic memory (general knowledge of facts, words and concepts, with 'noetic' consciousness, knowing without re-living). Procedural memory (skills and habits) and priming are non-declarative. Patient K.C. (Kent Cochrane, 1951 to 2014) was a Toronto man who, at age 30 in 1981, had a motorcycle accident causing diffuse brain injury including bilateral hippocampal and medial temporal damage. Tulving (1985, and with Shayna Rosenbaum and colleagues, 2005) showed that K.C. could not recollect a single specific personal event from any period of his life, even dramatic ones such as his brother's death or a train derailment near his home, and he also could not imagine his personal future, which inspired the idea that episodic memory supports 'mental time travel' both backward and forward. Yet his semantic memory for general and even personal facts (where he lived, the name of his school, the family's cottage) was largely preserved, his intelligence and language were normal, his short-term memory was intact, and he could slowly acquire some new semantic knowledge and skills through repetition. This dissociation is the strongest single-case evidence that episodic and semantic memory are separable systems. Compare H.M., whose problem was mainly anterograde (new learning) across declarative memory, and patients with semantic dementia (anterior temporal atrophy) who show the reverse pattern: lost semantic knowledge with relatively preserved recent episodic memory. Option c is right because K.C. is famous for losing episodic (autobiographical) memory while semantic, procedural and working memory were largely preserved.","traps":{"a":"Procedural memory (skills) was spared in K.C., as in H.M.; it is the classic 'preserved' system in amnesia, which is why it tempts.","b":"Semantic memory was the system K.C. largely kept, so this is the reverse of the finding; it is semantic dementia patients who lose semantic memory.","d":"Working memory, in the Baddeley and Hitch (1974) sense, was intact in K.C.; amnesia from medial temporal damage typically spares short-term and working memory."}},
{"q":"Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome is caused by deficiency of which vitamin?","concept":"Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome (WKS) is a spectrum of brain injury caused by thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency. Thiamine, as thiamine pyrophosphate, is a cofactor for key glucose-metabolism enzymes (pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, transketolase). When it runs out, regions with high metabolic demand fail: the mammillary bodies, the medial (dorsomedial and anterior) thalamus, periaqueductal grey and areas around the third and fourth ventricles. The commonest cause is chronic alcohol use (poor diet, impaired absorption and storage), but it also follows hyperemesis gravidarum, bariatric surgery, starvation, and prolonged vomiting. Wernicke's encephalopathy, described by Carl Wernicke (1881), is the ACUTE phase, with the classic triad of ophthalmoplegia (or nystagmus), ataxia and global confusion, although the full triad is seen in only a minority, so clinicians treat on suspicion. It is a medical emergency: high-dose parenteral thiamine is given, and glucose must not be given before thiamine, because glucose loading can precipitate or worsen it. Korsakoff's syndrome, described by Sergei Korsakoff (1887), is the CHRONIC residual state: dense anterograde amnesia with retrograde amnesia, confabulation (filling memory gaps with fabrications), apathy and lack of insight, with relatively preserved intelligence and procedural memory. MRI may show mammillary body atrophy. In DSM-5-TR (APA, 2022) it falls under alcohol-induced major neurocognitive disorder, amnestic-confabulatory type. Remember the nearby vitamins: B12 (cobalamin) deficiency causes subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord, megaloblastic anaemia and a reversible dementia; B3 (niacin) deficiency causes pellagra (dermatitis, diarrhoea, dementia); B6 deficiency causes peripheral neuropathy and seizures. Option c is right because Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome is caused by deficiency of vitamin B1, thiamine.","traps":{"a":"Pyridoxine (B6) deficiency causes peripheral neuropathy, seizures and anaemia, and isoniazid therapy can deplete it, but it does not cause Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome.","b":"B12 deficiency causes neuropsychiatric symptoms, megaloblastic anaemia and subacute combined degeneration, so it is the commonest 'vitamin and dementia' confusion, but WKS is a B1 problem.","d":"Vitamin D deficiency affects bone (rickets, osteomalacia) and is associated with mood symptoms, but it has no link to the WKS amnesia and eye-movement picture."}},
{"q":"The basal ganglia primarily regulate:","concept":"The basal ganglia are a group of interconnected subcortical nuclei: the striatum (caudate nucleus and putamen; the ventral striatum includes the nucleus accumbens), the globus pallidus (external and internal segments), the subthalamic nucleus, and the substantia nigra (pars compacta, which supplies dopamine, and pars reticulata, an output nucleus). They sit in cortico-basal ganglia-thalamo-cortical loops described by Garrett Alexander, Mahlon DeLong and Peter Strick (1986), which include motor, oculomotor, prefrontal and limbic loops. In the motor loop the basal ganglia do not issue movement commands themselves; they act as a gate that selects wanted movements and suppresses unwanted ones. The direct pathway (striatum to internal pallidus, D1 receptors) disinhibits the thalamus and facilitates movement; the indirect pathway (via external pallidus and subthalamic nucleus, D2 receptors) increases inhibition and suppresses movement. Dopamine from the substantia nigra pars compacta boosts the direct and dampens the indirect pathway. The basal ganglia also support reinforcement-driven habit and motor-sequence learning (dopamine prediction-error signals, Wolfram Schultz, 1997). Disorders follow directly: Parkinson's disease (loss of nigral dopamine neurons) is hypokinetic, with bradykinesia, rigidity, resting tremor and postural instability; Huntington's disease (loss of striatal indirect-pathway neurons, CAG repeat on chromosome 4) is hyperkinetic with chorea plus cognitive and psychiatric decline; subthalamic lesions give hemiballismus; dysfunction also appears in Tourette's disorder and OCD (cortico-striatal loops). Antipsychotic D2 blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway causes extrapyramidal side effects. Contrast the cerebellum, which coordinates and fine-tunes movement and balance rather than gating its initiation. Option a is right because the basal ganglia's primary role is initiating and gating voluntary movement and learning motor sequences and habits.","traps":{"b":"Visual processing and depth perception are handled by the occipital cortex and dorsal and ventral visual streams, not the basal ganglia.","c":"Long-term declarative memory consolidation depends on the hippocampus and medial temporal lobe; the basal ganglia support procedural or habit learning, which is the subtle trap.","d":"Cardiorespiratory control is a brainstem (medulla and pons) function, not a basal ganglia one."}},
{"q":"The reticular activating system (RAS) is primarily responsible for:","concept":"The reticular activating system (RAS) is the arousal network running from the brainstem reticular formation (a diffuse core of neurons in the medulla, pons and midbrain) up to the thalamus, hypothalamus, basal forebrain and cortex. Its foundational demonstration came from Giuseppe Moruzzi and Horace Magoun (1949): electrical stimulation of the midbrain reticular formation in anaesthetised cats switched the EEG from slow synchronised waves to the fast, desynchronised pattern of wakefulness, and lesions there produced a coma-like state (Frederic Bremer's 1935 'cerveau isole' midbrain transection had earlier produced constant sleep-like EEG). Today the 'ascending arousal system' is understood as several neurochemical streams: cholinergic neurons (pedunculopontine and laterodorsal tegmental nuclei, basal forebrain), noradrenergic locus coeruleus, serotonergic raphe nuclei, dopaminergic VTA, histaminergic tuberomammillary nucleus, and orexin (hypocretin) neurons in the lateral hypothalamus. One branch relays through the intralaminar thalamic nuclei, another bypasses the thalamus to the cortex directly. The RAS regulates wakefulness, sleep-wake transitions (with the hypothalamic 'flip-flop' switch described by Clifford Saper), alertness, orienting and the gating of sensory input for selective attention. Damage to the upper brainstem or bilateral thalamus causes coma or persistent impaired consciousness, while orexin loss causes narcolepsy. Many drugs act here: general anaesthetics and sedatives suppress the system, while stimulants enhance it. Do not confuse the RAS with the limbic system (emotion and memory), the cerebellum (coordination) or the language cortex. Option a is right because the reticular activating system is primarily responsible for arousal, wakefulness and the modulation of consciousness.","traps":{"b":"Long-term episodic memory depends on the hippocampus and medial temporal lobe, not the brainstem arousal network.","c":"Voluntary motor planning is a function of premotor and supplementary motor cortex with the basal ganglia and cerebellum, not the RAS; descending reticulospinal tracts influence posture, which is the faint trap.","d":"Reading comprehension relies on left-hemisphere language areas such as Wernicke's area and the angular gyrus; the RAS only supplies the alertness that reading needs."}},
{"q":"Which of the following hormones is NOT secreted by the ANTERIOR pituitary?","concept":"The pituitary gland has two embryologically and functionally different lobes. The anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis) develops from Rathke's pouch (oral ectoderm) and is real glandular tissue. Hypothalamic releasing and inhibiting hormones (CRH, TRH, GnRH, GHRH, somatostatin, dopamine) travel down the hypophyseal portal blood vessels and control it. It MAKES and secretes six hormones, remembered as FLAT PiG: follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH), luteinising hormone (LH), adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH), thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH), prolactin, and growth hormone (GH). Prolactin is unusual in being mainly under inhibitory control by hypothalamic dopamine, which is why D2-blocking antipsychotics raise prolactin (galactorrhoea, amenorrhoea). The posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis) is neural tissue, an extension of the hypothalamus. It does not synthesise hormones: oxytocin and antidiuretic hormone (ADH, vasopressin) are made by magnocellular neurons in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus, transported down the axons of the hypothalamo-hypophyseal tract, stored in nerve terminals and released into the blood. Oxytocin triggers uterine contractions and milk let-down and is involved in social bonding; ADH acts on the kidney to conserve water. The stress axis is a common exam link: the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis runs CRH to ACTH to cortisol, with negative feedback; its hyperactivity in depression was tested with the dexamethasone suppression test (Bernard Carroll, 1981). Hans Selye's (1936, 1956) general adaptation syndrome is the classic stress model built on this axis. Option c is right because oxytocin is produced in the hypothalamus and released from the POSTERIOR pituitary, whereas growth hormone, ACTH and prolactin are anterior pituitary hormones.","traps":{"a":"Growth hormone is secreted by anterior pituitary somatotrophs under GHRH and somatostatin control, so it IS anterior and does not answer a NOT question.","b":"ACTH is secreted by anterior pituitary corticotrophs as the middle step of the HPA stress axis; its fame in stress psychology may make it seem 'special', but it is anterior.","d":"Prolactin is secreted by anterior pituitary lactotrophs; it tempts because, like oxytocin, it relates to breastfeeding, but oxytocin controls milk ejection while prolactin controls milk production."}},
{"q":"Which extrapyramidal side effect of typical antipsychotics typically appears LAST, often after years of treatment, and is the most likely to be irreversible?","concept":"Extrapyramidal side effects (EPS) arise mainly from D2 dopamine receptor blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway by antipsychotics, most often high-potency first-generation drugs such as haloperidol. They appear in a characteristic time sequence. Acute dystonia comes first, within hours to the first few days: sustained involuntary muscle spasms such as torticollis, oculogyric crisis, tongue protrusion or opisthotonus, commonest in young males; it is treated with an anticholinergic (benztropine, trihexyphenidyl) or antihistamine. Akathisia follows within days to weeks: subjective inner restlessness with an urge to move, pacing and inability to sit still; it is often mistaken for agitation and is linked to suicidality and non-adherence; propranolol is a first-line treatment. Drug-induced parkinsonism develops over weeks to a few months: bradykinesia, rigidity (cogwheeling), tremor and masked face; it responds to dose reduction or anticholinergics. Tardive dyskinesia (TD) appears LAST, usually after months to years of exposure (DSM-5-TR, APA, 2022, requires at least a few months of use, or one month in people aged 60 or over): involuntary choreoathetoid movements, especially of the mouth, lips and tongue (chewing, lip smacking, tongue darting), and sometimes trunk and limbs. The favoured mechanism is D2 receptor supersensitivity (upregulation) after chronic blockade, which explains why reducing the dose can transiently worsen it and why it is often irreversible. Risk is higher with older age, female sex, longer exposure and first-generation drugs; it is assessed with the AIMS. Anticholinergics do not help and may worsen it; VMAT2 inhibitors (valbenazine, deutetrabenazine) are approved treatments. A mnemonic for the order: 'hours, days, weeks, months to years'. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome is a separate idiosyncratic emergency. Option d is right because tardive dyskinesia appears last, after prolonged treatment, and is the extrapyramidal side effect most likely to be irreversible.","traps":{"a":"Acute dystonia is the EARLIEST EPS, occurring within hours to days, and it reverses quickly with anticholinergics, so it is the opposite of the stem.","b":"Akathisia develops within days to weeks of starting or increasing the dose and usually remits when the drug is reduced; its distress and suicide link make it feel serious, but it is not the late, irreversible one.","c":"Drug-induced parkinsonism appears within weeks to months and is usually reversible on stopping or reducing the drug; the trap is that it is the latest of the three 'early' EPS."}},
{"q":"The mesolimbic dopamine pathway, hyperactive in untreated schizophrenia, projects from the ventral tegmental area to:","concept":"Four dopamine pathways are tested again and again. The mesolimbic pathway runs from the ventral tegmental area (VTA) in the midbrain to the nucleus accumbens (ventral striatum) and other limbic structures such as the amygdala and hippocampus; it is central to reward, motivation and salience, and its hyperactivity is thought to produce the positive symptoms of schizophrenia (hallucinations, delusions). The mesocortical pathway runs from the VTA to the prefrontal cortex, including the dorsolateral and ventromedial regions; hypoactivity here is linked to negative and cognitive symptoms. The nigrostriatal pathway runs from the substantia nigra pars compacta to the dorsal striatum (caudate and putamen) and controls movement; its degeneration causes Parkinson's disease and its blockade by antipsychotics causes extrapyramidal side effects. The tuberoinfundibular pathway runs from the arcuate nucleus of the hypothalamus to the median eminence and pituitary, where dopamine inhibits prolactin release; blockade causes hyperprolactinaemia. The dopamine hypothesis of schizophrenia grew from Arvid Carlsson's work (Carlsson and Lindqvist, 1963) and from Philip Seeman's (1975, 1976) finding that antipsychotic potency correlates with D2 binding. It was revised by Kenneth Davis and colleagues (1991) into the subcortical hyperdopaminergic, cortical hypodopaminergic version, and by Oliver Howes and Shitij Kapur (2009) into 'version III', with presynaptic striatal dopamine synthesis as the final common path; Kapur (2003) also framed psychosis as aberrant salience. Imaging shows the excess is most marked in the associative (dorsal) striatum, a nuance worth knowing for advanced questions, though the textbook answer remains mesolimbic. Option a is right because the mesolimbic pathway projects from the ventral tegmental area to the nucleus accumbens (ventral striatum) and limbic structures.","traps":{"b":"VTA to dorsolateral prefrontal cortex is the MESOCORTICAL pathway, linked to negative and cognitive symptoms; it shares the VTA origin, which is the trap.","c":"The caudate and putamen are the target of the NIGROSTRIATAL pathway from the substantia nigra, which controls movement and underlies EPS; it is not a VTA projection.","d":"The tuberoinfundibular system runs from the hypothalamus to the pituitary and regulates prolactin; it is a separate pathway, not a target of the VTA."}},
{"q":"The primary inhibitory neurotransmitter of the mammalian central nervous system is:","concept":"Neurotransmitters are excitatory or inhibitory depending on the receptors they act on, but two amino acids carry most fast signalling in the brain. Gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA) is the main inhibitory neurotransmitter of the mammalian central nervous system. It is synthesised from glutamate by the enzyme glutamic acid decarboxylase (GAD), with vitamin B6 as cofactor, and is used by roughly 20 to 30 percent of brain synapses, largely interneurons and also cerebellar Purkinje cells and striatal projection neurons. GABA-A receptors are ligand-gated chloride channels: chloride influx hyperpolarises the neuron (fast inhibitory postsynaptic potentials). They have separate binding sites for benzodiazepines (which increase how often the channel opens), barbiturates (which increase how long it stays open), alcohol, neurosteroids and some anaesthetics, which explains cross-tolerance and the danger of combining these depressants and of withdrawal seizures. GABA-B receptors are G-protein coupled, opening potassium channels for slower inhibition (baclofen is an agonist). GABA was identified in brain by Eugene Roberts and Jorge Awapara (1950), and its inhibitory role was established by Ernst Florey and Krnjevic in the 1950s and 1960s. Glycine is the other main inhibitory amino acid, dominant in the spinal cord and brainstem (strychnine blocks its receptor, causing convulsions). Glutamate is the main EXCITATORY neurotransmitter, acting on AMPA, kainate and NMDA receptors, with roles in learning (long-term potentiation) and in excitotoxicity. Dopamine and acetylcholine are modulatory and can excite or inhibit depending on receptor subtype. Low GABA function is implicated in anxiety and epilepsy. Option b is right because GABA is the primary inhibitory neurotransmitter of the mammalian central nervous system.","traps":{"a":"Glutamate is the primary EXCITATORY neurotransmitter, and it is GABA's precursor; the pairing makes it the classic swap trap.","c":"Dopamine is a modulatory monoamine whose D2-family receptors are inhibitory, but it is not the brain's main inhibitory transmitter; it acts in specific pathways only.","d":"Acetylcholine is excitatory at nicotinic receptors (for example the neuromuscular junction) and mixed at muscarinic receptors; it is not the main CNS inhibitory transmitter."}},
{"q":"Which hypothalamic nucleus is the master circadian pacemaker, receiving direct input from the retina?","concept":"Circadian rhythms are roughly 24-hour cycles in physiology and behaviour (sleep-wake, core body temperature, cortisol, melatonin). The master clock is the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN), a pair of small nuclei in the anterior hypothalamus sitting just above the optic chiasm. Evidence came from Robert Moore and Victor Eichler (1972) and Friedrich Stephan and Irving Zucker (1972), who showed that SCN lesions in rats abolish circadian rhythms of drinking, activity and corticosterone, and later from transplant studies by Martin Ralph and Michael Menaker (1990), in which SCN tissue from a mutant 'tau' hamster with a short (about 20-hour) period imposed its own period on a lesioned host. The clock ticks via a molecular transcription-translation feedback loop (CLOCK and BMAL1 drive the Period and Cryptochrome genes, whose proteins then inhibit them), work recognised by the 2017 Nobel Prize to Jeffrey Hall, Michael Rosbash and Michael Young. Because the intrinsic period is not exactly 24 hours, it must be entrained by zeitgebers (time-givers, a term from Jurgen Aschoff), chiefly light. Light reaches the SCN directly via the retinohypothalamic tract, driven by intrinsically photosensitive retinal ganglion cells containing melanopsin. The SCN then signals, via the paraventricular nucleus and sympathetic chain, the pineal gland, which secretes melatonin in darkness. Clinical links include jet lag, shift work disorder, delayed sleep phase in adolescents, and seasonal affective disorder (bright light therapy). Neighbouring nuclei: the paraventricular nucleus controls the stress (CRH) and autonomic responses and makes oxytocin and ADH; the ventromedial nucleus is the classic satiety centre; the lateral hypothalamus is the hunger centre; and the mammillary bodies are memory relays damaged in Korsakoff syndrome. Option a is right because the suprachiasmatic nucleus is the master circadian pacemaker and receives direct retinal input.","traps":{"b":"The paraventricular nucleus drives the stress response (CRH), autonomic output and oxytocin and ADH production; it relays SCN signals to the pineal but is not itself the pacemaker.","c":"The ventromedial nucleus is the classic satiety centre (lesions cause overeating and obesity in rats), not the circadian clock.","d":"The mammillary bodies are part of the Papez memory circuit and are damaged in Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome; they have no pacemaker role."}},
{"q":"The cerebellum's primary functions include:","concept":"The cerebellum ('little brain') sits behind the brainstem, contains more than half of the brain's neurons, and connects via three pairs of peduncles. Functionally it has three divisions: the vestibulocerebellum (flocculonodular lobe), which controls balance and eye movements; the spinocerebellum (vermis and intermediate zones), which regulates posture, muscle tone and ongoing limb movement using proprioceptive feedback; and the cerebrocerebellum (lateral hemispheres), which plans the timing and sequencing of skilled movement in loops with the cerebral cortex. Its central computation compares intended movement (an efference copy) with actual sensory feedback and corrects errors, so movements become smooth, accurate and well timed. David Marr (1969) and James Albus (1971) proposed that climbing fibres from the inferior olive signal errors that adjust Purkinje cell synapses, the basis of cerebellar motor learning, shown in eyeblink conditioning (Richard Thompson) and adaptation to prism goggles. The cerebellum influences the SAME side of the body. Damage produces ataxia (wide-based staggering gait), dysmetria (past-pointing on finger-nose testing), intention tremor (worse near the target, unlike Parkinson's resting tremor), dysdiadochokinesia (difficulty with rapid alternating movements), scanning or slurred speech, nystagmus and hypotonia. Alcohol intoxication mimics cerebellar signs. Beyond movement, Jeremy Schmahmann (1998) described the cerebellar cognitive affective syndrome (executive, spatial, language and affective changes), and the cerebellum contributes to timing and is implicated in autism, but the classical answer remains coordination and balance. Contrast the basal ganglia, which initiate and gate movements, and motor cortex, which issues commands. Option b is right because the cerebellum's primary functions are coordination of movement, balance, posture and motor learning.","traps":{"a":"Initiation of voluntary movement is a basal ganglia and motor cortex function, and language production is Broca's area; the cerebellum fine-tunes movement rather than starting it.","c":"Long-term declarative memory depends on the hippocampus and neocortex; the cerebellum stores procedural or conditioned learning, which makes this tempting but wrong.","d":"Emotion regulation via the amygdala is a limbic and prefrontal function; Schmahmann's cerebellar affective findings exist but are not the cerebellum's primary role."}},
{"q":"According to Erikson, the central psychosocial crisis of adolescence (12-18 years) is:","concept":"Erik H. Erikson (Childhood and Society, 1950; Identity: Youth and Crisis, 1968) extended Freud's psychosexual stages into eight lifelong psychosocial stages, each defined by a crisis (a turning point between two poles) and a virtue gained if it is resolved favourably. Stages are governed by the epigenetic principle: each has its ascendant time but builds on the earlier ones. The sequence: trust vs mistrust (infancy, hope); autonomy vs shame and doubt (early childhood, will); initiative vs guilt (play age, about 3 to 6, purpose); industry vs inferiority (school age, about 6 to 12, competence); identity vs role confusion (adolescence, about 12 to 18, fidelity); intimacy vs isolation (young adulthood, love); generativity vs stagnation (middle adulthood, care); and integrity vs despair (late adulthood, wisdom). In adolescence, puberty, new cognitive capacities and social demands push the young person to integrate childhood identifications, abilities and roles into a coherent, continuous sense of who they are and where they are going. Erikson described a psychosocial moratorium, a socially sanctioned delay of adult commitments allowing role experimentation, and warned of identity diffusion and negative identity. James Marcia (1966) operationalised this as four identity statuses based on exploration (crisis) and commitment: identity achievement (explored and committed), moratorium (exploring, not yet committed), foreclosure (committed without exploring, often adopting parents' values) and identity diffusion (neither). Fidelity, the virtue, is the ability to sustain loyalties freely pledged. Remember that Erikson coined the phrase 'identity crisis'. Option b is right because Erikson's central crisis of adolescence (roughly 12 to 18 years) is identity versus role confusion.","traps":{"a":"Industry vs inferiority is stage 4, the school age (about 6 to 12), centred on competence through skills and schoolwork; it is the stage just before adolescence.","c":"Intimacy vs isolation is stage 6, young adulthood, and Erikson argued true intimacy requires a secure identity first; it is tempting because adolescents begin romantic relationships.","d":"Generativity vs stagnation is stage 7, middle adulthood, about guiding the next generation through parenting, work and mentoring."}},
{"q":"Erikson's stage of late adulthood (65+) involves the psychosocial conflict of:","concept":"Erik H. Erikson's (1950, 1982) eighth and final psychosocial stage, ego integrity versus despair, belongs to late adulthood (roughly 65 onward). Facing retirement, bodily decline, the deaths of peers and one's own mortality, the older adult looks back over the life lived. Integrity is the acceptance of 'one's one and only life cycle as something that had to be', with its successes and failures, a sense of coherence and meaning, and a feeling of connection to past and future generations. Despair is the feeling that time is too short to start another life and try other routes to integrity, expressed as regret, bitterness, disgust with others, and fear of death. The virtue that emerges from a favourable balance is wisdom, 'informed and detached concern with life itself in the face of death itself'. Robert Butler (1963) independently described the life review, a naturally occurring process of reminiscence in old age that can lead to integration or to depression, which became the basis of life-review and reminiscence therapy. In The Life Cycle Completed (extended version, 1997) Joan Erikson proposed a ninth stage for the very old, in which the earlier crises are revisited with physical dependence, connected to gerotranscendence (Lars Tornstam, 1989). Do not confuse this with neighbouring stages: generativity versus stagnation is middle adulthood (care), intimacy versus isolation is young adulthood (love), and trust versus mistrust is infancy (hope). A useful bookend: the first stage gives hope, the last gives wisdom, and Erikson noted that healthy children will not fear life if their elders have integrity enough not to fear death. Option d is right because Erikson's late adulthood stage is integrity versus despair.","traps":{"a":"Trust vs mistrust is the first stage, infancy (roughly birth to 18 months), resolved through reliable caregiving and yielding hope; it tempts only as the mirror bookend of the lifespan.","b":"Generativity vs stagnation is stage 7, middle adulthood (roughly 40 to 65), about caring for the next generation; it is the stage just before old age, so it is the commonest slip.","c":"Intimacy vs isolation is stage 6, young adulthood, centred on forming close committed relationships, not reviewing a completed life."}},
{"q":"In Piaget's theory, the concrete operational stage typically begins at:","concept":"Jean Piaget (for example The Psychology of Intelligence, 1947; Piaget and Inhelder, 1969) proposed that children actively construct knowledge through schemes, adapted by assimilation (fitting new experience into existing schemes) and accommodation (changing schemes), driven by equilibration. He described four invariant, qualitatively different stages. Sensorimotor (birth to about 2): knowledge through action and the senses; six substages; object permanence develops, leading to symbolic thought at the end. Preoperational (about 2 to 7): symbolic function (language, pretend play) but thinking is pre-logical, marked by egocentrism (the three mountains task), centration, irreversibility, animism, and failure of conservation. Concrete operational (about 7 to 11 or 12): the child can perform mental operations, internalised reversible actions, on concrete, real objects: conservation (number about 6 to 7, mass and liquid about 7 to 8, weight about 9 to 10, volume about 11 to 12, the uneven pattern Piaget called horizontal decalage), seriation, transitivity, class inclusion and decentration. Formal operational (about 11 or 12 onward): abstract, hypothetico-deductive and propositional reasoning, shown in the pendulum problem (Inhelder and Piaget, 1958). Ages are approximate averages; the ORDER is what Piaget claimed is universal. Critics such as Margaret Donaldson (1978) and Renee Baillargeon showed that children reveal competence earlier when tasks make 'human sense' or use looking-time methods, and cross-cultural work shows formal operations are not universal. Lev Vygotsky (1978) stressed social and cultural mediation instead. Option c is right because Piaget's concrete operational stage spans roughly 7 to 11 years, so it typically begins around age 7.","traps":{"a":"Birth to 2 years is the sensorimotor stage, where infants learn through action and acquire object permanence.","b":"Ages 2 to 7 are the preoperational stage, the stage BEFORE concrete operations, characterised by egocentrism and failure of conservation; it tempts because it ends at 7.","d":"From about 11 onward is the formal operational stage of abstract, hypothetical reasoning, which follows concrete operations."}},
{"q":"A child can pour water from a tall, thin glass into a short, wide glass and recognise that the AMOUNT of water has not changed. Piaget called this:","concept":"Conservation, in Jean Piaget's theory (Piaget, 1952; Piaget and Inhelder, 1969), is the understanding that certain properties of an object or substance (number, length, mass, liquid quantity, weight, volume) stay the same despite changes in its appearance or arrangement, as long as nothing is added or taken away. In the classic liquid task, a child agrees two identical glasses hold the same amount; one is poured into a taller, thinner glass. A preoperational child (about 2 to 7) says the tall glass has more because of centration, focusing on one dimension (height) and ignoring compensation by the other (width), and because their thinking is tied to perceptual appearance and lacks reversibility. A concrete operational child (about 7 to 11) says it is the same and justifies it with three kinds of argument: identity (nothing was added or removed), reversibility (pour it back and it will be the same), and compensation or reciprocity (taller but thinner). Conservation does not appear all at once: number around 6 to 7, liquid and mass around 7 to 8, weight around 9 to 10, and volume around 11 to 12, an unevenness Piaget called horizontal decalage. Training studies and Margaret Donaldson's work (McGarrigle and Donaldson, 1974, 'naughty teddy') showed that children conserve earlier when the transformation looks accidental, suggesting the standard task's wording misleads them. Note the hierarchy that exam setters exploit: conservation is the ACHIEVEMENT being tested, and reversibility is one of the mental operations that makes it possible. Object permanence (sensorimotor) and egocentrism (preoperational) are different milestones entirely. Option b is right because recognising that the amount of water stays the same despite the change in shape of the glass is what Piaget called conservation.","traps":{"a":"Object permanence is the sensorimotor understanding that objects continue to exist when out of sight, normally established by about 18 to 24 months; it concerns existence, not quantity.","c":"Reversibility is the mental operation of undoing a transformation (pouring it back), one of the justifications children give for conserving; it is the reasoning behind the ability, not the name of the ability itself, which makes it the closest trap.","d":"Egocentrism is the preoperational difficulty in taking another person's viewpoint (the three mountains task); it is not about quantities staying constant."}},
{"q":"Vygotsky's 'zone of proximal development' refers to:","concept":"The zone of proximal development (ZPD) is the central idea of Lev Semyonovich Vygotsky's sociocultural theory of cognitive development, set out in his writings of the early 1930s and made famous in the West through 'Mind in Society' (1978). Vygotsky defined it as the distance between a child's actual developmental level, shown by independent problem solving, and the level of potential development, shown by problem solving under adult guidance or in collaboration with more capable peers. Three bands follow from this: tasks the child can already do alone (below the zone), tasks the child can do only with help (the zone itself), and tasks too hard even with help (beyond the zone). Instruction is useful only when it targets the middle band, because that is where functions are 'in the process of maturing'. The helper is called the More Knowledgeable Other (MKO), who may be a parent, teacher or a slightly more skilled peer. The word 'scaffolding' is not Vygotsky's own: it was introduced by David Wood, Jerome Bruner and Gail Ross (1976) to describe support that is fitted to the learner's current level and gradually withdrawn (fading) as competence grows. Vygotsky also argued that higher mental functions appear twice, first between people (interpsychological) and then inside the child (intrapsychological), and that private speech is the bridge by which external guidance becomes self-regulation. Contrast with Jean Piaget: for Piaget development leads learning and the child is a lone scientist; for Vygotsky learning, done socially, leads development. Do not confuse the ZPD with milestone norms, with brain growth spurts, or with the mental age versus chronological age comparison used in Binet's IQ. Option b is right because the ZPD is exactly the gap between what the child achieves alone and what the child achieves with scaffolded help from an adult or more capable peer.","traps":{"a":"This describes a normative milestone window, as in Arnold Gesell's maturational norms, not Vygotsky's ZPD. It tempts because the ZPD sounds like a developmental 'range', but the ZPD is a gap in ability between solo and assisted performance, not an age band.","c":"This is the early brain growth spurt (rapid synaptogenesis and myelination in infancy), a neurobiological fact unrelated to Vygotsky. It tempts because 'development' and 'zone' suggest a sensitive period, but the ZPD is about social assistance, not brain maturation.","d":"The mental age versus chronological age comparison is Alfred Binet's (1905) and William Stern's (1912) ratio IQ logic. It tempts because it is also a 'distance' between two levels, but it compares the child to age norms, not solo versus assisted performance."}},
{"q":"Bowlby's third phase of attachment behaviour (typically 7-24 months), characterised by clear preference for the primary caregiver and protest at separation, is called:","concept":"John Bowlby (1969, 'Attachment and Loss, Vol. 1: Attachment') proposed that attachment is an evolved behavioural system that keeps a vulnerable infant close to a protective caregiver, and that it unfolds through four phases. Phase 1, pre-attachment (birth to about 6 weeks): the infant uses innate signals such as crying, grasping, gazing and smiling, and is comforted by anyone; there is no discrimination between people. Phase 2, attachment in the making (about 6 weeks to 6 to 8 months): the infant now responds differently to familiar people, smiling and babbling more to the mother, but still does not protest when separated. Phase 3, clear-cut attachment (about 6 to 8 months to 18 to 24 months): a specific attachment figure is now preferred, the infant uses her as a secure base for exploration, actively follows and seeks her, and shows separation protest (separation anxiety); stranger wariness also emerges around 8 to 10 months. Phase 4, formation of a reciprocal relationship or goal-corrected partnership (from about 18 to 24 months): with growing language and representation the child understands the caregiver's goals and plans, so separation protest declines and the child can negotiate. Out of these experiences the child builds an internal working model of self and others. Related findings: Rudolph Schaffer and Peggy Emerson's (1964) Glasgow study gave a parallel sequence (asocial, indiscriminate, specific, multiple attachments) and showed specific attachment around 7 months. Mary Ainsworth later measured individual differences in this attachment in the Strange Situation. The examiner's clue in the stem is 'clear preference plus protest at separation', which is the hallmark of the third phase. Option c is right because clear-cut attachment is Bowlby's third phase, spanning roughly 6 to 8 months through 24 months, when a preferred caregiver and separation protest first appear.","traps":{"a":"Pre-attachment is Bowlby's first phase (birth to about 6 weeks), when the infant signals indiscriminately and is soothed by anyone. It tempts as the familiar first term in the list, but there is no caregiver preference or separation protest yet.","b":"Attachment in the making is the second phase (about 6 weeks to 6 to 8 months): the infant recognises familiar people but does not yet protest separation. It tempts because preference is starting to form, but protest at separation is the marker of the next phase.","d":"Goal-corrected partnership is the fourth phase (from about 2 years), when the child grasps the caregiver's goals and separation protest declines. It tempts because it overlaps the upper end of 24 months, but it is defined by negotiation, not by intense protest."}},
{"q":"In Ainsworth's Strange Situation, an infant who shows distress on separation but cannot be soothed on reunion, and oscillates between clinging and angry resistance, is classified as:","concept":"The Strange Situation is a laboratory procedure devised by Mary D. Salter Ainsworth and Barbara Wittig (1969) and reported fully by Ainsworth, Blehar, Waters and Wall (1978) to assess attachment quality in infants of about 12 to 18 months. It has eight short episodes in which the mother and a stranger enter and leave, so the infant experiences two separations and two reunions. The crucial data are reunion behaviour, not the amount of crying at separation. Ainsworth's original Baltimore sample yielded three patterns. Secure (type B, about 65 to 70 percent): uses mother as a secure base, may be distressed at separation, greets her actively on return, is quickly comforted and returns to play. Insecure-avoidant (type A, about 20 percent): little distress at separation, ignores or turns away from mother on reunion. Insecure-resistant or ambivalent (type C, about 10 to 15 percent): clingy and anxious even before separation, very distressed when left, and on reunion mixes contact seeking with angry resistance, such as reaching to be picked up and then arching away or hitting, and cannot be settled. Mary Main and Judith Solomon (1986, 1990) added a fourth category, disorganised or disoriented (type D): contradictory, frozen or fearful behaviour without a coherent strategy, linked with frightened or frightening caregiving and maltreatment. Ainsworth tied the patterns to maternal sensitivity: sensitive, responsive care yields security; consistent rejection yields avoidance; inconsistent, unpredictable responsiveness yields the resistant pattern, because the infant amplifies demands to secure attention it cannot count on. The stem's cues, distress at separation, failure to be soothed and oscillation between clinging and anger, are the textbook signature of type C. Option c is right because an infant who is distressed, inconsolable on reunion and alternates between seeking contact and angrily resisting it is classified insecure-resistant (ambivalent).","traps":{"a":"Secure (type B) infants may cry at separation but are readily comforted on reunion and resume play. It tempts because the infant here also shows distress, but the inability to be soothed rules out security.","b":"Insecure-avoidant (type A) infants show little overt distress and ignore or avoid the mother on reunion. It tempts as the other 'insecure' label, but avoidance is the opposite of the clinging, angry contact seeking described.","d":"Disorganised (type D, Main and Solomon, 1986) shows contradictory, frozen or fearful behaviour with no coherent strategy. It tempts because 'oscillating' sounds contradictory, but the stem describes a coherent ambivalent strategy (clinging plus anger), which is the resistant pattern."}},
{"q":"Marcia's identity status characterised by commitment WITHOUT prior exploration is:","concept":"James E. Marcia (1966) operationalised Erik Erikson's adolescent crisis of identity versus role confusion by crossing two dimensions measured in a semi-structured Identity Status Interview: exploration (originally called crisis), meaning actively weighing alternatives in areas such as occupation, religion and political ideology, and commitment, meaning a firm investment in a chosen set of values and goals. The resulting 2 by 2 grid gives four identity statuses. Identity diffusion: no exploration and no commitment; the young person is drifting, apathetic or avoids the question. Identity foreclosure: commitment without exploration; values and goals are adopted wholesale from parents or other authorities, for example taking up the family business or religion without ever questioning it. Foreclosed individuals tend to be rigid, authoritarian, high in need for approval and low in anxiety. Identity moratorium: active exploration but no commitment yet; this is the status of 'crisis', anxious but open, and it is the status Erikson considered a healthy psychosocial moratorium. Identity achievement: exploration followed by commitment; the most mature status, associated with higher self-esteem, autonomy and capacity for intimacy. The statuses are not strict stages: a common route is diffusion or foreclosure to moratorium to achievement, but people can regress (the MAMA cycle: moratorium, achievement, moratorium, achievement). A memory hook: foreclosure is like a bank foreclosing early, the question is closed before it was ever opened. Examiners usually swap foreclosure and moratorium, so tie each to its pattern: foreclosure equals commitment yes, exploration no; moratorium equals exploration yes, commitment no. Option c is right because Marcia's foreclosure status is defined precisely as commitment that was made without any prior period of exploration.","traps":{"a":"Identity diffusion is no exploration and no commitment. It tempts because it also lacks exploration, but the stem specifies that commitment is present.","b":"Identity moratorium is active exploration without commitment, the reverse of the stem. It is the classic swap distractor with foreclosure.","d":"Identity achievement is commitment that follows exploration. It tempts because commitment is present, but it requires a prior period of exploration, which the stem rules out."}},
{"q":"Kohlberg's conventional level of moral reasoning is characterised by:","concept":"Lawrence Kohlberg (1958 dissertation; 1969, 1984) extended Jean Piaget's work on moral judgement by giving boys hypothetical dilemmas, most famously the Heinz dilemma (should Heinz steal a drug he cannot afford to save his dying wife?), and scoring the reasoning behind the answer rather than the answer itself. He described three levels, each with two stages, forming an invariant, universal sequence. Pre-conventional level (most children under about 9): Stage 1, punishment and obedience orientation, where right is whatever avoids punishment; Stage 2, instrumental purpose and exchange ('you scratch my back, I scratch yours'), where right is what serves one's own interests. Conventional level (most adolescents and many adults): Stage 3, interpersonal concordance or 'good boy, good girl' orientation, where right is what pleases others and earns approval; Stage 4, law and order or social system maintenance, where right is doing one's duty and upholding rules so society does not collapse. Post-conventional or principled level (a minority of adults): Stage 5, social contract and individual rights, where laws are seen as changeable agreements serving welfare; Stage 6, universal ethical principles such as justice and human dignity, followed even against the law. The defining feature of the conventional level is that morality is defined by the expectations of family, group and society, so the person conforms to and maintains social rules. Key critiques: Carol Gilligan (1982) argued the scheme is male centred and neglects an ethic of care; others note cultural bias and a gap between moral reasoning and moral behaviour. Option c is right because conformity to social rules and maintaining social order, through the good boy, good girl and law and order stages, is exactly what defines Kohlberg's conventional level.","traps":{"a":"Obedience to avoid punishment is Stage 1, the first stage of the pre-conventional level. It tempts because obedience to rules sounds conventional, but here the motive is avoiding punishment, not upholding social order.","b":"Seeking concrete rewards in exchange for good behaviour is Stage 2, instrumental exchange, still pre-conventional. It tempts as a step beyond simple obedience, but it is self-interested, not oriented to group norms.","d":"Reasoning from universal ethical principles is Stage 6, the top of the post-conventional level. It tempts as the 'highest' answer, but it goes beyond, and may even override, conventional social rules."}},
{"q":"In Bronfenbrenner's ecological systems theory, the system that includes interactions between two or more of the child's microsystems (e.g., home-school link) is called the:","concept":"Urie Bronfenbrenner's ecological systems theory ('The Ecology of Human Development', 1979) pictures the developing child at the centre of nested environmental systems, like Russian dolls. The microsystem is the innermost layer: settings where the child directly participates face to face, such as family, school, peer group, neighbourhood and religious group, with bidirectional influences (the child also shapes the parents). The mesosystem is the set of interrelations between two or more microsystems in which the child actively participates, for example parent and teacher communication, the link between home and school, or how family rules fit with peer group norms. A strong mesosystem, such as parents involved with the school, supports development; conflicting microsystems strain it. The exosystem consists of settings the child is not part of that nevertheless affect the child indirectly, such as a parent's workplace (long hours, job loss), the school board or local health services. The macrosystem is the overarching culture: values, laws, customs, ideologies and economic system that shape all inner layers. The chronosystem, added later (1986), adds the dimension of time: life transitions like divorce and historical events. Bronfenbrenner later reframed the model as the bioecological model (Bronfenbrenner and Morris, 1998, 2006), with Process, Person, Context and Time, and proximal processes as the engine of development. To tell the layers apart, ask: is the child physically in it (micro), is it a link between places the child is in (meso), is it a place the child is not in but which affects the child (exo), or is it the cultural blueprint (macro)? Option b is right because the mesosystem is precisely the system made of connections between two or more of the child's microsystems, such as the home and school link.","traps":{"a":"The microsystem is each single immediate setting (family, school, peers) where the child interacts directly. It tempts because home and school are both microsystems, but the stem asks about the interaction between them, which is the next layer.","c":"The exosystem is a setting the child does not participate in but which affects the child indirectly, such as a parent's workplace. It tempts because it also involves linkages, but the home and school link involves two settings the child does enter.","d":"The macrosystem is the broad cultural context of values, laws and customs. It tempts as the 'big picture' layer, but it is not about connections between specific settings."}},
{"q":"Thomas and Chess identified which three primary temperament categories?","concept":"Temperament refers to early appearing, biologically based individual differences in emotional reactivity and self regulation. The landmark study is the New York Longitudinal Study (NYLS), begun in 1956 by psychiatrists Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess (with Herbert Birch), following 133 children from infancy into adulthood (Thomas, Chess and Birch, 1968, 1970; Thomas and Chess, 1977). From parent interviews they rated nine dimensions: activity level, rhythmicity (regularity of biological functions), approach or withdrawal to new stimuli, adaptability, threshold of responsiveness, intensity of reaction, quality of mood, distractibility, and attention span and persistence. These clustered into three types. Easy children (about 40 percent): regular rhythms, approach novelty, adapt quickly, positive mood of mild to moderate intensity. Difficult children (about 10 percent): irregular routines, withdraw from novelty, slow to adapt, intense and often negative reactions; they were at higher risk of later behaviour problems. Slow to warm up children (about 15 percent): low activity, mild negative reactions to novelty and slow adaptation, but they warm up with repeated exposure. The remaining about 35 percent did not fit any category. Their key clinical concept is goodness of fit: outcomes depend on the match between the child's temperament and the demands and expectations of the environment, not on temperament alone; a difficult infant with patient, consistent parents can do well. Neighbouring models: Arnold Buss and Robert Plomin (EAS: emotionality, activity, sociability) and Mary Rothbart (surgency or extraversion, negative affectivity, effortful control), and Jerome Kagan's behavioural inhibition. Option b is right because easy, difficult and slow to warm up are the three temperament categories derived by Thomas and Chess from the NYLS.","traps":{"a":"Sanguine, choleric and melancholic (with phlegmatic) are the ancient humoral temperaments of Hippocrates and Galen. They tempt because they are the oldest 'temperament types', but they are not Thomas and Chess's empirical categories.","c":"Type A and Type B are behaviour patterns linked to coronary heart disease by Meyer Friedman and Ray Rosenman (1959), with Type C later proposed as a cancer-prone style by Lydia Temoshok. They are adult personality and health constructs, not infant temperament.","d":"Sensitive, resilient and withdrawn are plausible-sounding descriptors with no standing as a classic typology. They tempt because 'withdrawn' echoes the approach or withdrawal dimension and 'resilient' echoes Jack Block's resilient type, but they are not the NYLS categories."}},
{"q":"Harry Harlow's experiments with rhesus monkeys and surrogate mothers (wire vs cloth) primarily demonstrated:","concept":"Harry F. Harlow's surrogate mother studies at the University of Wisconsin (Harlow, 1958, 'The Nature of Love'; Harlow and Zimmermann, 1959) tested what makes an infant attach to its mother. At the time both psychoanalysis and drive reduction learning theory (Clark Hull, John Dollard and Neal Miller) held a 'cupboard love' or secondary drive view: the mother becomes attractive because she is associated with feeding, which reduces the primary drive of hunger. Harlow separated infant rhesus monkeys from their mothers at birth and raised them with two surrogates: a bare wire mesh figure and a figure covered in soft terry cloth. In the key condition only the wire mother had a feeding bottle. The infants went to the wire mother just to feed, then spent most of the day (often 17 to 18 hours) clinging to the cloth mother. When frightened by a novel object, such as a drumming mechanical bear, they ran to the cloth mother and, once comforted, used her as a secure base to explore. Harlow concluded that contact comfort, not feeding, is the primary basis of affectional ties. Later work showed the limits of surrogates: monkeys reared in isolation or only with surrogates became socially abnormal, rocked and self clasped, could not mate normally and were often neglectful or abusive mothers, and peer contact partly protected them. These findings strongly influenced John Bowlby's ethological attachment theory and fuelled ethical debate about animal research. Do not confuse Harlow with Konrad Lorenz's imprinting in geese (1935), which is a rapid following response during a critical period. Option b is right because Harlow's wire versus cloth experiments showed that contact comfort outweighs feeding in forming attachment, refuting the secondary drive account.","traps":{"a":"Imprinting is Konrad Lorenz's (1935) finding that goslings follow the first moving object during a critical period. It tempts because both are classic animal attachment studies, but Harlow's design contrasted comfort with food, not following behaviour.","c":"Operant shaping of behaviour is B. F. Skinner's method of reinforcing successive approximations. It tempts because Harlow was working in a learning-theory era, but his result actually undercut a reinforcement (feeding) account of attachment.","d":"Critical periods in language acquisition relate to Eric Lenneberg's (1967) hypothesis and cases like Genie. It tempts because Harlow's isolation work also implies sensitive periods for social development, but the surrogate studies concerned attachment, not language."}},
{"q":"A new screening test for depression has a sensitivity of 90% and specificity of 70%. This means the test correctly identifies:","concept":"Sensitivity and specificity describe how well a test classifies people whose true status is already known from a gold standard. Lay out a 2 by 2 table: true positives (TP, ill and test positive), false negatives (FN, ill but test negative), false positives (FP, healthy but test positive) and true negatives (TN, healthy and test negative). Sensitivity = TP divided by (TP plus FN): the proportion of truly ill people the test correctly calls positive, the true positive rate. Specificity = TN divided by (TN plus FP): the proportion of truly healthy people the test correctly calls negative, the true negative rate. So sensitivity 90 percent means the test catches 90 of every 100 depressed people (missing 10, the false negative rate), and specificity 70 percent means it correctly clears 70 of every 100 healthy people (wrongly flagging 30, the false positive rate). Both are properties of the test itself and do not change with prevalence. Mnemonics: SnNout (a highly Sensitive test, when Negative, rules the condition out) and SpPin (a highly Specific test, when Positive, rules it in). Screening tools favour high sensitivity so few cases are missed; confirmatory tests favour high specificity. The reverse questions, 'given a positive result, how likely is the person ill?' and 'given a negative result, how likely is the person healthy?', are answered by positive predictive value, TP divided by (TP plus FP), and negative predictive value, TN divided by (TN plus FN). These depend heavily on prevalence: in a low-prevalence population even a good test has a low PPV. Likelihood ratios combine sensitivity and specificity: LR positive = sensitivity divided by (1 minus specificity). Option b is right because a sensitivity of 90 percent and specificity of 70 percent mean the test correctly identifies 90 percent of depressed people as depressed and 70 percent of healthy people as healthy.","traps":{"a":"This reverses the two terms: it assigns 90 percent to the healthy and 70 percent to the depressed. It tempts students who mix up which measure refers to the ill group; sensitivity always refers to the people who have the condition.","c":"The proportion of positive results that are true positives is the positive predictive value, not sensitivity. It tempts because it also sounds like 'correct positives', but PPV conditions on the test result and varies with prevalence.","d":"The proportion of negative results that are true negatives is the negative predictive value, not specificity. It tempts for the same reason as the PPV option, but NPV conditions on the test result rather than on true health status."}},
{"q":"Cohen's d is a measure of:","concept":"An effect size expresses how large an effect is, independently of sample size, whereas a p value only says how surprising the data would be if the null hypothesis were true. Jacob Cohen ('Statistical Power Analysis for the Behavioral Sciences', 1969, 2nd edition 1988) proposed d as the standardised mean difference: d = (mean of group 1 minus mean of group 2) divided by the pooled standard deviation. Because it is in standard deviation units, it can be compared across studies using different scales, which makes it a building block of meta-analysis. Cohen's rough benchmarks for the behavioural sciences are 0.2 small, 0.5 medium and 0.8 large; for example, d = 0.5 means the group means are half a standard deviation apart, and about 69 percent of the treated group would score above the control mean. Close relatives: Hedges' g (Larry Hedges, 1981) applies a small-sample bias correction; Glass's delta (Gene Glass, 1976) divides by the control group's SD only. For other designs there are other effect sizes: r and r squared for correlations, eta squared and partial eta squared for ANOVA, Cohen's f, odds ratios and risk ratios for categorical outcomes, and Cohen's kappa for agreement (a different Cohen statistic). Effect size is also one of the four linked quantities in power analysis (effect size, alpha, power, sample size): fixing three determines the fourth, which is how sample size is planned. A large sample can make a trivial d 'significant', and a small sample can miss a large d, which is why reporting guidelines (APA) ask for effect sizes and confidence intervals alongside p. Option c is right because Cohen's d is the standardised effect size for the difference between two means, the mean difference divided by the pooled SD.","traps":{"a":"Statistical significance is judged by the p value against alpha. It tempts because d is reported with significance tests, but d measures magnitude, and a significant result can still have a tiny d.","b":"Sample size adequacy is the domain of power analysis. It tempts because d is an input to power calculations, but d itself measures effect magnitude, not whether n is adequate.","d":"Correlation strength between two variables is indexed by Pearson's r (or r squared). It tempts because r is also an effect size and can be converted to d, but d specifically compares two group means."}},
{"q":"Which statistical test would you use to compare the means of THREE OR MORE independent groups assuming normality and homogeneity of variance?","concept":"Analysis of variance (ANOVA) was developed by Ronald A. Fisher in the 1920s ('Statistical Methods for Research Workers', 1925). One-way ANOVA tests the null hypothesis that three or more independent group means are equal on one continuous dependent variable, with one categorical independent variable (factor). Its logic is partitioning variance: total sum of squares splits into between-groups variation (differences among group means) and within-groups or error variation (spread inside each group). Each is divided by its degrees of freedom to give mean squares, and F = MS between divided by MS within. If the null is true, F is near 1; a large F means group differences exceed what chance variation predicts. Assumptions are independence of observations, approximately normal distributions within groups, and homogeneity of variance (checked with Levene's test). A significant F is an omnibus result: it says at least one mean differs but not which, so post hoc tests (Tukey HSD, Scheffe, Bonferroni) or planned contrasts follow. Why not run many t tests? Each test at alpha .05 inflates the familywise Type I error: with three groups and three pairwise tests the chance of at least one false positive rises to about 14 percent. Neighbours to keep straight: two groups use the independent t test (with two groups F = t squared); repeated measures on the same people use repeated-measures ANOVA; two or more factors use factorial ANOVA; adding a covariate gives ANCOVA; if normality fails with three or more independent groups, use the Kruskal-Wallis H test; for categorical outcomes, chi-square. Effect size is reported as eta squared. Option b is right because one-way ANOVA is the parametric test designed to compare the means of three or more independent groups under the assumptions of normality and equal variances.","traps":{"a":"The independent samples t test compares only two independent group means. It tempts because it is the parametric test for independent groups, but using it repeatedly across three or more groups inflates Type I error.","c":"The chi-square test analyses frequencies of categorical variables, not means. It tempts as a common multi-group test, but it applies to counts in a contingency table, not continuous outcomes.","d":"The Mann-Whitney U test is a non-parametric test for two independent groups based on ranks. It tempts because it handles independent groups, but it is for two groups only and is used when normality is not assumed; the three-plus group analogue is Kruskal-Wallis."}},
{"q":"Cronbach's alpha is a measure of:","concept":"Reliability is the consistency of a measure, and different reliability coefficients target different sources of error. Internal consistency asks whether the items of one scale, given once, hang together as measures of the same construct. The classic index is coefficient alpha, introduced by Lee J. Cronbach (1951), which generalises the Kuder-Richardson formula 20 (Kuder and Richardson, 1937) used for dichotomous (right or wrong) items. Conceptually alpha reflects the average inter-item correlation and the number of items: alpha = (k divided by (k minus 1)) multiplied by (1 minus the sum of item variances divided by total score variance). Common rules of thumb: 0.70 or above acceptable for research, 0.80 good, 0.90 or above for high-stakes clinical decisions; a very high alpha (above about 0.95) may mean redundant items. Important caveats: alpha rises with the number of items, assumes items are essentially tau equivalent, and does not prove unidimensionality; McDonald's omega is a modern alternative. Split-half reliability is an older internal consistency method, correlating two halves and correcting with the Spearman-Brown prophecy formula. Other types of reliability: test-retest (stability over time, Pearson r between two administrations), parallel or alternate forms (equivalence of two versions), and inter-rater reliability (agreement between observers, measured by Cohen's kappa for two raters, Fleiss' kappa for many raters, or the intraclass correlation coefficient for continuous ratings). Reliability is necessary but not sufficient for validity: a scale can be consistent yet measure the wrong thing. Option b is right because Cronbach's alpha estimates the internal consistency of a multi-item scale, that is, how well its items measure a single underlying construct.","traps":{"a":"Inter-rater reliability is agreement between different raters, usually measured with Cohen's kappa or the ICC. It tempts because both are reliability indices, but alpha concerns items within a scale, not raters.","c":"Test-retest reliability is the stability of scores over time, a correlation between two administrations. It tempts as the most familiar reliability type, but alpha comes from a single administration.","d":"Criterion validity is how well scores relate to an external criterion, such as a gold-standard measure. It tempts because it is also a psychometric coefficient, but it concerns validity, not reliability."}},
{"q":"If a 20-item questionnaire and an established gold-standard depression scale correlate at r = 0.85, this provides evidence of:","concept":"Validity is the degree to which evidence supports the intended interpretation of test scores (Standards for Educational and Psychological Testing, AERA, APA and NCME, 2014). The traditional tripartite division is content, criterion-related and construct validity. Content validity asks whether items adequately sample the domain, judged by experts. Face validity is a weaker cousin: whether the test looks, to lay test takers, like it measures what it claims; it is not statistical evidence. Criterion-related validity is the correlation between test scores and an external criterion, reported as a validity coefficient. It has two forms distinguished by timing: concurrent validity, where test and criterion are measured at about the same time (for example a new depression questionnaire against the Hamilton Rating Scale or a structured clinical diagnosis), and predictive validity, where the criterion is measured later (for example entrance test scores predicting first-year grades). Construct validity, as framed by Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955), asks whether the test measures the theoretical construct; evidence includes factor structure and the multitrait-multimethod approach of Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske (1959), with convergent validity (high correlation with measures of the same construct) and discriminant validity (low correlation with different constructs). Note an overlap examiners exploit: correlating with another measure of the same construct can also be described as convergent validity; but when the comparison measure is an established gold standard given at the same time, the standard textbook label is concurrent, criterion-related validity. A correlation of 0.85 is very high for a validity coefficient. Option c is right because a strong correlation between the new questionnaire and an established gold-standard scale, measured at the same time, is evidence of concurrent (criterion-related) validity.","traps":{"a":"Internal consistency is reliability among items within one scale, measured with Cronbach's alpha. It tempts because it is also an r-type statistic, but it involves no external measure.","b":"Test-retest reliability correlates the same test with itself across time. It tempts because it is also a correlation, but here two different instruments are correlated, which is validity evidence.","d":"Face validity is whether a test superficially appears to measure its target, judged without statistics. It tempts as a familiar validity term, but a correlation coefficient with a criterion is empirical, not face, evidence."}},
{"q":"Repeated-measures ANOVA differs from one-way ANOVA in that it:","concept":"Repeated-measures (within-subjects) ANOVA is used when the same participants are measured under three or more conditions or time points, for example depression scores at baseline, mid-treatment and post-treatment. Scores from the same person are correlated, which violates the independence assumption of ordinary one-way (between-subjects) ANOVA. The repeated-measures model handles this by estimating and removing the variance due to stable individual differences between subjects. Total variation is split into between-subjects variance and within-subjects variance; the within part is further divided into the effect of the condition (time or treatment) and residual error. Because individual differences are taken out of the error term, the F test, F = MS conditions divided by MS error, is usually more powerful, so fewer participants are needed. Its distinctive assumption is sphericity: the variances of the differences between all pairs of conditions should be equal. It is checked with Mauchly's test, and when violated the degrees of freedom are corrected with the Greenhouse-Geisser (1959) epsilon or the less conservative Huynh-Feldt correction; a multivariate approach (MANOVA) or linear mixed models are alternatives. Drawbacks include carryover, practice and fatigue effects, addressed by counterbalancing. It is still a parametric test, assuming approximately normal residuals; the non-parametric equivalent is the Friedman test. With only two related conditions, the paired samples t test is used. Mixed (split-plot) ANOVA combines a between factor (group) with a within factor (time), the standard design for a randomised trial with repeated assessments. Option b is right because the defining feature of repeated-measures ANOVA is that it accounts for the correlation among repeated assessments on the same participants, which one-way ANOVA ignores.","traps":{"a":"Repeated-measures ANOVA is still parametric and assumes approximately normal residuals. It tempts because it adds a different assumption (sphericity), but it does not drop normality.","c":"It compares two or more conditions and is typically used for three or more; for exactly two related conditions the paired t test is the usual choice. It tempts by confusion with the paired t test.","d":"It is parametric; the non-parametric analogue for repeated measures is the Friedman test. It tempts because some students equate 'related samples' with non-parametric methods."}},
{"q":"A Cohen's kappa of 0.85 between two clinicians rating the same set of patients indicates:","concept":"Cohen's kappa, introduced by Jacob Cohen (1960), measures agreement between two raters assigning cases to categories, corrected for the agreement expected by chance. Raw percentage agreement is misleading because two raters guessing according to base rates will agree some of the time anyway. Kappa = (observed agreement minus chance agreement) divided by (1 minus chance agreement), where chance agreement is computed from each rater's marginal proportions. Kappa is 1 for perfect agreement, 0 for agreement at chance level, and negative for less than chance agreement. The most widely cited interpretation benchmarks come from J. Richard Landis and Gary G. Koch (1977): below 0 poor, 0.00 to 0.20 slight, 0.21 to 0.40 fair, 0.41 to 0.60 moderate, 0.61 to 0.80 substantial, and 0.81 to 1.00 almost perfect. Joseph Fleiss (1981) proposed a simpler scheme: below 0.40 poor, 0.40 to 0.75 fair to good, above 0.75 excellent; on either scheme 0.85 is at the top. Extensions: weighted kappa (Cohen, 1968) gives partial credit for near misses on ordinal scales; Fleiss' kappa handles more than two raters; the intraclass correlation coefficient is used for continuous ratings. Known limitation, the kappa paradox: when prevalence is very high or very low, kappa can be low even when raw agreement is high (Feinstein and Cicchetti, 1990). In psychiatry, kappa is the standard statistic for diagnostic reliability, for example in the DSM-5 field trials and in evaluating structured interviews like the SCID. Option d is right because a kappa of 0.85 falls in the 0.81 to 1.00 band that Landis and Koch label almost perfect agreement.","traps":{"a":"Poor agreement in Landis and Koch's scheme is kappa below 0, worse than chance. It tempts only if one misreads kappa as a disagreement index.","b":"Fair agreement is kappa 0.21 to 0.40. It tempts because in Fleiss' alternative scheme 'fair to good' spans 0.40 to 0.75, but 0.85 lies well above both ranges.","c":"Moderate agreement is kappa 0.41 to 0.60. It tempts students who assume kappa runs lower than a correlation, but 0.85 is in the top band."}},
{"q":"A chi-square test of independence is used to:","concept":"Karl Pearson (1900) introduced the chi-square statistic to compare observed frequencies with the frequencies expected under a hypothesis. The chi-square test of independence (also called test of association) is used when you have two categorical variables measured on the same sample, for example gender (male, female) and diagnosis (depressed, not depressed), arranged in an r by c contingency table of counts. The null hypothesis is that the variables are independent, meaning the distribution of one does not depend on the level of the other. For each cell, the expected frequency = (row total multiplied by column total) divided by grand total. The statistic is the sum over all cells of (observed minus expected) squared divided by expected, with degrees of freedom (rows minus 1) multiplied by (columns minus 1). A large value means the observed pattern departs from independence more than chance would allow. Assumptions: data are frequencies (not percentages or means), observations are independent with each person in only one cell, and expected counts are adequate (commonly at least 5 in most cells); with small expected counts in a 2 by 2 table use Fisher's exact test, and Yates' continuity correction is sometimes applied. For paired categorical data (before and after on the same people) use McNemar's test. Strength of association is reported with phi (2 by 2) or Cramer's V. Contrast the chi-square goodness of fit test, which compares one categorical variable to a theoretical distribution. Chi-square is non-parametric in the sense that it makes no assumptions about population distributions of a continuous variable. Option b is right because the chi-square test of independence tests whether two categorical variables are associated, by comparing observed with expected cell frequencies.","traps":{"a":"Comparing the means of two groups is done with the t test (independent samples). It tempts because chi-square is also a group comparison, but chi-square analyses counts, not means.","c":"Paired observations on a continuous variable call for the paired t test or Wilcoxon signed-rank test; paired categorical data use McNemar's test. It tempts because McNemar's is a chi-square type test, but the test of independence assumes independent observations.","d":"Correlation between two continuous variables is measured by Pearson's r (or Spearman's rho for ranks). It tempts because chi-square also assesses association, but only between categorical variables."}},
{"q":"If a trial shows the Number Needed to Treat (NNT) for a new antidepressant is 8, this means:","concept":"The number needed to treat (NNT) was introduced by Andreas Laupacis, David Sackett and Robin Roberts (1988) as a clinically intuitive way of expressing a treatment effect from a trial with a binary outcome (for example response or remission). Start with the event rates: control event rate (CER) is the proportion of control patients who respond, and experimental event rate (EER) is the proportion in the treated group. The absolute risk reduction or, for a good outcome, absolute benefit increase is the difference between them. NNT = 1 divided by that absolute difference, rounded up to a whole number. Example: if 50 percent respond to the drug and 37.5 percent to placebo, the difference is 12.5 percent, and 1 divided by 0.125 = 8. So on average 8 patients must be treated with the new drug rather than the control for one additional patient to benefit; the other seven would have responded anyway or would not respond at all. Lower NNT means a more effective treatment; an NNT of 1 would mean every treated patient benefits who would not have otherwise. Antidepressant versus placebo NNTs in meta-analyses are commonly in the range of about 6 to 9. Related measures: number needed to harm (NNH) = 1 divided by the absolute risk increase of an adverse effect; comparing NNT with NNH expresses the benefit to risk balance. Relative risk reduction can look impressive even when absolute benefit is tiny, which is why evidence-based medicine (Sackett and colleagues) emphasises absolute measures like NNT. An NNT always refers to a specific comparator, outcome and time frame. Option b is right because an NNT of 8 means that, on average, 8 patients must be treated with the new drug instead of the control for one additional patient to benefit.","traps":{"a":"Dropout counts are about attrition and stopping rules, unrelated to NNT. It tempts because 'number' and 'patients' appear, but NNT is derived from the difference in outcome rates between arms.","c":"Saying the drug works in 8 percent of patients misreads NNT as a percentage. An NNT of 8 corresponds to an absolute benefit of 1 divided by 8, about 12.5 percent, over the control.","d":"There is no NNT threshold for FDA approval; regulators judge efficacy and safety across adequate, well-controlled trials. It tempts by sounding like a regulatory rule of thumb."}},
{"q":"The standard error of the mean is BEST described as:","concept":"The standard error of the mean (SEM) answers the question 'how much would my sample mean bounce around if I repeated the study many times?' Imagine drawing every possible random sample of size n from a population and computing each sample's mean: the resulting distribution is the sampling distribution of the mean. Its standard deviation is the standard error. By the central limit theorem, that distribution is approximately normal for reasonably large n (often n of 30 or more), centred on the population mean mu, with standard deviation sigma divided by the square root of n; in practice it is estimated as s divided by the square root of n, using the sample SD. Two consequences follow. First, the SEM shrinks as sample size grows, but only with the square root: quadrupling n halves the SEM. Second, the SEM is the building block of inference: a 95 percent confidence interval is roughly the mean plus or minus 1.96 SEM, and the t statistic is the mean difference divided by its standard error. Keep it distinct from the standard deviation: the SD describes how spread out individual scores are and does not shrink with larger samples, whereas the SEM describes the precision of the mean as an estimate. A common reporting error is showing mean plus or minus SEM in place of SD, which makes data look less variable. Do not confuse the SEM with the standard error of measurement in classical test theory (SD multiplied by the square root of 1 minus reliability), which concerns the precision of an individual's test score. Sampling error is the actual difference between a sample statistic and the parameter. Option b is right because the standard error of the mean is, by definition, the standard deviation of the sampling distribution of the mean.","traps":{"a":"The SD of the sample describes the spread of individual scores. It tempts because the SEM is calculated from it, but SEM equals SD divided by the square root of n and reflects precision of the mean, not spread of data.","c":"The difference between a particular sample mean and the population mean is the sampling error for that sample. It tempts because the SEM describes the typical size of such errors, but it is their standard deviation across samples, not one difference.","d":"Total variability in the population is the population variance or SD (sigma). It tempts because it is a 'standard' measure of spread, but the SEM refers to means of samples, not individual population scores."}},
{"q":"A Receiver Operating Characteristic (ROC) curve plots:","concept":"A receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve comes from signal detection theory, developed for radar operators in World War II and brought into psychology by Wilson Tanner and John Swets (1954) and David Green and John Swets (1966), and into medicine for evaluating diagnostic tests. Any continuous screening score, such as a depression questionnaire total, needs a cut-off to classify people as positive or negative. Moving the cut-off trades sensitivity against specificity: a lower cut-off catches more cases (higher sensitivity) but also more false positives (lower specificity). The ROC curve plots this trade-off at every possible cut-off: sensitivity, the true positive rate, on the y axis, against 1 minus specificity, the false positive rate, on the x axis. A useless test lies on the diagonal line from (0,0) to (1,1); a perfect test hugs the top left corner. The area under the curve (AUC) summarises overall discrimination: 0.5 is chance, 1.0 perfect, around 0.7 to 0.8 acceptable, 0.8 to 0.9 excellent, above 0.9 outstanding (Hosmer and Lemeshow's guide). AUC equals the probability that a randomly chosen case scores higher than a randomly chosen non-case. The optimal cut-off is often chosen with the Youden index (J = sensitivity plus specificity minus 1, William Youden, 1950), or by weighting the relative costs of misses and false alarms. In signal detection terms, d prime reflects sensitivity of the observer and the criterion (beta) reflects response bias, which corresponds to the position along the curve. Option a is right because an ROC curve plots sensitivity on the y axis against 1 minus specificity on the x axis across all possible cut-off scores.","traps":{"b":"Type I versus Type II error is the trade-off between alpha and beta in hypothesis testing, often shown in power analysis. It tempts because false positives and misses are analogous, but an ROC curve plots sensitivity and the false positive rate of a test across cut-offs.","c":"Predictor values against outcome values describes a scatterplot or regression line. It tempts because ROC analysis evaluates predictors, but its axes are classification rates, not raw values.","d":"Means against standard deviations is not a standard ROC plot (it resembles a mean-variance plot). It tempts only through vague familiarity with summary statistics."}},
{"q":"In a randomised controlled trial, allocation concealment refers to:","concept":"Randomisation protects a trial from selection bias only if nobody can predict or subvert the next assignment. Allocation concealment is the set of procedures that keeps the upcoming allocation sequence hidden from those who enrol participants until each participant has been irreversibly entered into the trial. Without it, a recruiter who knows the next envelope says 'active drug' might steer a healthier patient into that slot, or delay enrolling a sicker one, recreating selection bias even though the sequence itself was random. Kenneth Schulz and colleagues (1995, JAMA) showed that trials with inadequate or unclear concealment exaggerated treatment effects by about 30 to 40 percent on average. Adequate methods: central randomisation by telephone or web, pharmacy-controlled allocation, or sequentially numbered, opaque, sealed envelopes (SNOSE); inadequate methods: alternation, date of birth, case record number, or open lists. Concealment is distinct from blinding (masking). Concealment operates before and at the moment of allocation, protects against selection bias, and is always possible. Blinding operates after allocation, keeps participants, clinicians, outcome assessors and sometimes analysts unaware of which arm each person is in, protects against performance and detection (ascertainment) bias, and is not always possible, for example in psychotherapy trials. Both appear as separate items in the Cochrane Risk of Bias tool and the CONSORT statement (most recent update 2025; earlier 2010). Related ideas: sequence generation (how the random list is made, such as block or stratified randomisation) and intention-to-treat analysis. Option b is right because allocation concealment means hiding the upcoming allocation from the recruiter until after the participant is enrolled, which prevents selection bias at the point of entry.","traps":{"a":"Keeping participants unaware of their assigned treatment after randomisation is blinding, which guards against performance and detection bias. It is the classic confusion with concealment, which happens before and at allocation.","c":"Hiding group labels from the statistician is analyst blinding, a form of masking after allocation. It tempts because it also involves concealing information, but it concerns analysis bias, not enrolment.","d":"Keeping investigators unaware of the hypothesis is not a standard trial safeguard (and investigators usually design the hypothesis). It tempts by resembling demand characteristic controls, but it is not allocation concealment."}},
{"q":"The WAIS-IV yields how many primary composite index scores in addition to the Full-Scale IQ?","concept":"The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale, Fourth Edition (WAIS-IV; David Wechsler's scale, published by Pearson in 2008) for ages 16:0 to 90:11 dropped the traditional Verbal IQ and Performance IQ split that dated back to the Wechsler-Bellevue (1939). Instead it reports a Full Scale IQ (FSIQ) plus four primary index scores, reflecting factor analytic and Cattell-Horn-Carroll informed research. Verbal Comprehension Index (VCI): core subtests Similarities, Vocabulary, Information (supplemental Comprehension); measures crystallised verbal knowledge and reasoning. Perceptual Reasoning Index (PRI): Block Design, Matrix Reasoning, Visual Puzzles (supplemental Picture Completion, Figure Weights); fluid and visual spatial reasoning. Working Memory Index (WMI): Digit Span, Arithmetic (supplemental Letter-Number Sequencing); holding and manipulating information. Processing Speed Index (PSI): Symbol Search, Coding (supplemental Cancellation). The ten core subtests produce the FSIQ; index scores, like FSIQ, have a mean of 100 and SD of 15, while subtest scaled scores have mean 10 and SD 3. Optional ancillary composites are the General Ability Index (GAI, from VCI plus PRI, less affected by working memory and speed weaknesses) and the Cognitive Proficiency Index (CPI, from WMI plus PSI). Compare the WISC-V (2014), which expanded to five primary indices by splitting perceptual reasoning into Visual Spatial and Fluid Reasoning, and the WAIS-5 (2024), which also adopts five primary indices. For exam purposes, WAIS-IV equals four indices. Option c is right because the WAIS-IV yields four primary index scores, VCI, PRI, WMI and PSI, in addition to the Full Scale IQ.","traps":{"a":"Verbal IQ and Performance IQ were the summary scores of earlier editions up to the WAIS-III; the WAIS-IV removed them. It tempts because the VIQ and PIQ split is the best known Wechsler structure.","b":"Three indices omits Processing Speed. It tempts because VCI, PRI and WMI are the more 'reasoning' composites, but PSI is a full primary index contributing to FSIQ.","d":"The GAI is an optional ancillary composite derived from VCI and PRI, not a fifth primary index. It tempts because five-index structures exist in the WISC-V and WAIS-5, and because the GAI is reported in WAIS-IV score summaries."}},
{"q":"The age range for the WISC-V is:","concept":"The Wechsler family of intelligence scales, originated by David Wechsler (first scale, the Wechsler-Bellevue, 1939), is divided by age, and the age bands, written in years:months, are frequent exam items. The Wechsler Preschool and Primary Scale of Intelligence, Fourth Edition (WPPSI-IV, 2012) covers 2:6 to 7:7, with separate batteries for 2:6 to 3:11 and 4:0 to 7:7. The Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children, Fifth Edition (WISC-V, 2014) covers 6:0 to 16:11. The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale, Fourth Edition (WAIS-IV, 2008) covers 16:0 to 90:11, and the WAIS-5 (2024) keeps a similar adult range. The overlaps are deliberate: at 6:0 to 7:7 a clinician can choose WPPSI-IV or WISC-V (for example WPPSI for a child with suspected low ability, to avoid floor effects), and at age 16 the choice is WISC-V or WAIS-IV (WISC for low ability, WAIS for higher ability, to avoid ceiling effects). The WISC-V yields a Full Scale IQ (from seven subtests) and five primary indices: Verbal Comprehension, Visual Spatial, Fluid Reasoning, Working Memory and Processing Speed, plus ancillary indices such as Quantitative Reasoning, Auditory Working Memory, Nonverbal, General Ability and Cognitive Proficiency. For infants and toddlers the standard tool is not a Wechsler scale but the Bayley Scales of Infant and Toddler Development (Nancy Bayley; Bayley-III, 2006, and Bayley-4, 2019), covering 1 to 42 months. In India, adaptations such as Malin's Intelligence Scale for Indian Children (MISIC, an adaptation of the original WISC) are often used. Option b is right because the WISC-V is standardised for children aged 6 years 0 months to 16 years 11 months.","traps":{"a":"2:6 to 7:7 is the age range of the WPPSI-IV, the Wechsler preschool scale. It tempts because it overlaps the youngest WISC-V ages.","c":"16:0 to 90:11 is the age range of the WAIS-IV, the adult Wechsler scale. It tempts because it shares age 16 with the WISC-V upper limit.","d":"0 to 42 months corresponds roughly to the Bayley Scales of Infant and Toddler Development (1 to 42 months), not a Wechsler test. It tempts as another commonly memorised developmental age range."}},
{"q":"Which of the following are the THREE main validity scales on the MMPI-2?","concept":"The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory was developed by Starke R. Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943) using empirical criterion keying: items were retained if they statistically separated psychiatric groups from normal controls, regardless of content. Because self-report can be distorted, the original MMPI carried validity scales alongside the ten clinical scales (Hs, D, Hy, Pd, Mf, Pa, Pt, Sc, Ma, Si). The classic three validity scales are L, F and K, often taught as the validity triad. The L (Lie) scale contains items describing minor flaws almost everyone admits (for example occasionally getting angry); high scores suggest naive, unsophisticated attempts to present oneself in an overly virtuous light. The F (Infrequency) scale contains items rarely endorsed by normal people; high scores suggest random responding, severe psychopathology, a cry for help, or faking bad (malingering). The K (Correction or Defensiveness) scale, added by Paul Meehl and Starke Hathaway (1946), detects more subtle defensiveness and is used to K-correct five clinical scales (Hs, Pd, Pt, Sc, Ma). Profile shapes help: an inverted V (L and K high, F low) indicates defensiveness; a V shape (F high, L and K low) suggests exaggeration of symptoms. The MMPI-2 (James Butcher and colleagues, 1989) kept L, F and K and added Cannot Say (?), VRIN (variable response inconsistency), TRIN (true response inconsistency), F(B) back infrequency, F(p) infrequency psychopathology, FBS (symptom validity) and S (superlative self-presentation). The MMPI-2-RF (2008) and MMPI-3 (2020) use revised forms such as L-r, F-r and K-r. So VRIN, TRIN and S are genuine MMPI-2 validity scales, but they are additions rather than the main classic three. Option a is right because L, F and K are the three original and main validity scales retained on the MMPI-2.","traps":{"b":"Hs (Hypochondriasis) and D (Depression) are clinical scales 1 and 2, not validity scales, paired here with the genuine L scale. It tempts because it starts with L, but only one of the three is a validity scale.","c":"Pa (Paranoia) and Sc (Schizophrenia) are clinical scales 6 and 8; only F is a validity scale. It tempts by mixing one real validity scale with clinical ones.","d":"VRIN, TRIN and S are real validity scales, but they were added in the MMPI-2 to supplement the original triad. It tempts precisely because they are genuine validity scales; however the question asks for the three main ones, which are L, F and K."}},
{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a recognised Rorschach scoring system?","concept":"The Rorschach Inkblot Test was published by Swiss psychiatrist Hermann Rorschach in 'Psychodiagnostik' (1921): ten symmetrical inkblots (five achromatic, two black and red, three multicoloured) to which the person says what each might be. Rorschach died in 1922, and his scoring ideas were developed in the United States into several competing systems from the 1930s to 1960s. The five major American systems were those of Samuel J. Beck (from 1937), Bruno Klopfer (from 1942, with Kelley), Marguerite Hertz, Zygmunt Piotrowski, and David Rapaport with Roy Schafer (1946). John E. Exner Jr. integrated the empirically supported elements of these into the Comprehensive System (1974 onward), which became the dominant standard, coding location (W whole, D common detail, Dd unusual detail, S space), determinants (form, colour, shading, human movement M, animal movement FM, and so on), form quality, content, popular responses, and special scores, summarised in a structural summary with indices such as the Perceptual Thinking Index. Its successor is the Rorschach Performance Assessment System (R-PAS; Gregory Meyer, Donald Viglione, Joni Mihura, Robert Erard and Philip Erdberg, 2011). Henry A. Murray, by contrast, is famous for a different projective test, the Thematic Apperception Test, developed with Christiana Morgan (1935; manual 1943), in which the person tells stories to ambiguous pictures; it is interpreted in terms of Murray's needs and press. Murray did not create a Rorschach scoring system, which makes his name the planted distractor. Other projective tests to keep separate: Holtzman Inkblot Technique (1961), Rosenzweig Picture-Frustration Study, sentence completion tests, and the Draw-A-Person. Option d is right because Murray is associated with the TAT, not with any recognised Rorschach scoring system.","traps":{"a":"Samuel J. Beck's system (from 1937) was one of the first and most influential American Rorschach scoring systems. It is a genuine system, so it cannot be the 'NOT' answer; it tempts if confused with Aaron Beck of cognitive therapy.","b":"Bruno Klopfer's system (from 1942) was a major, more phenomenological Rorschach approach. It is a real Rorschach system, so it is not the exception.","c":"John Exner's Comprehensive System (1974 onward) integrated earlier systems and became the standard method of Rorschach scoring. It is clearly a recognised system, so it is not the answer."}},
{"q":"Murray's TAT (Thematic Apperception Test) is rooted in his theory of:","concept":"Henry A. Murray, at the Harvard Psychological Clinic, set out his personology in 'Explorations in Personality' (1938). He held that behaviour is driven by needs, internal forces that organise perception, thought and action to change an unsatisfying situation. He listed about 20 psychogenic (secondary, psychological) needs, as distinct from viscerogenic (bodily) needs: for example achievement (n Ach), affiliation (n Aff), dominance, autonomy, aggression, abasement, nurturance, succorance, order, play, exhibition and understanding. Needs interact with press, the environmental forces that help or hinder need satisfaction; Murray distinguished alpha press (the objective environment) from beta press (the environment as the person perceives it). A characteristic need and press combination is called a thema, and recurring themas reveal personality. The Thematic Apperception Test (Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray, 1935; Murray, 1943 manual) puts this into practice: the person tells a story about each of a series of ambiguous pictures (31 cards, typically about 10 to 20 used), saying what led up to the scene, what is happening, what the characters feel and think, and how it ends. The assumption is apperception: people project their own needs and perceived press onto the hero of the story. Interpretation identifies the hero's needs, the press acting on the hero, outcomes and themas. David McClelland and John Atkinson later used TAT-type stories to score need for achievement, need for affiliation and need for power. Related tests: the Children's Apperception Test (Leopold Bellak, 1949) with animal figures. Option b is right because the TAT is rooted in Murray's theory of psychogenic needs and environmental press, the need-press theory of personology.","traps":{"a":"Personal constructs is George Kelly's (1955) theory, assessed with the Role Construct Repertory (Rep) Test. It tempts because it is also a classic personality theory with its own test, but it is not Murray's.","c":"Operant conditioning is B. F. Skinner's learning theory, which rejects inner needs as explanations. It tempts only as a famous theory name; it has no link to projective storytelling.","d":"Cognitive distortions are part of Aaron T. Beck's (1967) cognitive model of depression. It tempts because stories reveal thinking, but the TAT is a psychodynamic, needs-based projective method."}},
{"q":"The 16PF (Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire) was developed by:","concept":"The Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16PF) was built by Raymond B. Cattell and first published in 1949 through his Institute for Personality and Ability Testing (IPAT). Cattell's route to it is the classic example of the lexical-plus-factor-analytic approach to personality. He started from the lexical hypothesis: important individual differences get encoded as words in everyday language. Gordon Allport and Henry Odbert (1936) had pulled about 4,500 stable trait terms out of the dictionary; Cattell boiled these down to roughly 170 clusters, then to about 35 rating scales, and factor-analysed three kinds of data: L-data (life records and observer ratings), Q-data (questionnaire self-report) and T-data (objective test behaviour). The factors that kept recurring became his 16 'source traits' (for example Warmth, Reasoning, Emotional Stability, Dominance, Liveliness, Rule-Consciousness, Social Boldness, Sensitivity, Vigilance, Abstractedness, Privateness, Apprehension, Openness to Change, Self-Reliance, Perfectionism, Tension). Source traits are the deep underlying causes; 'surface traits' are clusters of behaviour that merely co-occur. Because Cattell used oblique (correlated) rotation, the 16 primaries themselves correlate, and second-order factor analysis yields five 'global factors' (Extraversion, Anxiety, Tough-Mindedness, Independence, Self-Control) in the fifth edition (1993), which map loosely onto the Big Five. Cattell is also the source of the fluid versus crystallised intelligence split (with John Horn) and the 'specification equation' predicting behaviour from weighted traits. Examiners like to set him against Hans Eysenck, who preferred a few broad orthogonal super-factors, and against Costa and McCrae's five-factor NEO inventories. Option a is right because the 16PF is Raymond B. Cattell's instrument, the direct product of his factor analysis of lexical trait descriptors into 16 primary source traits.","traps":{"b":"Hans Eysenck built the PEN model (Psychoticism, Extraversion, Neuroticism) measured by the EPI (1964) and EPQ (1975); he argued for three broad, biologically based super-factors and criticised Cattell's 16 as unstable and redundant. Tempting because both were factor-analytic trait theorists in London-linked traditions.","c":"Paul Costa and Robert McCrae developed the NEO-PI (1985) and NEO-PI-R (1992) measuring the Five-Factor Model, five domains with six facets each, not sixteen factors. Tempting because the 16PF global factors resemble the Big Five.","d":"Henry Murray proposed a theory of psychogenic needs (1938) and, with Christiana Morgan, created the Thematic Apperception Test (1935), a projective test, not a factor-analytic questionnaire. Tempting only as a famous personality-test name."}},
{"q":"The five factors of the NEO-PI-R / Big Five model are:","concept":"The Big Five or Five-Factor Model (FFM) is the dominant descriptive model of personality traits. It grew out of the lexical tradition: Ernest Tupes and Raymond Christal (1961) re-analysed Cattell's rating data and kept finding five factors, Warren Norman (1963) replicated them, and Lewis Goldberg (1981, 1990) coined and popularised the label 'Big Five' from adjective studies. Paul T. Costa Jr. and Robert R. McCrae turned the model into a questionnaire framework: the NEO Personality Inventory (1985) and the revised NEO-PI-R (1992), 240 items on a five-point scale, plus the 60-item short NEO-FFI. The acronym OCEAN (or CANOE) gives the five domains: Openness to Experience (imagination, curiosity, aesthetic sensitivity), Conscientiousness (organisation, self-discipline, dutifulness), Extraversion (sociability, assertiveness, positive emotion), Agreeableness (trust, altruism, compliance, modesty) and Neuroticism (anxiety, angry hostility, depression, vulnerability). The NEO-PI-R structure is hierarchical: 5 domains x 6 facets = 30 facets, each facet measured by 8 items. McCrae and Costa treat the traits as 'basic tendencies' that are substantially heritable (twin studies suggest roughly 40 to 60 percent), fairly stable after age 30, and found across many cultures. Clinically the FFM matters because DSM-5-TR (APA, 2022) Section III's Alternative Model of Personality Disorders uses five pathological trait domains (Negative Affectivity, Detachment, Antagonism, Disinhibition, Psychoticism) that are maladaptive variants of the Big Five. Do not confuse the FFM with Eysenck's three-factor PEN model, Cattell's 16 primaries, the HEXACO six-factor model (which adds Honesty-Humility), Jungian types, or motive lists. Option b is right because Openness, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness and Neuroticism are exactly the five domains of the NEO-PI-R and the Big Five.","traps":{"a":"Sanguine, choleric, melancholic and phlegmatic are the four ancient temperaments from Hippocrates' humoral theory as developed by Galen; 'Mixed' is not a fifth humour. Tempting because it is also a list of personality types, and Eysenck later mapped these temperaments onto his E and N dimensions.","c":"This is a scrambled list of Carl Jung's (1921) attitudes and functions as operationalised in the Myers-Briggs Type Indicator (Introversion/Extraversion, Sensation, Thinking, Judging); these are types, not the Big Five traits. Tempting because Extraversion appears in both.","d":"Achievement, affiliation and power are David McClelland's learned needs (with intimacy from Dan McAdams and autonomy from Murray's need list or self-determination theory); these are motives measured by the TAT, not trait domains. Tempting because it is another five-item personality list."}},
{"q":"The Bhatia Battery of Performance Tests of Intelligence is suitable primarily for which population?","concept":"The Bhatia Battery of Performance Tests of Intelligence was developed by C. M. Bhatia (1955) in India as a performance (non-verbal) intelligence battery. Verbal IQ tests assume literacy, schooling and familiarity with the language and culture of the test, so they underestimate the ability of people who cannot read or who come from rural backgrounds with little formal education. A performance battery instead asks the person to manipulate materials, copy designs and solve visual problems, so the instructions can be given orally or by demonstration and responses are non-verbal. Bhatia's battery has five subtests: Koh's Block Design (reproducing coloured designs with blocks), Alexander's Pass-Along test (sliding blocks to match a pattern), Pattern Drawing, Immediate Memory (digit span forward and backward, the one component that needs auditory recall rather than reading), and Picture Construction. It was standardised on Indian samples (roughly 11 to 16 years and adults), with separate norms for literate and illiterate groups, and is still widely used in Indian clinical settings to estimate IQ in adolescents and adults, particularly for intellectual disability certification. It belongs to the family of culture-reduced or non-verbal measures alongside Raven's Progressive Matrices (1938), the Kohs Block Design and the Wechsler performance subtests. Examiners also like to ask which of its subtests are borrowed (Koh's blocks, Alexander's pass-along) and the 1955 date. Limits: performance tests are not culture-free (they still depend on speed and familiarity with test-like tasks), and the norms are now old. Option b is right because the battery was designed precisely for illiterate or rural Indian adolescents and adults for whom verbal intelligence tests are not applicable.","traps":{"a":"English-speaking urban adults are exactly the group who can take verbal tests such as the WAIS or Binet-type scales, so they do not need a performance battery. Tempting because the battery can be used with literates too, but that is not its primary purpose.","c":"Pre-school children are assessed with scales such as the Seguin Form Board, the Gesell schedules, the Bayley scales or the Developmental Screening Test; the Bhatia norms start in early adolescence. Tempting because non-verbal tests are often used with young children.","d":"Cortically blind patients cannot see the blocks, designs or pictures that every visual subtest depends on, so a visual performance battery would be invalid for them. Tempting only because 'cannot use verbal tests' suggests a disability group."}},
{"q":"The Vineland Social Maturity Scale, used to assess social/adaptive behaviour from infancy to adulthood, was developed by:","concept":"The Vineland Social Maturity Scale (VSMS) was created by Edgar A. Doll at the Vineland Training School in New Jersey; it was published in 1935 (with a fuller manual in 1947). Doll's insight was that intellectual disability should be defined not only by a low IQ but by a failure of social competence, meaning the ability to look after oneself and take part in community life. The scale is not a test the child performs: an informant (usually a parent or caregiver) is interviewed about what the person habitually does, and items are arranged by age level from birth to adulthood. Doll's original items covered eight areas: self-help general, self-help eating, self-help dressing, self-direction, occupation, communication, locomotion and socialisation. Total credits convert into a Social Age (SA), and a Social Quotient is computed as SQ = (SA / CA) x 100, directly parallel to the ratio IQ. In India the standard version is A. J. Malin's (1965, Nagpur) adaptation for Indian children, still used for disability certification. The modern revisions are the Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales by Sara Sparrow, David Balla and Domenic Cicchetti (1984), the Vineland-II (2005) and Vineland-3 (2016), organised into Communication, Daily Living Skills, Socialization and Motor Skills domains with an optional Maladaptive Behavior index. This links to DSM-5-TR (APA, 2022), where intellectual developmental disorder requires deficits in both intellectual functions and adaptive functioning (conceptual, social and practical domains), and severity is graded by adaptive functioning rather than IQ. Option b is right because Edgar Doll developed the Vineland Social Maturity Scale to measure social competence from infancy to adulthood.","traps":{"a":"Lewis Terman of Stanford revised Binet's scale into the Stanford-Binet (1916) and popularised the ratio IQ; he also ran the longitudinal gifted study. Tempting because Doll's Social Quotient copies Terman's IQ formula.","c":"David Wechsler created the Wechsler-Bellevue (1939), WAIS and WISC with the deviation IQ; these are intelligence tests, not informant-based adaptive behaviour scales. Tempting because he is the default 'test author' name.","d":"Alfred Binet, with Theodore Simon, wrote the first practical intelligence scale (1905) and introduced mental age; he did not develop a social maturity scale. Tempting because both instruments use an age-scale format."}},
{"q":"The key conceptual difference between the Stanford-Binet (Terman 1916) ratio IQ and the Wechsler deviation IQ is that:","concept":"Intelligence quotients have been computed in two very different ways. The ratio IQ was proposed by William Stern (1912) and popularised by Lewis Terman in the 1916 Stanford-Binet: IQ = (Mental Age / Chronological Age) x 100. Mental age comes from Binet and Simon's age-scale logic: if a child passes the items typically passed by 10-year-olds, MA = 10. A 10-year-old with MA 12 gets IQ 120. The problem is that mental age stops growing in the mid to late teens while chronological age keeps rising, so the ratio produces falling IQs in adults; also, the spread of ratio IQs differs from age to age, so an IQ of 115 does not mean the same relative standing at 6 and at 12. David Wechsler solved this with the deviation IQ in the Wechsler-Bellevue (1939): raw scores are converted into standard scores relative to the person's own age group, with mean 100 and standard deviation 15. A score of 115 is therefore always one SD above the age mean (about the 84th percentile), 130 is two SDs above (about the 98th percentile), and 70 is two SDs below, the traditional cut-off associated with intellectual disability. The Stanford-Binet itself switched to deviation IQs in 1960 (it used SD 16 until the fifth edition in 2003, which moved to 15). Related standard-score metrics: z (mean 0, SD 1), T (mean 50, SD 10), and Wechsler subtest scaled scores (mean 10, SD 3). Both IQ types can be used with children; the difference is how the number is derived, not who it is used on. Option a is right because ratio IQ is MA divided by CA times 100, whereas deviation IQ is a standard score (mean 100, SD 15) locating the person relative to same-age peers.","traps":{"b":"Deviation IQ is used for children as well as adults (WISC, WPPSI and the modern Stanford-Binet all use it), so the difference is not age of use. Tempting because the ratio IQ's breakdown in adulthood is its best-known flaw.","c":"Reliability is not the distinguishing feature, and the ratio IQ is psychometrically weaker because its variability changes across ages. Tempting because older tests sound 'established'.","d":"This is backwards: the deviation IQ is precisely a standardised score derived from age-group norms, not a raw score. Tempting only through confusion of 'deviation' with 'raw deviation from a total'."}},
{"q":"Festinger's cognitive dissonance theory predicts that when our attitudes and behaviour conflict, we typically:","concept":"Cognitive dissonance theory was proposed by Leon Festinger (1957, A Theory of Cognitive Dissonance). Dissonance is an aversive state of tension, like hunger, that arises when a person holds two psychologically inconsistent cognitions, for example 'I lied to someone' and 'I am an honest person'. Because it is unpleasant, people are motivated to reduce it, and they have three routes: change one of the cognitions (usually the attitude), add consonant cognitions that justify the inconsistency, or reduce the importance of the conflicting cognitions. Behaviour that has already happened cannot be undone, so the usual result is that the attitude shifts toward the behaviour. The classic demonstration is Festinger and J. Merrill Carlsmith (1959): students did a boring peg-turning task, then were paid either 1 dollar or 20 dollars to tell the next participant it was fun. Those paid 1 dollar later rated the task as more enjoyable, because they lacked sufficient external justification (insufficient justification effect); the 20 dollar group could blame the money. Related paradigms: effort justification (Aronson and Mills, 1959, harsh initiation makes a dull group seem more attractive), post-decision dissonance or 'spreading of alternatives' (Brehm, 1956), and induced hypocrisy. Dissonance needs choice and responsibility for foreseeable consequences. The main rival explanation is Daryl Bem's (1967, 1972) self-perception theory, which says people simply infer their attitudes by observing their own behaviour with no tension involved; self-perception fits small shifts in weak attitudes, dissonance fits counter-attitudinal acts that produce measurable arousal. Option b is right because Festinger predicts that, when behaviour and attitude clash and the act cannot be undone, people reduce dissonance mainly by changing the attitude to fit the behaviour.","traps":{"a":"Changing behaviour is a theoretically possible route but usually the harder one, because a past act cannot be reversed; the induced-compliance studies show attitude change, not behaviour change. Tempting because it sounds like the 'rational' fix.","c":"Permanent suppression of awareness resembles psychoanalytic repression, not Festinger's mechanism; dissonance theory predicts active reduction of the tension, not the inconsistency vanishing from awareness. Tempting because trivialisation and avoidance of information are real minor strategies.","d":"This contradicts the core of the theory: dissonance is defined as aversive psychological tension, and later studies (for example misattribution-of-arousal experiments by Zanna and Cooper, 1974) showed it behaves like arousal. Tempting only as a reading of Bem's self-perception theory."}},
{"q":"Solomon Asch's line-judgement experiments demonstrated:","concept":"Solomon Asch's conformity studies (1951, 1955, 1956) asked a simple question: will people deny the evidence of their own eyes to agree with a group? A real participant sat with 6 to 8 confederates and judged which of three comparison lines matched a standard line; the answer was obvious (control participants alone erred less than 1 percent of the time). On 12 of 18 'critical' trials the confederates unanimously gave the same wrong answer before the participant spoke. Results: participants conformed on about 37 percent of critical trials, roughly 75 percent conformed at least once, and about 25 percent never conformed. Asch's variations are high-yield: conformity rose with group size up to about 3 or 4 confederates and then levelled off; a single dissenting ally cut conformity by about three quarters (breaking unanimity is the key); and writing answers privately sharply reduced conformity. That last finding shows the main driver was normative social influence, the wish to be liked and avoid rejection, which produces public compliance without private acceptance. This is distinguished, in Morton Deutsch and Harold Gerard's (1955) terms, from informational social influence, where people accept others' views as evidence about reality (seen in Muzafer Sherif's ambiguous autokinetic task). Herbert Kelman (1958) further separated compliance, identification and internalisation. Cross-cultural meta-analysis (Bond and Smith, 1996) found higher conformity in collectivist cultures. Do not mix Asch with Milgram (obedience to a direct command from authority) or with Darley and Latane (bystander intervention). Option b is right because the line-judgement studies showed people conforming to a clearly incorrect unanimous majority, mainly through normative social influence.","traps":{"a":"Obedience to authority is Stanley Milgram's (1963) shock paradigm, where an experimenter gives direct orders; in Asch's study peers of equal status give no orders at all. Tempting because Milgram was Asch's student and both are 'social influence' classics.","c":"Bystander apathy comes from John Darley and Bibb Latane (1968), prompted by the Kitty Genovese case: people are less likely to help when others are present. Tempting because Asch's experiment also involves the presence of a group.","d":"Diffusion of responsibility is the mechanism Darley and Latane proposed for the bystander effect (each person feels less personally responsible in a group). Tempting because it is another group-presence effect, but it concerns helping, not judging lines."}},
{"q":"In Milgram's obedience studies, approximately what percentage of participants delivered the maximum 450-volt shock in the baseline condition?","concept":"Stanley Milgram's obedience studies at Yale (1963; full series in Obedience to Authority, 1974) tested whether ordinary people would obey an authority who told them to hurt someone. A participant in the 'teacher' role was told to give increasing electric shocks, in 15-volt steps from 15 to 450 volts (labelled 'Danger: Severe Shock' and finally 'XXX'), to a 'learner' (a confederate, actually receiving no shocks) for every wrong answer on a word-pair task. The learner protested, screamed, complained of a heart condition and then fell silent. When the teacher hesitated, the experimenter used four standard prods, ending with 'You have no other choice, you must go on.' Before the study, psychiatrists polled by Milgram predicted that only about 1 in 1,000 would go to the end. In the baseline (remote-victim) condition, 26 of 40 men, that is 65 percent, went all the way to 450 volts, and every participant went to at least 300 volts. The variations are frequently examined: obedience fell to about 40 percent when the teacher was in the same room as the learner, 30 percent when the teacher had to hold the learner's hand on the shock plate, about 20.5 percent when the experimenter gave orders by telephone, about 48 percent when the study moved to a run-down office in Bridgeport, and about 10 percent when two peer teachers refused. Milgram explained the effect through the 'agentic state', where people see themselves as instruments of another's will and shift responsibility to the authority. The studies prompted major ethics reforms on deception and the right to withdraw. Jerry Burger's (2009) partial replication, stopping at 150 volts, found similar obedience rates. Option c is right because about 65 percent of baseline participants delivered the full 450-volt shock.","traps":{"a":"About 10 percent is the obedience level in Milgram's variation where two peer teachers rebelled, not the baseline. Tempting because it sits close to the psychiatrists' very low predictions.","b":"About 35 percent is roughly the fraction who DISOBEYED in the baseline (14 of 40), or close to Asch's 37 percent conformity rate on critical trials. Tempting because it mixes up the two classic percentages.","d":"100 percent is the proportion who went to at least 300 volts, not to the maximum 450 volts; 14 participants stopped before the end. Tempting because 'everyone obeyed to 300' is a well-known secondary finding."}},
{"q":"Muzafer Sherif's autokinetic-effect experiments demonstrated:","concept":"Muzafer Sherif (1935, 1936, The Psychology of Social Norms) studied how group norms are born when reality is ambiguous. He used the autokinetic effect, a perceptual illusion in which a stationary pinpoint of light in a completely dark room seems to move, because without a visual frame of reference small eye movements are misread as motion. Participants judged how far the light moved. Tested alone, each person quickly settled on a personal range (some said 2 inches, others 8). Then they were tested in groups of two or three, announcing estimates aloud; over a few sessions their estimates converged on a shared group norm. Crucially, when later tested alone again, people kept using the group norm, which shows private acceptance (internalisation), not just public compliance. Later work (Jacobs and Campbell, 1961) showed such arbitrary norms are transmitted across 'generations' as members are replaced. The mechanism is informational social influence (Deutsch and Gerard, 1955): when we are uncertain, other people's judgements serve as evidence about what is real. Contrast this with Solomon Asch's line task, where the correct answer was obvious and conformity was mainly normative (fear of rejection) and largely disappeared in private. A neat exam pairing: ambiguous task, informational influence, private acceptance (Sherif) versus unambiguous task, normative influence, public compliance (Asch). Sherif is also known for the Robbers Cave field experiment (1954/1961) and realistic conflict theory, which concerns intergroup hostility, not norm formation. Option a is right because the autokinetic studies showed people relying on each other's judgements in an ambiguous situation, forming a lasting group norm through informational social influence.","traps":{"b":"Pure obedience to authority is Stanley Milgram's paradigm; in Sherif's study no one gave orders and participants were equal peers. Tempting because both are classic social influence experiments.","c":"The bystander effect (Darley and Latane, 1968) concerns reduced helping when others are present in an emergency, not convergence of perceptual estimates. Tempting because it also involves groups changing individual behaviour.","d":"Implicit bias in stereotype activation belongs to later social cognition research (for example Patricia Devine, 1989; Greenwald's Implicit Association Test, 1998), not to Sherif's 1930s perceptual norms. Tempting only as a distractor about automatic social processes."}},
{"q":"Fundamental Attribution Error refers to the tendency to:","concept":"The fundamental attribution error (FAE) is the tendency, when explaining other people's behaviour, to overestimate dispositional (personality, trait, attitude) causes and underestimate situational causes. The term was coined by Lee Ross (1977); Edward Jones and Daniel Gilbert prefer 'correspondence bias', the inference that behaviour corresponds to a stable trait. It builds on Fritz Heider's (1958) naive psychology, which first distinguished internal and external attributions, and on Harold Kelley's (1967) covariation model (consensus, distinctiveness, consistency). The classic demonstration is Edward Jones and Victor Harris (1967): American students read essays for or against Fidel Castro and judged the writer's true attitude. Even when told the writer had been ASSIGNED the position, they still inferred the writer held it. Ross, Amabile and Steinmetz's (1977) quiz-master study showed observers rated randomly assigned questioners as more knowledgeable than contestants. Explanations include perceptual salience (the actor dominates the visual field), the two-step model of Gilbert (1989), in which trait inference is automatic while situational correction is effortful and gets skipped under cognitive load, and cultural norms: Michael Morris and Kaiping Peng (1994) and Joan Miller (1984) found the bias weaker in collectivist cultures such as India and China. Near neighbours: the actor-observer bias (Jones and Nisbett, 1971) adds that we explain our OWN behaviour situationally; the self-serving bias takes credit for success and blames failure on the situation; the just-world belief (Melvin Lerner) assumes people get what they deserve. Option b is right because the FAE is precisely the overestimation of dispositional and underestimation of situational causes when we explain other people's behaviour.","traps":{"a":"This states the error in reverse; overweighting the situation for others is not the FAE (if anything, situational explanations are what people use for their own behaviour in the actor-observer bias). Tempting because it uses the same words with the direction flipped.","c":"Taking credit for successes and blaming failures on outside factors is the self-serving bias, which concerns one's own outcomes, not observers' explanations of others. Tempting because it is another attribution bias.","d":"Believing the world is fair is Melvin Lerner's just-world hypothesis (1965, 1980), which leads to victim blaming. Tempting because victim blaming is also a dispositional explanation, but it is a motivated belief, not the FAE itself."}},
{"q":"The concept of the 'self-fulfilling prophecy' was formalised by which sociologist?","concept":"The self-fulfilling prophecy was formalised by the American sociologist Robert K. Merton in a 1948 essay of that title (Antioch Review), later included in Social Theory and Social Structure. Merton built on the 'Thomas theorem' of W. I. Thomas and Dorothy Swaine Thomas (1928): 'If men define situations as real, they are real in their consequences.' Merton's definition: a self-fulfilling prophecy is, in the beginning, a false definition of the situation that evokes new behaviour which makes the originally false conception come true. His famous example is the Last National Bank: a solvent bank is rumoured to be failing, depositors rush to withdraw their money, and the bank actually collapses. He applied the idea to racial prejudice, showing how beliefs about a group led to exclusion that then produced the very conditions used to justify the belief. Psychology took the idea into the classroom and lab. Robert Rosenthal and Lenore Jacobson (1968, Pygmalion in the Classroom) told teachers that randomly chosen pupils were 'late bloomers'; those pupils gained more IQ points, the Pygmalion effect (effects are real but smaller than first claimed). Rosenthal also showed experimenter expectancy effects, and Mark Snyder, Elizabeth Tanke and Ellen Berscheid (1977) showed men who believed they were talking to an attractive woman elicited warmer behaviour from her. The mechanism has three steps: a perceiver's expectation, perceiver behaviour that conveys it, and the target's behaviour coming to confirm it. Related but different: the Hawthorne effect (change from being observed) and stereotype threat. Option b is right because Robert K. Merton is the sociologist who named and formalised the self-fulfilling prophecy.","traps":{"a":"Erving Goffman developed dramaturgical analysis (The Presentation of Self in Everyday Life, 1959), stigma and total institutions (Asylums, 1961). Tempting because labelling and stigma also involve others' definitions shaping a person.","c":"Charles Horton Cooley (1902) proposed the looking-glass self and the concept of primary groups. Tempting because the looking-glass self also describes others' views shaping the self, but he did not formalise the self-fulfilling prophecy.","d":"Emile Durkheim wrote on anomie, social facts and suicide (Suicide, 1897). Tempting simply as a founding sociologist name."}},
{"q":"Henri Tajfel's Social Identity Theory proposes that group-based behaviour is driven primarily by:","concept":"Social Identity Theory (SIT) was developed by Henri Tajfel and John Turner (1979, 1986) to explain intergroup discrimination. Its starting point was Tajfel's minimal group paradigm (Tajfel, Billig, Bundy and Flament, 1971): British schoolboys were split into groups on trivial grounds (supposedly preferring Klee or Kandinsky paintings, or over- versus under-estimating dots), never met their group, and had no self-interest at stake. Yet when allocating points to anonymous others, they favoured their in-group and often chose options that maximised the DIFFERENCE between groups over options that gave their own group more in absolute terms. Mere categorisation was enough for in-group favouritism. SIT explains this through three processes: social categorisation (sorting people into groups), social identification (adopting the group identity as part of the self-concept) and social comparison (comparing the in-group with out-groups). Because part of self-esteem comes from group membership, people seek positive distinctiveness, a favourable comparison of 'us' with 'them'. When a group's identity is negative, members may use individual mobility (leave the group), social creativity (redefine the comparison dimension) or social competition. Turner's later self-categorisation theory (1987) extends SIT. The key contrast is Muzafer Sherif's realistic conflict theory: in the Robbers Cave study (1954/1961), boys in two camps became hostile when competing for scarce prizes and reconciled through superordinate goals. SIT shows conflict can arise even without any real competition. Gordon Allport's (1954) contact hypothesis is the classic prejudice-reduction counterpart. Option b is right because Tajfel's theory holds that group behaviour is driven by the need for a positive social identity achieved through favourable in-group versus out-group comparison.","traps":{"a":"Competition for scarce resources is Muzafer Sherif's realistic conflict theory (Robbers Cave); Tajfel designed the minimal group studies precisely to show bias without any such competition. Tempting because it is the main rival theory of intergroup conflict.","c":"Genetic kin selection is W. D. Hamilton's (1964) evolutionary account of favouring relatives; minimal groups are strangers, so kinship cannot explain the bias. Tempting as an evolutionary 'us versus them' explanation.","d":"Operant reinforcement of in-group behaviours is a Skinnerian learning account; in minimal groups no one is rewarded for favouritism, which emerges immediately. Tempting because learning theories also explain prejudice acquisition."}},
{"q":"Zimbardo's Stanford Prison Experiment (1971) is most often cited as a demonstration of:","concept":"The Stanford Prison Experiment (SPE) was run by Philip Zimbardo with Craig Haney and Curtis Banks in August 1971 in the basement of Stanford's psychology building (published as Haney, Banks and Zimbardo, 1973). Twenty-four male college students, screened as psychologically healthy, were randomly assigned by coin flip to be 'guards' or 'prisoners'. Prisoners were 'arrested' at home by real police, deindividuated with smocks, stocking caps and ID numbers instead of names; guards wore khaki uniforms and mirrored sunglasses and had batons. Zimbardo acted as prison superintendent. Within days, guards became increasingly harsh and degrading (sleep deprivation, forced exercise, solitary confinement in a closet), some prisoners rebelled and others showed acute stress, and five prisoners were released early. The study, planned for two weeks, was stopped after six days, partly after Christina Maslach objected. Zimbardo's conclusion: the situation and the social roles people are placed in can overwhelm individual dispositions, turning ordinary people abusive, which he later applied to Abu Ghraib (The Lucifer Effect, 2007). Concepts tested here include deindividuation, role internalisation, and the 'power of the situation'. Critiques are now standard knowledge: no control group, Zimbardo's dual role as researcher and superintendent, guards were coached to be tough (Thibault Le Texier, 2018), demand characteristics, and serious ethical harm. Stephen Reicher and Alexander Haslam's BBC Prison Study (2006) found guards did not automatically become tyrannical, supporting a social identity explanation instead. Despite this, exam questions treat the SPE as the canonical demonstration of situational power. Option a is right because the SPE is cited as showing how assigned social roles and situational forces can shape behaviour.","traps":{"b":"The SPE tested no genetic factor; participants were randomly assigned, which is the opposite logic (it rules out dispositions, including genes, as the cause of group differences). Tempting because the study involved aggression.","c":"Classical conditioning of fear is Watson and Rayner's (1920) Little Albert study, pairing a white rat with a loud noise. Tempting because prisoners showed fear and distress.","d":"Reinforcement schedules are B. F. Skinner's (Ferster and Skinner, 1957) operant work on fixed and variable ratio and interval schedules. Tempting because guards used rewards and punishments, but the study was not about schedules."}},
{"q":"Cooley's 'looking-glass self' proposes that self-concept is formed through:","concept":"The looking-glass self was proposed by the American sociologist Charles Horton Cooley in Human Nature and the Social Order (1902). His couplet sums it up: 'Each to each a looking-glass / Reflects the other that doth pass.' The idea is that we do not discover the self by private introspection; we build it from the reactions we imagine others have to us, as if other people were mirrors. Cooley described three components: first, we imagine how we appear to another person; second, we imagine that person's judgement of that appearance; third, we experience a self-feeling, such as pride or mortification (shame), based on that imagined judgement. Note the word 'imagine': what matters is our perception of others' views, which can be wrong. Cooley also introduced the concept of the primary group (family, close friends) as the main setting for this process. The idea became a foundation of symbolic interactionism, developed by George Herbert Mead (Mind, Self and Society, 1934), who distinguished the spontaneous 'I' from the socialised 'Me', described taking the role of the 'generalised other', and traced development through play and game stages; Herbert Blumer coined 'symbolic interactionism' in 1937. Psychological descendants include reflected appraisals in self-esteem research, Mark Leary's sociometer theory (self-esteem as a gauge of social acceptance), and labelling theory. Contrast with William James's (1890) material, social and spiritual selves and Carl Rogers's self-concept shaped by conditions of worth. Option c is right because Cooley's model forms the self-concept through imagining how we appear to others, imagining their judgement, and developing self-feelings from it.","traps":{"a":"Solitary introspection is the opposite of Cooley's point, which is that the self is a social product; introspection as a method is associated with Wundt and Titchener. Tempting because 'self-concept' sounds like something found by looking inward.","b":"Genetic inheritance of personality traits is a behaviour-genetic account (twin and adoption studies), not a sociological theory of self. Tempting as a generic origin-of-personality answer.","d":"Operant reinforcement of self-statements is a behaviourist (Skinnerian) account of self-description. Tempting because others' reactions can act like reinforcement, but Cooley stressed imagined judgement and self-feelings, not reinforcement contingencies."}},
{"q":"Which component was ADDED to Baddeley's working memory model in 2000?","concept":"Working memory is the limited-capacity system that holds and manipulates information during ongoing tasks such as reasoning, comprehension and mental arithmetic. Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch (1974) replaced Atkinson and Shiffrin's single short-term store with a multi-component model, based on dual-task studies showing that people could hold digits while reasoning with only modest interference. The original model had three parts: the central executive, an attentional control system with no storage of its own that allocates resources, switches tasks and inhibits irrelevant information; the phonological loop, which stores speech-based material in a passive phonological store (about 2 seconds of sound) refreshed by an articulatory rehearsal process (evidence: phonological similarity effect, word-length effect, articulatory suppression); and the visuospatial sketchpad, which holds visual and spatial information (Logie later split it into a visual cache and an inner scribe). In 2000, Baddeley (Trends in Cognitive Sciences) added a fourth component, the episodic buffer: a limited-capacity, multimodal store (about 4 chunks) that binds information from the subsystems and from long-term memory into integrated episodes, and that is thought to be accessible to conscious awareness. It was needed to explain findings the three-part model could not, such as recall of prose far exceeding span for unrelated words (chunking via long-term knowledge), immediate recall in amnesic patients with intact intelligence, and how visual and verbal codes are combined. Do not confuse with Nelson Cowan's embedded-processes model or Tulving's episodic long-term memory; the buffer is a working-memory store, not long-term episodic memory. Option d is right because the episodic buffer is the component Baddeley added in 2000; the other three belonged to the original 1974 model.","traps":{"a":"The phonological loop was one of the original 1974 slave systems (articulatory loop in the earliest version). Tempting because it was later subdivided into store and rehearsal, which can look like an 'addition'.","b":"The visuospatial sketchpad was also part of the original 1974 model. Tempting because Logie's later visual cache and inner scribe split looks like a revision.","c":"The central executive was the core controlling component from the start in 1974; Baddeley refined it in 1986 (borrowing Norman and Shallice's supervisory attentional system) but did not add it in 2000. Tempting because it is the least well specified component."}},
{"q":"In Atkinson and Shiffrin's multi-store memory model, which store has the LARGEST capacity?","concept":"Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin's (1968) multi-store or 'modal' model divides memory into three structural stores with control processes moving information between them. The sensory register holds a brief, raw, modality-specific copy of input: iconic memory for vision lasts roughly 250 to 500 milliseconds (George Sperling, 1960, partial-report technique showed people had most of a 12-letter display available for a split second), and echoic memory for sound lasts about 2 to 4 seconds (Darwin, Turvey and Crowder, 1972). Its capacity is large but decays almost immediately; attention selects what moves on. The short-term store (STS) holds a small amount: George Miller (1956) estimated 7 plus or minus 2 chunks, later revised by Nelson Cowan (2001) to about 4. Without rehearsal, information is lost in about 18 to 30 seconds (Brown, 1958; Peterson and Peterson, 1959), and it is coded mainly acoustically (Conrad, 1964; Baddeley, 1966). Rehearsal in the STS transfers information to the long-term store (LTS), which is coded mainly semantically and has essentially unlimited capacity and potentially lifelong duration. Evidence for separate stores: the serial position curve (primacy from LTS, recency from STS; Glanzer and Cunitz, 1966, delay abolished recency), and the double dissociation between patient H.M. (poor new LTS, normal span) and patient K.F. (Shallice and Warrington, 1970, poor span, normal LTS). Criticisms led to Craik and Lockhart's (1972) levels of processing and Baddeley and Hitch's working memory. Option c is right because the long-term store is the component with the largest, effectively unlimited, capacity in the Atkinson-Shiffrin model.","traps":{"a":"The sensory register does have a large capacity, which makes it tempting, but it holds only a fleeting, unprocessed snapshot of current input, whereas the long-term store holds a lifetime of knowledge with no known limit.","b":"The short-term store is the bottleneck of the model, about 7 plus or minus 2 chunks (or about 4), so it has the smallest capacity. Tempting only through misreading the question.","d":"The model's whole point is that the stores differ in capacity, duration and coding, so they are not equal. Tempting as a catch-all for unsure candidates."}},
{"q":"Sternberg's triarchic theory of intelligence comprises:","concept":"Robert J. Sternberg's triarchic theory of intelligence (1985, Beyond IQ) argues that IQ tests capture only one slice of intelligence. It has three sub-theories. The componential (analytical) sub-theory describes the internal mental mechanisms: metacomponents (executive planning, monitoring and evaluating), performance components (actually carrying out the strategy, such as encoding and inference) and knowledge-acquisition components (selective encoding, combination and comparison). This is the intelligence measured by school tests: analysing, comparing, judging. The experiential (creative) sub-theory concerns how a person handles novelty and how well they automatise familiar tasks; creative intelligence is the ability to deal with new problems and generate ideas. The contextual (practical) sub-theory concerns adapting to, shaping, or selecting environments in everyday life, often relying on tacit knowledge (know-how not explicitly taught), which Sternberg measured with tests of practical managerial knowledge; this is 'street smarts'. Sternberg later folded this into the theory of 'successful intelligence' (1997) and developed the Sternberg Triarchic Abilities Test (STAT). Classic comparison: Howard Gardner's theory of multiple intelligences (1983, Frames of Mind) proposes separate, relatively independent intelligences (linguistic, logical-mathematical, spatial, musical, bodily-kinesthetic, interpersonal, intrapersonal, later naturalistic), while Sternberg describes three interacting aspects of one information-processing system. Other near neighbours: Raymond Cattell and John Horn's fluid and crystallised intelligence, the Cattell-Horn-Carroll (CHC) model, Spearman's g, Thurstone's primary mental abilities, and Wechsler index scores. Sternberg also proposed the triangular theory of love (intimacy, passion, commitment), a common mix-up. Option b is right because the triarchic theory consists of analytical, creative and practical intelligence.","traps":{"a":"Verbal, performance and working memory are Wechsler test score labels (verbal IQ, performance IQ, and a later index), not theoretical components. Tempting because they are a familiar three-part split of intelligence.","c":"Crystallised, fluid and processing speed are broad abilities from Cattell and Horn's Gf-Gc theory and the Cattell-Horn-Carroll model. Tempting because it is also a three-item list of intelligence components.","d":"Linguistic, logical-mathematical and spatial are the first three of Howard Gardner's multiple intelligences (1983). Tempting because Gardner is the theorist most often contrasted with Sternberg."}},
{"q":"Spearman's 'g' refers to:","concept":"Charles Spearman (1904, 'General intelligence, objectively determined and measured') noticed that children's scores on very different school subjects and sensory tasks (classics, French, mathematics, pitch discrimination) were all positively correlated, a pattern now called the positive manifold. Using an early form of factor analysis, which he largely invented for this purpose, he concluded that each test measured two things: a general factor, g, common to all intellectual tasks, and a specific factor, s, unique to that test. This is the two-factor theory, set out fully in The Abilities of Man (1927). Spearman described g as 'mental energy', and argued it was best reflected in tasks involving the 'eduction of relations and correlates' (seeing relationships and applying them), which is why matrices and analogy tests load highly on g; John Raven's Progressive Matrices (1938) was designed as a g measure. Responses: Louis Thurstone (1938) found about seven primary mental abilities (verbal comprehension, word fluency, number, space, memory, perceptual speed, reasoning) and initially rejected g, though his factors themselves correlated. Cyril Burt and Philip Vernon (1950) proposed hierarchical models with g at the top, major group factors (verbal-educational and spatial-mechanical) beneath, then minor group and specific factors. Raymond Cattell and John Horn split g into fluid and crystallised, and John Carroll's (1993) three-stratum theory, merged into the Cattell-Horn-Carroll model, places g at stratum III. Guilford's structure-of-intellect model (up to 180 abilities) rejected g entirely. Today g accounts for roughly 40 to 50 percent of variance in test batteries and predicts educational and occupational outcomes. Option b is right because g is Spearman's general factor underlying performance on all diverse cognitive tasks.","traps":{"a":"Abilities unique to each task are Spearman's s factors, the other half of the two-factor theory, not g. Tempting because both letters come from the same theory.","c":"Genetic predisposition to mental disorders is a diathesis or polygenic risk concept, unrelated to Spearman. Tempting because 'g' looks like an abbreviation for 'genetic'.","d":"Group factors are broader than s but narrower than g, emphasised by Burt, Vernon and Thurstone in hierarchical and multiple-factor models. Tempting because 'g' could be read as 'group'."}},
{"q":"Signal Detection Theory introduces which decision parameter that classical psychophysical threshold theory ignored?","concept":"Signal Detection Theory (SDT) entered psychology from radar and communications engineering through Wilson Tanner and John Swets (1954) and was codified by David Green and John Swets (1966, Signal Detection Theory and Psychophysics). Classical psychophysics, from Gustav Fechner (1860), assumed a fixed sensory threshold: below the absolute threshold (defined as the intensity detected 50 percent of the time) a stimulus is not sensed, above it it is. SDT argues that detection is a decision made under uncertainty. There is always background 'noise' (neural and environmental), and a weak signal adds to it, giving two overlapping distributions of internal evidence: noise alone and signal plus noise. The observer sets a cut-off and says 'yes' whenever the evidence exceeds it. This creates four outcomes: hit (signal present, yes), miss (present, no), false alarm (absent, yes) and correct rejection (absent, no). SDT separates two independent quantities. Sensitivity, d' (d-prime), is the distance between the two distributions in standard deviation units and reflects how discriminable the signal is. The response criterion or bias, beta (likelihood ratio at the cut-off) or c (criterion location), reflects how willing the observer is to say yes, and it shifts with payoffs, costs and expectations (base rates). A lenient criterion raises both hits and false alarms; a strict one lowers both. Plotting hits against false alarms across criteria gives the ROC (receiver operating characteristic) curve; area under it indexes sensitivity. Clinical applications include radiology, eyewitness line-ups and diagnostic test cut-offs. Option c is right because the response criterion (beta or c) is the decision parameter SDT introduced, which classical threshold theory ignored.","traps":{"a":"The just-noticeable difference is a classical psychophysics concept (Ernst Weber; Fechner) for the smallest detectable change between two stimuli; it is a threshold idea SDT criticised, not a new decision parameter. Tempting because it is a core psychophysics term.","b":"Weber's constant k is the ratio in Weber's law (delta I / I = k), from Ernst Heinrich Weber (1834), describing difference thresholds. Tempting because it is a famous psychophysical parameter, but it predates and ignores decision processes.","d":"The absolute threshold is the very concept of classical psychophysics that SDT replaced with the sensitivity versus criterion distinction. Tempting because questions about SDT often mention thresholds."}},
{"q":"Wallas's four stages of the creative process are:","concept":"Graham Wallas, a British social psychologist and co-founder of the London School of Economics, proposed his four-stage model of the creative process in The Art of Thought (1926), drawing on introspective accounts by Hermann von Helmholtz and Henri Poincare. The stages are: Preparation, consciously studying the problem, gathering information and trying solutions; Incubation, setting the problem aside while unconscious or non-focused processing continues; Illumination, the sudden insight or 'flash' when the solution appears (Wallas also described a brief 'intimation' sensing that insight is near); and Verification, consciously testing, checking and elaborating the idea. The model remains the standard textbook description, though incubation is debated. Explanations for incubation include rest from fatigue, forgetting of misleading fixations (set-breaking), and spreading activation; meta-analysis (Sio and Ormerod, 2009) found a modest real incubation effect. Illumination links to Gestalt work on insight: Wolfgang Kohler's chimpanzees (1925), Karl Duncker's functional fixedness (1945), and Norman Maier's two-string problem (1931). Do not confuse with Joy Paul Guilford's (1950, 1967) creativity abilities, sensitivity to problems, fluency, flexibility, originality and elaboration, which are traits measured by divergent thinking tests (later operationalised in Ellis Paul Torrance's Torrance Tests of Creative Thinking, 1966); nor with Guilford's divergent versus convergent production; nor with Alex Osborn's (1953) brainstorming and the Osborn-Parnes Creative Problem Solving model. Wallas describes a temporal sequence of the creative act; Guilford describes abilities of the creative person. Option a is right because Wallas's four stages are preparation, incubation, illumination and verification.","traps":{"b":"Problem-finding, brainstorming, selection and implementation resemble applied creative problem-solving models such as Alex Osborn's (1953) brainstorming and the Osborn-Parnes CPS model, not Wallas. Tempting because they also form a creative sequence.","c":"Sensitivity, fluency, flexibility and originality are J. P. Guilford's creativity abilities (with elaboration), measured in divergent thinking tests and by Torrance. Tempting because it is the most famous other four-item creativity list.","d":"Divergent and convergent come from Guilford's structure-of-intellect operations; analytical and practical come from Sternberg's triarchic theory. Tempting because the terms are all creativity and intelligence vocabulary."}},
{"q":"The defense mechanism in which unacceptable wishes are unconsciously redirected into socially valued activities (e.g., aggressive drives channelled into competitive sport) is called:","concept":"Defense mechanisms are unconscious psychological operations that protect the ego from anxiety arising from conflict between id impulses, superego demands and reality. Sigmund Freud introduced the idea (repression from the 1890s) and Anna Freud systematised it in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence (1936). Sublimation is the redirection of an unacceptable drive (sexual or aggressive) into a socially valued, productive activity, so the impulse is expressed but in a transformed, acceptable form: aggression into competitive sport, surgery or debating; sexual energy into art, music or writing. Freud saw sublimation as the basis of civilisation and culture (Civilization and Its Discontents, 1930), and it is the only classical defense he regarded as genuinely healthy. George Vaillant (1977, Adaptation to Life, from the Harvard Grant Study) placed sublimation among the mature defenses with altruism, humour, suppression and anticipation, and found mature defenses predicted better adult adjustment. The exam skill is separating it from neighbours. Displacement redirects the impulse toward a safer TARGET (angry at the boss, kicks the dog) without making it socially valuable. Reaction formation turns the impulse into its OPPOSITE, often exaggerated (hostility shown as excessive sweetness). Projection attributes one's own unacceptable impulse to someone else ('he hates me'). Others: rationalisation (plausible excuses), intellectualisation (abstract talk to avoid feeling), regression (returning to earlier behaviour) and denial (refusing to accept reality). DSM-IV included a Defensive Functioning Scale with levels from high adaptive (sublimation) to psychotic. Option c is right because channelling an unacceptable aggressive drive into a socially valued activity such as competitive sport is the definition of sublimation.","traps":{"a":"Reaction formation converts an impulse into its opposite, for example a person with hostile wishes acting overly kind; the drive is not channelled into productive activity. Tempting because it also transforms the impulse.","b":"Displacement shifts the impulse onto a less threatening target, such as yelling at a sibling instead of a parent, with no socially valuable outcome. It is the closest trap, since sublimation can be seen as a socially useful form of displacement, but the defining feature here is social value.","d":"Projection attributes one's own unacceptable feelings to others, an immature defense in Vaillant's scheme. Tempting only as a well-known defense mechanism."}},
{"q":"In Vaillant's hierarchical model of defense mechanisms, which level includes denial, distortion, and delusional projection?","concept":"George E. Vaillant, a psychiatrist who directed the Harvard Study of Adult Development (Grant Study), arranged defense mechanisms in a developmental hierarchy of maturity (Adaptation to Life, 1977; Ego Mechanisms of Defense, 1992). Level I, psychotic (narcissistic) defenses, are common in psychosis, dreams and early childhood and grossly reshape external reality: delusional projection (frank persecutory delusions), psychotic denial (denial of external reality, for example insisting a dead relative is alive) and distortion (grossly reshaping reality to fit inner needs, such as wish-fulfilling grandiose delusions). Level II, immature defenses, are common in adolescence, personality disorders and depression: projection, schizoid fantasy, hypochondriasis, passive aggression, acting out and (in some versions) dissociation. Level III, neurotic (intermediate) defenses, are common in everyone under stress and in anxiety disorders: intellectualisation (isolation), repression, reaction formation, displacement and dissociation. Level IV, mature defenses, are adaptive and integrate reality, relationships and feelings: altruism, sublimation, suppression (conscious postponement), anticipation and humour. Vaillant's longitudinal data showed that men using mature defenses had better health, marriages, income and happiness decades later. Points examiners test: suppression is mature (conscious) whereas repression is neurotic (unconscious); ordinary projection is immature but delusional projection is psychotic; denial appears at the psychotic level when it denies external reality. DSM-IV's Defensive Functioning Scale used a related seven-level hierarchy (high adaptive, mental inhibitions, minor image-distorting, disavowal, major image-distorting, action, defensive dysregulation). Option d is right because denial, distortion and delusional projection make up Vaillant's psychotic level.","traps":{"a":"Mature defenses are altruism, sublimation, suppression, anticipation and humour, the healthiest level. Tempting only for candidates reversing the hierarchy.","b":"Neurotic defenses are repression, intellectualisation, reaction formation, displacement and dissociation, which distort inner feelings but not external reality. Tempting because 'neurotic' sounds pathological.","c":"Immature defenses include projection, acting out, passive aggression, hypochondriasis and schizoid fantasy. It is the closest trap because plain projection sits here, but DELUSIONAL projection, distortion and psychotic denial belong to the psychotic level."}},
{"q":"A lesion confined to the arcuate fasciculus, with intact comprehension and fluent speech, produces:","concept":"The classic Wernicke-Lichtheim-Geschwind model of language places speech comprehension in Wernicke's area (posterior superior temporal gyrus, left hemisphere), speech production planning in Broca's area (left inferior frontal gyrus, Brodmann areas 44 and 45), and links them by a white-matter tract, the arcuate fasciculus, which arches around the Sylvian fissure through the parietal lobe. Carl Wernicke (1874) predicted that cutting this connection would produce a distinct syndrome, later called conduction aphasia, and Norman Geschwind (1965) revived the disconnection framework. In conduction aphasia, spontaneous speech is fluent (normal rate and grammar), comprehension is relatively intact, but REPETITION is disproportionately impaired, because the heard words cannot be relayed to the production system. Speech contains many phonemic (literal) paraphasias, such as 'tevelision', and patients make repeated self-correcting attempts (conduite d'approche). Naming is often impaired. Lesions are typically in the left supramarginal gyrus, the arcuate fasciculus or the left posterior superior temporal and inferior parietal region; modern work suggests cortical damage there matters as much as the tract itself. A quick classification grid: Broca's aphasia, non-fluent, comprehension good, repetition poor; Wernicke's aphasia, fluent but empty with neologisms, comprehension poor, repetition poor; conduction, fluent, comprehension good, repetition poor; global, non-fluent, comprehension poor, repetition poor; transcortical motor and sensory aphasias resemble Broca's and Wernicke's but with repetition PRESERVED; anomic aphasia, fluent with word-finding difficulty only. Option c is right because a lesion of the arcuate fasciculus with fluent speech and intact comprehension produces conduction aphasia, marked by poor repetition.","traps":{"a":"Broca's aphasia comes from left inferior frontal damage and produces non-fluent, effortful, telegraphic speech, which contradicts the fluent speech in the stem. Tempting because the arcuate fasciculus connects to Broca's area.","b":"Wernicke's aphasia from posterior superior temporal damage produces fluent speech with poor comprehension, contradicting the intact comprehension in the stem. Tempting because the tract begins in Wernicke's region.","d":"Global aphasia from large perisylvian lesions impairs fluency, comprehension and repetition together, so it cannot fit 'fluent speech with intact comprehension'. Tempting as a severe-lesion catch-all."}},
{"q":"Cronbach's alpha is an index of:","concept":"Reliability is the consistency of a measurement, and different coefficients capture different sources of error. Internal consistency asks whether the items of a scale hang together, that is, measure the same construct. Lee J. Cronbach (1951, Psychometrika) introduced coefficient alpha as a general index: alpha = (k / (k - 1)) x (1 - sum of item variances / total score variance), where k is the number of items. Conceptually, alpha is the average of all possible split-half reliabilities, and it rises when items intercorrelate highly and when the number of items increases. For dichotomous (right or wrong) items, alpha reduces to the Kuder-Richardson formula 20 (Kuder and Richardson, 1937). Older split-half reliability correlates two halves of the test and corrects with the Spearman-Brown prophecy formula. Conventional benchmarks (Jum Nunnally, 1978) are about .70 for research and .80 to .90 or higher for clinical decisions about individuals; very high alpha (above about .95) may signal redundant items. Cautions: alpha assumes tau-equivalence (equal item loadings) and is a lower bound under violations, so McDonald's omega is often preferred; and a high alpha does not prove unidimensionality. The other reliability types use different designs: test-retest (same test twice, correlation indexes temporal stability, error from time sampling), parallel or alternate forms (two equivalent versions, coefficient of equivalence), and inter-rater (agreement between scorers, Cohen's kappa, 1960, for two raters with categorical data or the intraclass correlation coefficient for continuous ratings). Validity (criterion, content, construct) is a separate question: whether the test measures what it claims. Option b is right because Cronbach's alpha indexes internal consistency reliability.","traps":{"a":"Test-retest reliability is estimated by correlating scores from two administrations over time; alpha uses a single administration. Tempting because both are reliability coefficients.","c":"Criterion validity is the correlation of the test with an external criterion (concurrent or predictive), a validity rather than reliability concept. Tempting because both are expressed as coefficients.","d":"Inter-rater reliability is agreement between independent scorers, indexed by Cohen's kappa or the intraclass correlation. Tempting because it is another reliability type, but alpha concerns item consistency, not rater agreement."}},
{"q":"Which test compares the means of three or more independent groups?","concept":"Analysis of variance (ANOVA) was developed by Ronald A. Fisher in the 1920s (Statistical Methods for Research Workers, 1925); the F statistic is named after him. One-way ANOVA tests whether the means of three or more independent groups defined by a single factor (independent variable) differ, for example depression scores under CBT, medication and waitlist. The null hypothesis is that all population means are equal; the alternative is that at least one differs. The logic partitions total variability into between-groups variance (how far group means are from the grand mean) and within-groups or error variance (spread of individuals inside each group). F = MS between / MS within. If the null is true, both estimate the same error variance and F is near 1; a large F suggests real group differences. Degrees of freedom are k - 1 between and N - k within. Why not several t-tests? Each test at alpha .05 adds risk; with three groups, three pairwise tests give a familywise Type I error of about 1 - (.95 cubed), roughly .14. ANOVA tests everything in one omnibus test. A significant F does not say which groups differ, so post hoc tests follow (Tukey's HSD, Scheffe, Bonferroni) or planned contrasts. Assumptions: normality, homogeneity of variance (Levene's test) and independence. Effect size: eta squared. Neighbours: repeated-measures ANOVA for the same participants measured several times; factorial (two-way) ANOVA for two factors and interactions; the Kruskal-Wallis H test as the non-parametric alternative; ANCOVA adds a covariate. With exactly two groups, F equals t squared. Option c is right because one-way ANOVA is the standard test comparing the means of three or more independent groups.","traps":{"a":"The independent-samples t-test compares the means of exactly two independent groups; using it repeatedly for three or more groups inflates Type I error. Tempting because it is the closest test for independent means.","b":"The paired-samples t-test compares two related measurements (pre-post or matched pairs), so it is wrong twice over: only two conditions and not independent groups. Tempting as another mean-comparison test.","d":"The chi-square test (Karl Pearson, 1900) compares observed and expected frequencies of categorical data, not means of continuous scores. Tempting because it can handle three or more groups."}}
]});
